      "title": "Assistant Professor Of Finance",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Of Finance | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor Of Finance Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants are required to submit their applications electronically by visiting Interfolio [https://apply.interfolio.com/195129] and uploading the following: 1. Cover letter 2. CV 3. Job market paper and up to 2 other research papers 4. Teaching statement 5. Research statement 6. 3 letters of recommendation The application deadline is Friday, November 13, 2026 . For questions related to the application process, please contact Shelly Whitmer at [email protected] , Finance Area Administrator. Who We Are ABOUT THE STEPHEN M. ROSS SCHOOL OF BUSINESS The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan is a diverse learning community grounded in the principle that business can be an extraordinary vehicle for positive change in today's dynamic global economy. The Ross School of Business mission is to build a better world through business. Through thought and action, members of the Ross community drive change and innovation that improve business and society. Michigan Ross is consistently ranked among the world's leading business schools. Academic degree programs include the Bachelor of Business Administration, Full-Time MBA, Part-Time MBA (Online and Weekend formats), Executive MBA, Global MBA, Master of Accounting, Master of Business Analytics, Master of Management, Master of Supply Chain Management, and PhD. The school also offers open-enrollment and custom executive education programs focused on general management, leadership development, and strategic human resource management. For more information, visit MichiganRoss.umich.edu. Job Summary The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan has one tenure-track position available in Finance starting in September 2027. The position is at the untenured Assistant Professor level. Research and publishing, teaching at the graduate and/or undergraduate level, supervising doctoral research, and service contributions are required. Required Qualifications* Earned doctorate by September 2027. A qualified candidate must demonstrate the ability and interest in conducting high-quality scholarly research, as well as a high level of teaching competence. Desired Qualifications* Candidates with research and teaching interests in all areas of Finance will be considered. Additional Information For more detailed descriptions of the Finance Area, Stephen M. Ross School of Business, and the University of Michigan, please visit our websites: Finance Area Stephen M. Ross School of Business University of Michigan University of Michigan Benefits Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third-party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284393 Working Title Assistant Professor Of Finance Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Bus Admin Department Ross School of Business Posting Begin/End Date 10/14/2026 - 10/22/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284203": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applications are due by December 1, 2026, and will be reviewed on a rolling basis. A subset of applicants will be invited to conduct a Zoom interview; from that group, a select number will be invited for in-person interviews and a job talk. In-person interviews are planned to be completed by the end of February 2027. Applicants should send the following materials: 1) Curriculum vitae: this should include information on the applicant's education and training, publications, funding, presentations at conferences, teaching and mentoring activities, media coverage, and service activities (e.g., local or national committee work, serving as a peer reviewer for journals). 2) Cover letter (2 page maximum, single-spaced, 12-point font). The cover letter should include information on: The reasons for the applicant's interest in joining the CHEAR Center The applicant's research focus to date and planned research agenda as a CHEAR faculty member The applicant's experience with qualitative research, mixed methods research, and/or implementation science The applicant's experience with mentorship Application materials and questions should be directed to Katherine Luppino at [email protected] . Job Summary The Susan B. Meister Child Health Evaluation and Research (CHEAR) Center in the Department of Pediatrics at the University of Michigan Medical School is seeking applications for a full-time, faculty position at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor level on the instructional track (tenure track). The search committee is specifically seeking an individual with methodological expertise in 1 or more of the following 3 areas: qualitative methods, mixed-methods research, and implementation science. The candidate will lead grant applications, author peer-reviewed publications, and present at national meetings.  Qualified candidates may have an opportunity to play a leadership role in CHEAR's postdoctoral fellowship program, a role that would entail protected time. The CHEAR Center is the oldest pediatric health services research center in the U.S. The Center includes 15 general pediatricians, pediatric subspecialists, economists, epidemiologists, and research scientists. Areas of expertise include but are not limited to the care of children with chronic conditions, pediatric health care utilization and spending, immunizations, Medicaid policy, and health equity.  Key strengths of the CHEAR Center include an interdisciplinary and collaborative environment, national reputation, strong administrative support, excellent research staff, longstanding relationships with the State of Michigan, and access to a broad range of administrative databases. Moreover, the CHEAR Center has access to the resources of the Institute for Healthcare Policy and Innovation, which unites 850 faculty across the University of Michigan who conduct health services research. The CHEAR Center and the University of Michigan are located in Ann Arbor, which is consistently rated as one of the best places to live in the U.S. by media outlets. Position Overview Appointment: Full-time position at Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor level. Sponsorship: sponsorship is not available for this position. Required Qualifications* For non-clinicians: doctoral degree (Ph.D. or equivalent) in field relevant to health services research, including but not limit General pediatricians: One of the following 1) Doctor of Medicine (MD) or Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO) and a doctoral degree in field relevant to health services research; or 2) Doctor of Medicine (MD) or Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO) and a master's degree (e.g., MS, MPH, MPP) and completion of a health services research postdoctoral fellowship Methodological expertise in 1 or more of the following 3 areas: qualitative research, mixed methods research, implementation science Significant mentorship experience Existing grant funding  that can be transferred to CHEAR upon the start of the faculty position (e.g., career development award, R01/R21/R03, foundation grant) To be considered for this position at the rank of Assistant Professor, candidates must have at least 3 years of experience as a faculty member and existing NIH or equivalent funding Why Join Michigan Medicine? Michigan Medicine is one of the largest health care complexes in the world and has been the site of many groundbreaking medical and technological advancements since the opening of the U-M Medical School in 1850. Michigan Medicine is comprised of over 30,000 employees and our vision is to attract, inspire, and develop outstanding people in medicine, sciences, and healthcare to become one of the world's most distinguished academic health systems.  In some way, great or small, every person here helps to advance this world-class institution. Work at Michigan Medicine and become a victor for the greater good. What Benefits can you Look Forward to? Excellent medical, dental and vision coverage effective on your very first day 2:1 Match on retirement savings Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend on qualifications. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284203 Working Title ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Pediatrics-CHEAR Posting Begin/End Date 10/13/2026 - 12/05/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284083": {
      "title": "PROFESSOR (Full, Associate, Assistant)",
      "description": "PROFESSOR (Full, Associate, Assistant) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ PROFESSOR (Full, Associate, Assistant) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should electronically submit through Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/195226 the following: Cover letter, Curriculum vitae, Statement of research interests and future research vision, Statement of teaching philosophy and interests, Names and contact information for three or four qualified individuals who can provide letters of reference, and Copies of three publications that best represent the applicant's scholarly contributions. Applications will be reviewed continuously, but those received by November 30th, 2026 will be given priority. If you have any questions regarding the search, please contact Professor Scott Baalrud, Search Committee Chair, at [email protected] . Who We Are The Department of Nuclear Engineering and Radiological Sciences (NERS) is consistently ranked as the best nuclear engineering program in the country. Our interdisciplinary team of instructional and research faculty are at the forefront of sustainable energy solutions, nuclear security and defense, and plasma applications. NERS research is novel and far-reaching, spanning space exploration, radiation for medical treatment, next-generation particle accelerators, non-proliferation, fusion materials, and more. We are a tight-knit community of researchers, students, and support staff aiming to advance the benefits of nuclear, radiological, and plasma technology to bolster societal improvements. At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement, enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Department of Nuclear Engineering and Radiological Sciences in the College of Engineering of the University of Michigan, an international leader in the field of nuclear engineering and radiological sciences, invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position. All academic ranks (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) will be considered, commensurate with experience. The department will consider all qualified applicants across the fields of nuclear engineering, including fission and radiation transport, radiation measurements, materials, plasma/fusion science, and policy. However, priority will be placed on expertise in reactor thermal hydraulics, radiation detection, or nuclear materials and fuel cycles. Applicants are expected to have demonstrated outstanding research and scholarship in their fields. In addition to conducting research, faculty members are expected to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, mentor students, and supervise student research. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Professor (Full/Associate/Assistant). The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Background Screening Employment will require a criminal background check and may require an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. If an institutional reference check is necessary, candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284083 Working Title PROFESSOR (Full, Associate, Assistant) Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Nuclear Eng & Rad Science Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 10/16/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283797": {
      "title": "ASST PROFESSOR, ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASST PROFESSOR, ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASST PROFESSOR, ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Submit your application to Interfolio here: https://apply.interfolio.com/194357 . A complete application packet includes at least the following six items: (1) a cover letter (up to two pages); (2) a current C.V., two summary statements of up to two pages each describing, respectively: (3) career teaching plans; (4) research plans; and (5) three names of references. For candidates with prior teaching experience, please provide (6) a summary of course (teaching) evaluations. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Applications received by October 20th, 2026 will receive full consideration. Please contact the IOE Faculty Search Committee at [email protected] if you have any questions. Who We Are Michigan Engineering provides scientific and technological leadership to the people of the world. We seek to improve the quality of life by developing intellectually curious and socially conscious minds, creating collaborative solutions to societal problems, and promoting an inclusive and innovative community of service for the common good. The College of Engineering is especially interested in candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. Michigan Engineering's vision is to be the world's preeminent college of engineering serving the common good. This global outlook, leadership focus, and service commitment permeate our culture. Our vision is supported by a mission and values that, together, provide the framework for all that we do. Information about our vision, mission and values can be found at: http://strategicvision.engin.umich.edu/ . The IOE Department is a nationally recognized leader in education and research focused on improving complex systems through data analytics, human systems integration, optimization, and stochastic systems. IOE brings together outstanding faculty, students, and collaborators to address consequential challenges across areas such as business operations, energy and sustainability, health and human safety, and mobility and transportation networks. The department has a longstanding record of excellence and is consistently ranked among the top industrial engineering programs in the nation; in the 2026 U.S. News & World Report rankings, Michigan IOE is ranked #2 nationally for its graduate program and #3 for its undergraduate program. The University of Michigan is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer and is responsive to the needs of dual career families. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Summary The Department of Industrial and Operations Engineering (IOE) at the University of Michigan (UM) welcomes applications for a faculty position at any rank (assistant, associate, or full professor) in all areas related to IOE, including human factors and ergonomics, operations research, and data analytics. Of particular interest are candidates whose research intersects with artificial intelligence (AI), broadly defined, and emerging technologies in sociotechnical systems. An ideal candidate will have a strong methodological emphasis (drawing on fields such as applied probability, computation, neuroscience, optimization, psychology, statistics, and social sciences) and a significant engagement with applications, including but not limited to advanced manufacturing, business operations, energy systems, healthcare, human-systems integration, and transportation. Senior candidates are expected to have a track record of excellence in both research as well as graduate and undergraduate education and have a demonstrated ability to establish independent and externally-funded research programs in their fields of expertise. Particular attention will be placed on candidates who embrace the global outlook, leadership focus, and service commitment that are fundamental to the culture of Michigan Engineering. Required Qualifications* You must have a Ph.D. and show a commitment to high-quality research and evidence of teaching success and/or potential, as appropriate. You are dedicated to excellence in graduate and undergraduate education, and will develop impactful, productive, and innovative research programs. We welcome candidates at all levels (Assistant, Associate, and Full Professor). Our department's application areas include energy & sustainability, health & human safety, manufacturing, mobility & transportation, operations management, public policy, and robotics. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Background Screening Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283797 Working Title ASST PROFESSOR, ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Industrial & Oper Engin Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 10/29/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283807": {
      "title": "ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR in Sports Analytics",
      "description": "ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR in Sports Analytics | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR in Sports Analytics Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Submit your application to Interfolio here: https://apply.interfolio.com/194464 . A complete application packet includes at least the following six items: (1) a cover letter (up to two pages); (2) a current C.V., two summary statements of up to two pages each describing,  respectively: (3) career teaching plans; (4) research plans; and (5) three names of references. For candidates with prior teaching experience, please provide (6) a summary of course (teaching) evaluations. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Applications received by November 30th, 2026 will receive full consideration. Please contact the IOE Faculty Search Committee at [email protected] if you have any questions. Who We Are Michigan Engineering provides scientific and technological leadership to the people of the world. We seek to improve the quality of life by developing intellectually curious and socially conscious minds, creating collaborative solutions to societal problems, and promoting an inclusive and innovative community of service for the common good. The College of Engineering is especially interested in candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. Michigan Engineering's vision is to be the world's preeminent college of engineering serving the common good. This global outlook, leadership focus, and service commitment permeate our culture. Our vision is supported by a mission and values that, together, provide the framework for all that we do. Information about our vision, mission and values can be found at: http://strategicvision.engin.umich.edu/ . The University of Michigan is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer and is responsive to the needs of dual career families. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Summary The Department of Industrial and Operations Engineering (IOE) at the University of Michigan (UM) welcomes applications for a faculty position at mid-career or higher rank (associate, and full professor) in the area of Sports Analytics (SA) for an appointment beginning in August 2027. Note that an assistant professor with an exceptional track record may be considered. SA represents an expansive field that connects disciplines within and beyond the College of Engineering in the shared pursuit of understanding performance, decision-making, and human movement. This is part of a campus-wide faculty expansion program directed towards building a campus-wide cluster focused on SA. The new cluster is expected to leverage and augment IOE?s enduring strengths in optimization, stochastics, data analytics, statistics, machine learning, and human factors. Our multidisciplinary department integrates theoretical, methodological, and applied research interests. We are seeking individuals with an enduring commitment in sports analytics from fields that include but are not restricted to operations research, human factors, statistics, applied mathematics, and computer science. The ideal candidate will have a track record of excellence in both research as well as graduate and undergraduate education and a demonstrated ability to establish independent and externally-funded research programs in their fields of expertise. Particular attention will be placed on candidates who embrace the global outlook, leadership focus, and service commitment that are fundamental to the culture of Michigan Engineering. Naturally, the search will place a greater emphasis on senior candidates who have an established record of scholarship and innovation in the field and have the  potential to build an impactful and expansive research program in SA. Required Qualifications* You have a Ph.D. and show a commitment to high-quality research and evidence of teaching success and/or potential, as appropriate. You are dedicated to excellence in graduate and undergraduate education, and will develop impactful, productive, and innovative research programs. We welcome candidates at a mid-career or higher level (tenure-track Associate Professor, and Full Professor) from all areas related to Industrial and Operations Engineering, including Operations Research, Data and Decision Analytics, Artificial Intelligence, Human Factors, and related disciplines. Assistant professors with an established stellar track record may be considered. Our department's application areas include energy & sustainability, health & human safety, manufacturing, mobility & transportation, operations management, public policy, and robotics. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Associate Professor or Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Note that an Assistant Professor with an exceptional track record may be considered. Background Screening Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283807 Working Title ASSOC PROFESSOR, OR PROFESSOR in Sports Analytics Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Industrial & Oper Engin Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 12/08/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283874": {
      "title": "Asst/Assoc/Full PROFESSOR",
      "description": "Asst/Assoc/Full PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Asst/Assoc/Full PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applications should include, in PDF format: (1) cover letter (2-page limit), (2) Curriculum Vitae (no page limit), (3) research statement (3-page limit), (4) mentoring, teaching and pedagogy statement (2-page limit), (5) a list of three significant publications (with DOIs), (6) and a list of four references with contact details.  For full consideration, applications should be received by November 13, 2026. Candidates should click here to apply: https://apply.interfolio.com/194094 Questions about the position or application process can be directed to the search committee chair Christiane Jablonowski ( [email protected] ). Who We Are The Department of Climate and Space Sciences and Engineering (CLaSP) is housed within the University of Michigan's (U-M) College of Engineering in Ann Arbor, Michigan. The CLaSP research portfolio spans climate, space, and planetary science with annual research expenditures of $30 million. CLaSP has 25 tenured and tenure-track faculty members, 26 research faculty, over 50 undergraduates and 110 graduate students. CLaSP offers an exciting research and teaching environment with wide scope and rich expertise within the department as well as diversified interdisciplinary opportunities within the university. The research themes in CLaSP include weather and climate modeling and observations, atmospheric dynamics, radiation and remote sensing, atmospheric chemistry and air pollution, atmosphere-biosphere interactions, as well as hydrosphere and cryosphere research. Please visit the CLaSP website to explore the breadth and depth of our research: https://clasp.engin.umich.edu/ Mission Statement Michigan Engineering's vision is to be the world's preeminent college of engineering serving the common good. This global outlook, leadership focus, and service commitment permeate our culture. Our vision is supported by a mission and values that, together, provide the framework for all that we do.  Information about our vision, mission and values can be found at: https://strategicvision.engin.umich.edu/. Job Summary The U-M CLaSP department invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position. We seek to hire a faculty member at the assistant professor level whose research advances our understanding of weather systems and extreme weather, with a strong foundation in atmospheric dynamics and the coupled Earth system. Areas of expertise could include high-resolution weather and climate modeling and forecasting; the detection and attribution of extreme events; and assessments of the subseasonal-to-seasonal or decadal evolution of weather and climate. Machine learning and artificial intelligence (AI) methods are widely applied in meteorology and atmospheric modeling and are revolutionizing prediction and forecasting; we expect the successful candidate to contribute a high level of expertise in this area. Responsibilities* We seek candidates capable of developing an internationally recognized research program, competing successfully for external funding, mentoring graduate and undergraduate students, contributing to our educational mission at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and strengthening the culture, diversity, and collaborative environment of the department, college, and university. We value applicants whose research and teaching interests are interdisciplinary and have the potential to address real-world weather and climate impacts. Required Qualifications* A PhD in a subject related to meteorological, atmospheric, or climate sciences, or other relevant disciplines in sciences or engineering is required. Desired Qualifications* Postgraduate research experience Underfill Statement This position may be underfilled at a lower classification depending on the qualifications of the selected candidate. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process will start on November 14, 2026. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283874 Working Title Asst/Assoc/Full PROFESSOR Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Climate & Space Sci Eng Posting Begin/End Date 10/08/2026 - 11/14/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283901": {
      "title": "Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested candidates should submit application materials to Professor Karthik Duraisamy, chair of the search committee, via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194876 Please prepare an application containing a cover letter, your curriculum vitae, statements of research and teaching interests, a service statement, and the names and contact information of five references. Those applications received by December 1, 2026 at https://apply.interfolio.com/194876 will be given full consideration. Applications received after this date may be reviewed on a case-by-case basis. Only complete applications received via Interfolio will be considered. Mission Statement Michigan Engineering's mission is to provide scientific and technological leadership to the people of the world, develop intellectually curious and socially conscious minds, and create collaborative solutions to societal problems. Job Summary The Department of Aerospace Engineering at the University of Michigan invites applications for multiple full-time tenure-track faculty positions at all ranks. We seek faculty members who commit to excellence in graduate and undergraduate education and who will develop impactful and internationally leading research programs. While we will consider all areas relevant to aerospace engineering, we will give priority for exceptional candidates in the following areas: Advanced computational methods and emerging computing paradigms for aerospace applications. Areas of interest include quantum and quantum-classical hybrid computing for physical systems; neuromorphic and probabilistic computing; resource-efficient onboard computation; novel paradigms of computational science and AI for aerospace systems. Science and technology of operating in and beyond Earth orbit. Areas of interest include orbital mechanics and navigation for cislunar and multi-body regime; space situational / domain awareness; space robotics and autonomy; space operation and logistics; in-space assembly and manufacturing; and space resource utilization and surface systems. Frontiers in aerospace engineering. We seek candidates who define directions beyond the areas above, particularly where new capabilities emerge at the intersection of disciplines such as high-speed flight, sustainable aviation, advanced air mobility, autonomy and advanced manufacturing. Areas of opportunity include experimental methods and diagnostics for extreme environments; novel ground and flight testing, including automated and hardware-in-the-loop approaches; and systems integration, from digital engineering to model-based design to qualification and certification of complex vehicles. More information about our department can be found at https://aero.eng.umich.edu. Required Qualifications* Successful candidates will teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, mentor students, and contribute to interdisciplinary efforts linking the department with industry, strategic partnerships with national laboratories, state- and national-level space initiatives, while supporting University-wide initiatives in priority areas. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The University of Michigan is highly responsive to dual career needs via our dual career program. Background Screening Employment will require a criminal background check and may require an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. If an institutional reference check is necessary, candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form and an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283901 Working Title Professor/Associate Professor/Assistant Professor Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Aerospace Engineering Posting Begin/End Date 10/07/2026 - 12/16/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283887": {
      "title": "ASST PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASST PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASST PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Candidates should provide a cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae (CV) including a complete list of publications , a research plan ( three pages maximum) , a teaching statement (two pages maximum), a single-page diversity statement, two publications, and the names of four references to our website at http://mse.engin.umich.edu/facultysearch . Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026. Applications received after that date will be considered until the position is filled. Contact information: E-mail: [email protected] Subject: Materials Science and Engineering (MSE) Faculty Search Chair Department of Materials Science and Engineering The University of Michigan 2300 Hayward Street Ann Arbor, MI 48109-2136 Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement ? enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Department of Materials Science and Engineering (MSE), College of Engineering, University of Michigan, seeks outstanding applicants for a tenure-track assistant professor faculty position. Applications are invited in all areas of materials science and engineering, but are particularly encouraged in sustainable metallurgy (including, but not limited to) critical minerals and metals, decarbonization of metals production and processing, and recyclability. Required Qualifications* We seek individuals who can develop and lead an externally-funded research program, collaborate across materials-related disciplines, teach undergraduate and graduate MSE courses, and provide service to the University and the materials profession. The applicant must hold a Ph.D. degree in Materials Science and Engineering or a related engineering or science discipline. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. Additional Information The U-M Materials Science and Engineering Department hosts highly-ranked undergraduate and graduate programs that support a world-class research enterprise. Department strengths span the core MSE areas of structural, functional, and soft materials as well as cross-cutting computational and interdisciplinary topics. More information about the Department can be found at: http://mse.engin.umich.edu/ . The University of Michigan has a long and distinguished history. It was founded in 1817, twenty years before the territory became a state, and was one of the first public universities in the nation. Throughout its 200-year history, it has maintained the highest levels of education, scholarship, and research. Ann Arbor, a vibrant mid-sized city known for its sprawling greenery and exposed-brick facades, its array of enticing eateries and historic theaters, and, of course, Michigan Football, is regularly ranked as one of the best places to live in the nation.  The U-M campus is also less than a thirty-minute drive from the Detroit Metropolitan Airport, an international hub for major airlines. Ann Arbor is also located within 45 miles of the sports, cultural, and economic attractions of downtown Detroit and its surrounding metropolitan area. Learn more about Life in Ann Arbor on YouTube. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283887 Working Title ASST PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Materials Science & Engin. Posting Begin/End Date 10/07/2026 - 11/14/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283791": {
      "title": "ASST PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASST PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASST PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants must submit a current curriculum vitae, job market paper, three reference letters, a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, a statement of current and future research plans, and evidence of teaching excellence to the departments Assistant Professor of Economics - Any Field job posting on EconJobMarket at https://www.econjobmarket.org . First consideration will be given to applications submitted by November 7, 2026. Applications will continue to be considered until the position is filled. Job Summary The Department of Economics at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor invites applications for full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in any field of economics to begin August 25, 2027. This is a university-year appointment at the assistant professor level. Responsibilities* Duties include teaching and conducting research in economics, advising students, and providing service to the department and university. A Ph.D. in Economics or a related field is required by the start date of the position. Required Qualifications* Employment will require a successful criminal background check and candidates must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283791 Working Title ASST PROFESSOR Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Economics Posting Begin/End Date 10/01/2026 - 11/07/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283755": {
      "title": "Asst Professor",
      "description": "Asst Professor | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Asst Professor Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement summarizing past and future research plans (3 pages), a teaching and service statement (1 page), and three letters of recommendation. Completed applications will start to be reviewed as they are received, so please submit your application as soon as possible. For full consideration, completed applications must be received by November 13, 2026. All materials should be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/190644 . For questions about the position or the application process, please contact the department chair, Dr. Anthony Antonellis ( [email protected] ). Job Summary The Department of Human Genetics at the University of Michigan Medical School (Ann Arbor, Michigan) is performing a broad search to fill one tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. We encourage candidates to apply who have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree, or equivalent, and who plan to employ classical and/or modern approaches to address fundamental questions in genetics and genomics. Recruited faculty will join a highly collaborative community of more than forty investigators, whose research is integrated with other basic science and clinical departments, and centers and institutes at the University of Michigan. Information about research currently pursued in the Department can be found at https://medschool.umich.edu/departments/human-genetics . Successful candidates will be provided with laboratory and office space, and a competitive start-up package to launch a robust research program. We expect candidates to contribute to our faculty's strong interest in developing trainees at all levels, including teaching in the classroom and mentoring in the laboratory. Desired Qualifications* Ph.D. and/or M.D. degree Curriculum vitae Three-page description of research experience with an emphasis on future plans Three letters of recommendation Teaching and service statement Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283755 Working Title Asst Professor Job Title Asst Professor Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Human Genetics Department Posting Begin/End Date 9/30/2026 - 11/14/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283543": {
      "title": "ASST PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASST PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASST PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In addition to applying via careers.umich.edu, please send a letter of application, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, evidence of teaching excellence, a statement of current and future research plans, and three (3) letters of reference to https://apply.interfolio.com/194128 Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of American Culture (AC) in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts (LSA) at the University of Michigan-Ann Arbor (U-M) seeks dynamic, early-career applicants for a 100% tenure-track appointment at the rank of assistant professor in the humanities or humanistic social sciences, with an option for a one-or two-year postdoctoral fellowship at the start. This position is part of the LSA Collegiate Fellowship (LCF) program, sponsored by the Phillip J. Bowman Center ( https://bowmancenter.umich.edu/initiatives/lsa-collegiate-fellowship-program/ ) for Scholarship to Practice at U-M, which aims to recruit faculty who have engaged in efforts to increase access and inclusion in higher education through their teaching and mentoring, AND/OR research and scholarship, AND/OR service and engagement. We encourage applicants from a wide range of institution types as we seek to diversify where we hire from. For more details on the LSA Collegiate Fellows Program, please visit the program's website. This university year appointment will begin August 25, 2027. Responsibilities* Instructional responsibilities include teaching graduate seminars and large undergraduate lecture courses in American cultural studies methodology and within broad temporal and topical frameworks. Required Qualifications* We are interested in applicants who hold a Ph.D. in American studies, ethnic studies, or adjacent fields, and whose work engages and forges new critical intersections across and between interdisciplinary humanities and ethnic studies. Employment will require a successful criminal background check, and candidates must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. Desired Qualifications* Candidates who work in pre-twentieth-century history, literature and culture; African diaspora; Pacific Islander, Native American, and Indigenous studies; Arab and Muslim American studies; digital studies; environmental humanities; performance studies; and visual culture are especially encouraged to apply. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information As one of the world?s great liberal arts and sciences colleges, LSA pushes the boundaries of what is understood about the human experience and the natural world, and we foster the next generation of rigorous and empathetic thinkers, creators, and contributors to the state of Michigan, the nation, and the world. To learn more about LSA?s Mission, Vision and Values, please visit lsa.umich.edu/strategicvision . The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual-career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information For any questions, please contact the LCF Committee, at [email protected] . Application Deadline The anticipated application deadline is October 23, 2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283543 Working Title ASST PROFESSOR Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA American Culture Posting Begin/End Date 9/25/2026 - 10/24/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283509": {
      "title": "Professor / Associate Professor in Human-Centered Embodied Artificial Intelligence (Robotics)",
      "description": "Professor / Associate Professor in Human-Centered Embodied Artificial Intelligence (Robotics) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Professor / Associate Professor in Human-Centered Embodied Artificial Intelligence (Robotics) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now Mission Statement At Michigan Engineering, we develop people-first engineers who will shape the world for the better. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies that address today's pressing challenges, power industries, and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement, enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Robotics Department in the College of Engineering at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor invites applications for one senior (tenure-track or tenured) faculty position in Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence, offered through the University of Michigan Faculty Expansion Program. The successful candidate will join our mission to accelerate advances in robotics for the benefit of society. Candidates will have a relevant doctorate or equivalent experience and an outstanding record of achievement and impactful research in academia, industry, and/or at national laboratories. They will have a strong record of commitment to teaching and mentorship at the undergraduate and graduate levels and to providing service to the university and to the profession. Rank is commensurate with experience, from promotion-ready assistant professor through full professor. We seek an outstanding scholar whose work advances the theory and practice of AI-driven embodied systems that collaborate with humans in physical environments. This position is a cornerstone of a broader university initiative in Human-Centered and Human-Embodied AI (HCAI). This position will reside in the Robotics Department, with the intention of strengthening synergies with the School of Information and the potential to seed a future multi-unit research center. The position supports the University's Vision 2034 strategic priorities, which emphasize impactful research in health, education, sustainability, and human-centered technology. An ideal candidate will bring a demonstrated record of expertise and scholarship that is deeply connected to leading robotics and AI research, student engagement and experiential learning, and the values of the Robotics Department.  Research contributions are expected to focus on developing responsible embodied AI systems for seamless human collaboration in physical environments, for areas including AI alignment, human-robot interaction, foundational models, collaborative robotics, multimodal interaction, AR/VR interfaces, and cognitive models of human-AI joint action.  A successful candidate will be expected to develop a vigorous, externally funded research program in human-centered and human-embodied AI. Please see application instructions and further information at https://robotics.umich.edu/jobs/ . Application review will begin on November 1, 2026  and will continue on a rolling basis. All applications submitted by December 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. If you have any questions regarding the search process, please contact [email protected] . The University of Michigan is one of the world's leading research universities, with $2.16B in research expenditures (FY2025). Consistently ranked among the top research universities by US News and NSF, it has 19 schools and colleges, and the University is rich with opportunities for collaboration and cross-disciplinary inquiry. The successful candidate will have access to Michigan's advanced robotics facilities and resources, opportunities to contribute to emerging research center development and industry partnerships, and support for technology transfer, spinoff companies, and licensable innovations. We are located in Ann Arbor, MI, which is ranked the #1 U.S. City for Quality of Life by U.S. News and World Report (2024), #2 City with the Best Public Schools in America by Niche.com (2021), and #1 Best Places to Live for Families by Fortune (2022). Employment will require a criminal background check and may require an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. If an institutional reference check is necessary, candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Required Qualifications* A relevant doctorate or equivalent experience. An outstanding record of achievement and impactful research in academia, industry, and/or at national laboratories. A strong record of commitment to teaching and mentorship at the undergraduate and graduate levels. A strong record of providing service to the university and to the profession. Demonstrated experience and rank commensurate with a promotion-ready assistant professor through full professor level. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Associate Professor/Professor.  The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283509 Working Title Professor / Associate Professor in Human-Centered Embodied Artificial Intelligence (Robotics) Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Robotics Posting Begin/End Date 9/25/2026 - 12/16/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283397": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To apply, please submit application materials, including a cover letter, CV, names of three references, and research and teaching statements to the link below.  Applications will be accepted and reviewed on an ongoing basis; applications received by November 15, 2026 will receive full consideration. https://deptapps.engin.umich.edu/facultycandidate/apply?dept=210600&searchID=73 Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement-enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Biomedical Engineering Department at the University of Michigan (bme.umich.edu), a joint department between the College of Engineering and the Medical School, is seeking to fill a tenured/tenure-track faculty position at any rank in our College of Engineering track. In alignment with our departmental strategic vision, we are searching for outstanding researchers with expertise in AI and AI applied to biomedical engineering research areas. Successful applicants will also be expected to contribute to teaching AI in biomedical engineering. Required Qualifications* Required qualifications include a Ph.D. in engineering or other relevant science-related discipline and demonstrated excellence in and a commitment to teaching, research, and scholarship. M.D./Ph.D. candidates are also encouraged to apply. Why Work at Michigan? In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, The University of Michigan offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions with immediate vesting Many choices for comprehensive health insurance Life insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Maternity and Parental Leave Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283397 Working Title Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Biomedical Engineering Posting Begin/End Date 9/24/2026 - 1/01/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282154": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The confidential review of applicants will occur as they are received and will continue until the position(s) are filled. A cover letter, attached as the first page of your Curriculum Vitae must be uploaded online via the http://careers.umich.edu/ (U-M Careers website) for this job ID 282154. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following materials to the UMSN Office of Human Resources via email to [email protected] - please put in your subject line that you are applying for the Faculty Tenure Track Recruitment opportunity. Required Application Materials for all Ranks - Teaching statement which should describe teaching philosophy, experience in teaching across programs and settings (i.e., didactic and clinical) Research statement which should include a description of three to five peer-reviewed publications, with an emphasis on the most recent and representative Service statement which should focus on contributions in academic, professional and community areas as is relevant. If selected to move forward in the process, applicants will be asked to submit additional materials as noted in the School of Nursing Faculty Handbook including professional letters of recommendation, transcripts of your highest degrees, and complete compliance requirements. For further information regarding these positions please contact [email protected] . Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Nursing is a vibrant community of scholars, educators, clinicians, and innovators embedded within one of the world's leading public research universities. Consistently ranked among the nation's top nursing schools, we are committed to advancing health and health equity through research, education, leadership, and service. Our faculty generate knowledge that improves lives, informs policy, strengthens healthcare deliver, and addresses pressing local and global health challenges. UMSN faculty benefit from exceptional opportunities to collaborate across nursing, medicine, public health, engineering, data science, and the social sciences. Supported by a premier academic health system and a robust culture of mentorship and faculty development, our researchers are positioned to advance bold ideas, foster meaningful partnerships, and create solutions that improve the health and well-being of individuals, families, and communities. We welcome faculty applicants whose work crosses disciplinary boundaries and who are inspired by the opportunity to address society's most complex health challenges alongside exceptional colleagues, students, research scholars, and community partners. We seek accomplished, creative, and mission-driven faculty whose scholarship improves health, advances equity, and informs practice, policy, and education. Academics who are eager to promote interdisciplinary collaboration, mentor the next generation of nurses and scientists, and help shape the future of health care are encouraged to apply. The University of Michigan School of Nursing seeks applications from outstanding nurse scientists and interdisciplinary scholars whose research advances health and healthcare across the lifespan. We welcome candidates at all stages of their careers, from emerging scholars with strong potential for sustained research success to established leaders with nationally or internationally recognized programs of research.  Applicants will be considered for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor based on experience, evidence of scholarly accomplishments, and expected future impact. Particular areas of interest in the UMSN include, but are not limited to, pediatric and family health; women's health; chronic disease prevention and management; digital health innovations; AI in health; and interventions that improve health outcomes for individuals, families, and communities. Candidates should demonstrate a record of research productivity and the potential to contribute meaningfully to the UMSN's mission of advancing health for all through science, education, mentorship, leadership, and service. Required Qualifications* NECESSARY QUALIFICATIONS FOR ALL RANKS PhD in nursing or highly related field. For nursing PhD's, a current MI nursing license is required or eligibility for a nursing license in the State of Michigan. Prior to start date must be in compliance with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, relevant licenses/certifications, policies and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s), and/or clinical site(s), and/or research site(s). Record of first-author, indexed, peer-reviewed publications, and presentations. Demonstrate excellent communication, networking and relationship-building skills. Demonstrated competence in scholarly activity. Evidence of teaching and mentoring ability. Additional Qualifications for the rank of Associate Professor - Record of excellence in teaching. Distinguished record of scholarly attainment in the realm of scientific investigation, publication, and utilization of research findings in teaching, research, and service. A reputation among colleagues for outstanding achievements and recognized contributions in their field of expertise. An admirable record of academic, professional and community service. Additional Qualifications for the rank of Professor - Sustained record of excellence in teaching including experience with advanced students; Sustained and growing record of scholarly eminence, which advances the frontier of knowledge; A reputation among the candidate's colleagues and peers throughout the nation and preferably internationally for outstanding and continuing achievements in their field of expertise. A reputation, supported by documented external evaluation, which recognizes the presence of leadership and the quality of research activities, publications, and contributions to the profession in comparison with other professors. A continuing record of recognized accomplishments in academic, professional and community service. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Direct nursing practice experience (e.g., RN, APRN) in clinical settings (e.g., hospital, community-based) Experience to teach students in basic sciences courses (e.g., microbiology, pharmacology, pathophysiology) \"The employee will promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind, and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and other routinely, publicly and privately.\" Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the selected candidate's qualifications. Application Deadline Complete application packets must be submitted by November 30, 2026. The Faculty Search Committee will review applications through early Spring term 2027. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282154 Working Title ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR/PROFESSOR Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 9/23/2026 - 11/30/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283050": {
      "title": "Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair",
      "description": "Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The confidential review of applications will occur as they are received and will continue until the position is filled. A cover letter, attached as the first page of your Curriculum Vitae must be uploaded online via the http://careers.umich.edu/ (U-M Careers website) for this Job ID 283050. Additional Required Materials (submitted via email) In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following materials to the UMSN Office of Human Resources via email to [email protected] - please put in the subject line that you are applying for the Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair opportunity. Teaching and Mentorship Statement (2 pages maximum): Describe your philosophy, accomplishments, and impact as an educator and mentor in oncology nursing. Highlight contributions that have advanced the development of the oncology nursing workforce and improved learning outcomes across educational settings. Scholarship and Research Statement (2 pages maximum): Describe the body of scholarship that best represents your contributions to oncology nursing and cancer care. Discuss three to five representative scholarly works within the narrative, emphasizing their scientific, clinical educational, or policy impact and their influence on the field. Leadership, Service, and Professional Impact Statement (1-2 pages maximum): Describe your leadership and service contributions to oncology nursing, health care organizations, professional societies, and communities. Highlight activities demonstrating sustained impact on the profession, cancer care delivery, advocacy, health equity, or patient and family outcomes. If selected to move forward in the process, applicants will be asked to submit additional materials as noted in the School of Nursing Faculty Handbook , including professional letters of recommendation, transcripts of your highest degrees, and complete compliance requirements. For further information regarding these positions, please contact [email protected] . Responsibilities* The University of Michigan School of Nursing (UMSN) invites distinguished nurse scientists to apply for the Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair. Appointment will be at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor with tenure, commensurate with the successful candidate's qualifications and experience. Candidates should demonstrate excellence in funded research, oncology nursing, interdisciplinary team science, and the mentorship of emerging investigators. They should also have a sustained record of scholarly productivity and impact, as well as experience integrating research and scholarship with teaching and service in undergraduate and/or graduate programs. The recipient of the Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair will advance a nationally or internationally recognized program of research focused on nursing care for people living with cancer and their families. The recipient will have opportunities to collaborate closely with faculty across the UMSN, Michigan Medicine, and the University of Michigan Rogel Cancer Center, as well as with national and international partners and funding organizations. Michigan Medicine and the Rogel Cancer Center offer an outstanding environment for interdisciplinary research, education, clinical innovation, and patient care, providing a strong foundation for scholarship that improves outcomes for the cancer population. The UMSN is a vibrant community of scholars, educators, clinicians, and innovators embedded within one of the world's leading public research universities. Consistently ranked among the nation's top nursing schools, we are committed to advancing health and health equity through research, education, leadership, and service. Our faculty generate knowledge that improves lives, informs policy, strengthens healthcare delivery, and addresses pressing local and global health challenges. UMSN faculty benefit from exceptional opportunities to collaborate across nursing, medicine, public health, engineering, data science, and the social sciences. Supported by a premier academic health system and a robust culture of mentorship and faculty development, our researchers are positioned to advance bold ideas, foster meaningful partnerships, and create solutions that improve the health and well-being of individuals, families, and communities. The successful candidate will advance oncology nursing science, lead interdisciplinary investigative teams, mentor the next generation of nurse scientists, and contribute to the school's education, leadership, and service missions. The University of Michigan is a world-class research institution offering an exceptional environment for education, patient care, and research. Learn more about the University of Michigan School of Nursing , Michigan Medicine , and the Rogel Cancer Center . This position is anticipated to be filled during the 2027-2028 academic year. All qualified candidates, including individuals from groups underrepresented in nursing, are encouraged to apply. Required Qualifications* Demonstrated expertise in oncology nursing Earned PhD in Nursing with scholarly credential commensurate with appointment at senior rank (Associate Professor or Professor) Current Michigan nursing license is required or eligibility for a nursing license in the State of Michigan Sustained record of first-authored and/or last-author, database, peer-reviewed publications, and presentations Excellent communication, networking, and relationship-building skills Sustained record of excellence in teaching, including mentoring experience with advanced students and early career faculty Sustained and growing record of scholarly eminence, which advances the frontier of knowledge A national, and preferably internationally, reputation for outstanding and continuing achievements in the field of oncology nursing A reputation, supported by documented external evaluation, which recognizes the presence of leadership and the quality of research activities, publications, and contributions to the profession in comparison with other professors A continuing record of recognized accomplishments in academic, professional and community service Prior to start date must be in compliance with all health requirements, including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s) and/or research site(s) \"The employee will promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind, and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately.\" Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283050 Working Title Mary Lou Willard French Endowed Chair Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 9/14/2026 - 12/31/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283076": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Sciences | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Sciences Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants must apply through the M-Pact application portal . Specific details about this application requirements and portal for submission are accessible through: https://research.umich.edu/m-pact/apply/ Applicants should submit: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement describing current accomplishments and future research plans for 3 projects (no more than 5 pages) Teaching statement (no more than 3 pages) M-PACT statement describing mentoring philosophy and contributions to fostering an inclusive academic community (no more than 3 pages. Please see the guidance below) Up to 5 reprints or preprints of representative publications 3 reference letters. Review of applications will begin immediately after the application deadline. Questions regarding this position may be directed to the Chair of the Search Committee. We highly encourage you to direct inquiries about this position to the chair of our faculty search committee, Dr. Guizhi (Julian) Zhu ( [email protected] ). Job Summary Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) Faculty Position Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences College of Pharmacy University of Michigan The Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences in the University of Michigan (UM) College of Pharmacy seeks outstanding candidates for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. We are seeking an innovative scientist who will establish a nationally recognized, externally funded research program that complements and expands the department's strengths in pharmaceutical sciences. As part of a new faculty cluster hire, this position is supported in partnership with the Michigan Program for Advancing Cultural Transformation (M-PACT), which seeks to build a sustainable scientific community that strengthens the capacity of those who serve as catalysts for change in academic and healthcare environments. M-PACT is funded in part by a grant from the National Institutes of Health. M-PACT is a state-of-the-art faculty development program for new tenure-track faculty with a demonstrated and ongoing commitment to advancing issues of diversity, equity, access, and inclusion in the health sciences. The successful candidate will have 75% protected research time for their first 5 years and will be expected to build an independent, externally funded research program. Teaching and service expectations will be defined in collaboration with the Department Chair. Candidates should demonstrate the potential for or experience with leadership in research and professional activities, including publication, research funding, participation in collaborative and interdisciplinary initiatives, and service to the College, University, and broader academic community. They must be able to articulate how their research, teaching, and/or service activities will support the College's mission . The University of Michigan offers an exceptional environment for computational and data-driven pharmaceutical sciences. Faculty benefit from world-class resources in artificial intelligence, machine learning, high-performance computing, and biomedical informatics, including the Great Lakes and ARMIS high-performance computing cluster and expanding AI computing infrastructure. Collaborative opportunities span the College of Pharmacy, Michigan Medicine, the Medical School, College of Engineering, School of Public Health, and campus-wide initiatives in AI and Precision Health. Investigators have access to extensive clinical, genomic, imaging, and real-world health datasets, enabling interdisciplinary research in computational biology, drug discovery, translational science, and precision therapeutics. Areas of Research We invite applications from exceptional investigators whose research advances pharmaceutical sciences through computational, quantitative, and data-driven approaches. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Computational (AI, machine learning) approaches to drug discovery, engineering, and development. Small-molecule therapeutics. Peptides and protein therapeutics. Nucleic acid medicine. Cell & gene therapies, and other next-generation therapeutics. Computational approaches to the development of drug formulations and delivery systems. Drug formulations and drug delivery systems: AI- and machine learning-enabled design of formulations and drug delivery systems for small molecules, biologics, nucleic acids, and advanced therapeutics. Biopharmaceutical performance: Mechanistic and data-driven modeling of drug release, transport, absorption, biodistribution, and pharmacokinetics. Digital pharmaceutics: AI, digital twins, and model-informed approaches for formulation development, manufacturing, and quality by design (QbD). Pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic (PK/PD) modeling and simulation. PK/PD modeling and simulation for drug development and precision dosing. Quantitative analysis of exposure?response relationships and therapeutic optimization. Model-informed strategies integrating preclinical and clinical data for translational medicine. Pharmacometrics, quantitative systems pharmacology, and model-informed drug development (MIDD). Applicants whose research integrates computational methods with experimental, translational, or clinical pharmaceutical sciences are particularly encouraged to apply. Candidates are expected to develop innovative methodologies that accelerate therapeutic discovery, optimize drug development, improve regulatory decision-making, and ultimately enhance patient outcomes. The successful candidate will be expected to develop an internationally recognized, independent, externally funded research program while contributing to the educational mission of the Department through graduate, professional (PharmD), and undergraduate teaching and mentoring. We are seeking someone who brings diverse experiences, perspectives, and/or ideas and is interested in addressing health equity through inclusive excellence in the UM community via research, teaching, and/or service activities. The successful applicant is also expected to participate actively in interdisciplinary collaborations across the College of Pharmacy, Michigan Medicine, the broader UM community, and external partners. Candidates should demonstrate strong potential for leadership in research and professional activities, including publication, extramural funding, mentoring, collaborative scholarship, and service to the department, college, university, and profession. Applicants should also articulate how their research, teaching, mentoring, and service align with the mission of the Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences and the College of Pharmacy. Eligibility A Ph.D. (or equivalent terminal degree) in pharmaceutical sciences, biomedical engineering, chemistry, chemical engineering, pharmacology, molecular biology, biotechnology, or a closely related field. Meet the NIH definition of Early Stage Investigator (ESI) at the start date of their appointment (anticipated July 2027). Unless extended, this means that the above terminal degree was or will be conferred between June 30th, 2017, and July 1, 2027. Have never had a tenure-track faculty appointment in an institution of higher education. Have an outstanding research record and strong potential to establish an innovative, externally funded research program. A demonstrated track record of promoting health equity within their field of study through their own research, teaching/mentoring, and/or service activities as well as a commitment to continue to promote health equity in the future through these and/or other activities. A demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring students at the undergraduate, graduate, and professional levels. A commitment to fostering an inclusive, collaborative, and supportive academic environment. Is currently and will continue to be eligible for NIH funding throughout their time in the program. About the M-PACT Statement (required) M-PACT is a federally funded, university-wide faculty initiative. It aims to drive transformative research, life-changing teaching, and accessible clinical care that will support the health of ALL, including a strong commitment to communities who have been underserved. By partnering with faculty throughout their careers, M-PACT strengthens the capacity of those who serve as catalysts for change in academic and healthcare environments. In order to ensure that the strongest candidates for the program are selected, please submit a statement of no more than 3 pages as a single .pdf document. This statement describes your record of past experiences and accomplishments related to addressing issues related to inequities in health outcomes including risk, disease progression, outcomes, and service as well as your future plans to address health equity issues through your scholarship/research, teaching/mentoring, and/or service/engagement throughout your career. To the best of your ability, please briefly describe how you plan to work towards greater health equity in your research, teaching, and/or service activities specifically at the UM. All selections will be made solely on the basis of applicants' professional experiences, qualifications, and demonstrated achievements. In keeping with federal and university policy, personal identity characteristics-such as race, gender, national origin, or sexual orientation-will not be considered at any stage of the hiring or selection process. About the UM College of Pharmacy Established in 1876, the UM College of Pharmacy is the first college of pharmacy created within a state-supported university. In addition to the PharmD. program, the College has a B.S. in pharmaceutical sciences, M.S. in Integrated Pharmaceutical Sciences, and three Ph.D. programs (Pharmaceutical Sciences, Medicinal Chemistry, and Clinical Pharmacy). The College of Pharmacy was recently named one of America's top colleges by US News and World Report (currently ranked #2).  Faculty benefit from exceptional research infrastructure and numerous opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across the UM, including Michigan Medicine, the Life Sciences Institute, Rogel Cancer Center, Biointerfaces Institute, Precision Health, and other research centers. For more information about UM-CoP, please visit: https://pharmacy.umich.edu/ . UM Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) Statement The Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, the College of Pharmacy, and the UM are committed to recruiting and retaining an outstanding and diverse faculty. The UM is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to creating an inclusive environment for all faculty, staff, and students. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, national origin, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283076 Working Title Assistant Professor in Pharmaceutical Sciences Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Pharmacy Department PHARMACY Pharmaceutical Sci Posting Begin/End Date 9/14/2026 - 12/13/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282822": {
      "title": "CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR",
      "description": "CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The confidential review of applications will occur as they are received and continue until the position(s) is filled. A cover letter and curriculum vitae must be uploaded online via the http://careers.umich.edu/ (U-M Careers website) for Job ID #282822 In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources at [email protected] . Teaching statement, which should describe teaching philosophy, experience in teaching across programs and settings (i.e., didactic, and clinical) Practice statement Practice Scholarship statement that includes a statement of the impact of his/her work in the first paragraph Service statement If selected to move forward in the process, applicants will be asked to submit additional materials as noted in the School of Nursing Faculty Handbook including professional letters of recommendation, transcripts of your highest degrees, and compliance requirements. For further information regarding these positions please contact [email protected] . Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Nursing (UMSN) is a vibrant community of scholars, educators, clinicians, and innovators embedded within one of the world's leading public research universities. Consistently ranked among the nation's top nursing schools, we are committed to advancing health and health equity through research, education, leadership, and service. Our faculty generate knowledge that improves lives, informs policy, strengthens healthcare delivery, and addresses pressing local and global health challenges. The UMSN invites applications for full-time, University-year, 9-month Clinical Instructor positions beginning January 2027. We seek expert clinicians interested in an integrated role of teaching, clinical practice, service, and scholarly endeavors. Specifically, these individuals will teach in the Undergraduate Nursing Program (BSN) aligned with their area of expertise (e.g., Medical-Surgical, Population Health, Perinatal Health or Reproductive Health, Pediatrics, Behavioral Health) and will provide excellence in clinical education. A successful candidate will engage in nursing practice (minimum of 20% FTE) within their respective specialty area and guide students through scholarly endeavors aimed at improving outcomes and practice. Their nursing practice expertise will aid in curriculum development and inform teaching-related practices. All undergraduate nursing courses are delivered in person, which may require travel throughout southeast Michigan. Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in clinical nursing education, healthcare delivery, and undergraduate student mentorship. Preference will be given to applicants with active clinical engagement and a passion for integrating practice, teaching, service, and scholarship. The University of Michigan is a world-class research institution. The School of Nursing offers a range of accredited educational programs and is consistently ranked among the top nursing schools in the country. Please visit http://www.nursing.umich.edu/ for more information about the School. Required Qualifications* *A Master's degree in nursing or related field is required. *A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). * Desire work that engages in the educational mission, with progressive, successful mastery of teaching skills, and commitment to program advancement. *Demonstrated high-level competence in clinical practice. *Demonstrated excellent communication, networking, and relationship-building skills. *Demonstrated ability to participate in the development and implementation of innovative curricular activities. *A reputation among colleagues for outstanding achievements and recognized contributions in their field of expertise. *Prior to start date, must be in compliance with all health requirements, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies, and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). The employee will promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Modes of Work This position requires a fully on-site presence. Learn more about the work modes here . Additional Information Authorization to work in the U.S. is a precondition of employment. Applicants will not be sponsored for work visas. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. The openings may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veteran's and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282822 Working Title CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR Job Title CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 9/09/2026 - 10/31/2026 Salary $70,000.00 - $70,000.00 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282699": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Business Law",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Business Law | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor of Business Law Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply PLEASE APPLY DIRECTLY THROUGH INTERFOLIO .  Do not apply through the UM Jobs site.  All applications received before October 15, 2026, will receive full consideration. However, applications received after the deadline may be considered until the position is filled. Candidates are required to submit their applications electronically by visiting apply.interfolio.com/ and uploading the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae One or two published or working papers that illustrate major research interests Research statement Teaching statement If available: teaching evaluations Three confidential letters of recommendation The application deadline to ensure full consideration is October 15, 2026. We will continue to accept applications until November 15th, but those applications may not receive consideration. Who We Are The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan is a diverse learning community grounded in the principle that business can be an extraordinary vehicle for positive change in today's dynamic global economy. The Ross School of Business mission is to build a better world through business. Through thought and action, members of the Ross community drive change and innovation that improves business and society. Ross is consistently ranked among the world's leading business schools. Academic degree programs include the BBA, MBA, part-time MBA (Evening and Weekend formats), Executive MBA, Global MBA, Master of Accounting, Master of Supply Chain Management, Master of Management, and PhD. In addition, the school delivers open-enrollment and custom executive education programs targeting general management, leadership development, and strategic human resource management. At Ross, you'll find a collaborative faculty culture and will be empowered to take full advantage of the resources at the University of Michigan and its world-class colleges and schools. Job Summary The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan has an Assistant Professor level position available in Business Law starting in the 2027-28 academic year. Responsibilities* Teaching at the graduate and/or undergraduate level. Research, publishing, and service contributions are required. Required Qualifications* Qualified candidates must have earned a J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school. The candidate must have an excellent academic record and demonstrate a strong interest, and ability, in conducting high-quality, scholarly research in an area relevant to business. Examples of such fields include, but are not limited to, corporate law, contract law, employment law, financial regulation, securities law, intellectual property, law and technology, and international trade.  A qualified candidate must also demonstrate excellence in university teaching or the potential to be an outstanding teacher in business law. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information For more detailed descriptions of the Stephen M. Ross School of Business, and the University of Michigan, please visit our websites: Stephen M. Ross School of Business Business Law Department University of Michigan University of Michigan Benefits The statements included in this description are intended to reflect the general nature and level of work assigned to this classification and should not be interpreted as all-inclusive. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282699 Working Title Assistant Professor of Business Law Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Bus Admin Department Ross School of Business Posting Begin/End Date 9/09/2026 - 11/21/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282591": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Catalan and Spanish Studies",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Catalan and Spanish Studies | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor of Catalan and Spanish Studies Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In addition to applying via careers.umich.edu, please submit the following materials via this Interfolio link: apply.interfolio.com/192614 a cover letter describing the applicant's research and teaching interests current curriculum vitae statements of research and teaching three letters of reference Additional materials, including a writing sample and evidence of teaching effectiveness, may be requested at a later stage. The deadline for submission is October 23, 2026. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of Romance Languages and Literatures at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, invites applications for the Ribas Family Early Career Professorship in Catalan and Romance Languages and Cultures, a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor. This endowed position will expand the Department's strengths in Romance languages, literatures, and cultures through a primary specialization in Catalan Studies, while contributing to the department's broader intellectual and curricular mission across the Romance languages. We seek a dynamic scholar whose research and teaching engage Catalan language, literature, and culture in a broad and innovative manner. We especially welcome candidates whose work places Catalan Studies in conversation with one or more of the following: comparative and transnational approaches, Iberian Studies, Mediterranean Studies, migration and diaspora, race and ethnicity, gender and sexuality, colonial and postcolonial studies, environmental humanities, media and visual culture, translation, multilingualism, and language and cultural contact. The historical period is open. The successful candidate will maintain a vigorous research agenda and contribute to teaching and mentoring at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The position carries a primary responsibility for the development and expansion of the Department's offerings in Catalan language and culture and will also contribute regularly to the Spanish curriculum. This is a full-time, nine-month, university-year appointment covering the fall and winter terms, with an anticipated start date of August 25, 2027. Required Qualifications* Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Catalan Studies, Hispanic Studies, Romance Languages and Literatures, Comparative Literature, or a related field by the time of appointment. Fluency in Catalan and Spanish, together with high competence in English, is required. Knowledge of or scholarly engagement with additional Romance languages and cultures is welcome but not required. Applicants should demonstrate evidence of scholarly excellence or exceptional promise and a strong commitment to effective undergraduate and graduate teaching. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information For any questions, please contact the executive assistant, Department of Romance Languages and Literatures, at [email protected] . Application Deadline The deadline for submission is October 23, 2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282591 Working Title Assistant Professor of Catalan and Spanish Studies Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Romance Languages & Lit. Posting Begin/End Date 9/01/2026 - 10/23/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282601": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor/Clinical Professor Pediatric Otolaryngologist Surgeon",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor/Clinical Professor Pediatric Otolaryngologist Surgeon | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor/Clinical Professor Pediatric Otolaryngologist Surgeon Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested applicants should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, and a list of references to the following email address: [email protected] Please address materials to: Mark E P Prince, M.D. FRCS(C) Charles J Krause M.D., Legacy Professor and Chair Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery 1500 East Medical Center Drive 1904 Taubman Center Ann Arbor, MI 48109-5312 Mission Statement Michigan Medicine improves the health of patients, populations and communities through excellence in education, patient care, community service, research and technology development, and through leadership activities in Michigan, nationally and internationally.  Our mission is guided by our Strategic Principles and has three critical components; patient care, education and research that together enhance our contribution to society. Job Summary The Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery at the University of Michigan has an outstanding opportunity for a Board Certified/Eligible Otolaryngologist with fellowship training or equivalent experience in Pediatric Otolaryngology. We are seeking an individual with skills and experience to practice the full scope of contemporary pediatric otolaryngology. This candidate must have a strong commitment to an academic career, including teaching and a defined area of scholarly interest.  The University of Michigan is located in culturally rich and thriving Ann Arbor, a community known for its walkability, parks, and nearby lakes and rivers. The University of Michigan Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery seeks to recruit and retain a workforce that reflects our commitment to serving all citizens of the State of Michigan, maintaining the excellence of the University, and providing our learners with a broad range of perspectives, experiences, and approaches to learning. The successful candidate will join one of the largest and most highly regarded clinical, teaching and research programs in the country. We offer competitive compensation and a comprehensive benefits package. Required Qualifications* M.D., D.O., Board certification in Otolaryngology, successful completion of a Pediatric Otolaryngology fellowship or similar clinical experience, hold or have the ability to obtain the appropriate medical licenses in the State of Michigan.  Evidence of clinical competence is expected.  Evidence of scholarly ability (publications in peer-reviewed journals, acquisition of external funding, presentation of research at national meetings) and competence in teaching is also expected. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position may be filled at the Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor or Clinical Professor rank based on experience. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third-party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282601 Working Title Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor/Clinical Professor Pediatric Otolaryngologist Surgeon Job Title CLINICAL PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Otolaryngology - HNS Posting Begin/End Date 9/01/2026 - 11/20/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/280385": {
      "title": "ASST PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASST PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASST PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To apply, please submit through the MathJobs website at https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28608 the following: a curriculum vitae; an academic transcript, if less than three years post-Ph.D.; a statement of current and future research plans; a statement of teaching philosophy and experience; evidence of teaching excellence; a statement demonstrating your commitment to service; and up to three selected publications. Please also arrange for three letters of recommendation to be submitted directly through MathJobs. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of Statistics at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning August 25, 2027. This is a university-year (nine-month) appointment. Responsibilities* Applicants in all areas of statistics and data science who have demonstrated outstanding research potential, excellence in teaching, and a commitment to service are encouraged to apply. Required Qualifications* A Ph.D. in Statistics or a related field is required. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information As one of the world's great liberal arts and sciences colleges, LSA pushes the boundaries of what is understood about the human experience and the natural world, and we foster the next generation of rigorous and empathetic thinkers, creators, and contributors to the state of Michigan, the nation, and the world. To learn more about LSA's Mission, Vision and Values, please visit lsa.umich.edu/strategicvision . Background Screening Employment will require a successful criminal background check and candidates must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position may be emailed to [email protected] Application Deadline Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received by November 1 will receive full consideration. For more information about the department, please visit http://www.lsa.umich.edu/stats/ . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 280385 Working Title ASST PROFESSOR Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Statistics Posting Begin/End Date 9/01/2026 - 11/01/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Research Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282260": {
      "title": "Associate Professor w/ tenure",
      "description": "Associate Professor w/ tenure | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Associate Professor w/ tenure Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In addition to applying on careers.umich.edu, applications should be submitted to https://apply.interfolio.com/192299 . Materials should include a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy and experience, samples of creative work (a maximum of 40 written pages and/or links or files of creative work produced), teaching evaluations and other evidence of teaching excellence, and contact information for three letters recommenders. Letter will be requested after the first round of review. Who We Are The Department of Film, Television, and Media offers a variety of courses in screenwriting and television writing that build on the University of Michigan's long and distinguished dramatic writing tradition. With a team of talented and produced screenwriters as lecturers and a variety of unique resources (including a vast library of scripts in the Donald Hall Collection, the James Gindin Visiting Artist Series, and the Peter and Barbara Benedek Artist in Residence Program), the Screenwriting sub major at Michigan provides students with a unique opportunity to learn from accomplished teachers and an array of A-List Hollywood and Independent artists. Job Summary The Department of Film, Television, and Media at the University of Michigan (Ann Arbor) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position to teach in the screenwriting field to begin August 25, 2027. This is a university year appointment and will be at the level of Associate Professor, with tenure. Responsibilities* The successful candidate will teach upper-level screenwriting courses and electives, direct the Screenwriting Program, maintain an active record of writing and production, and contribute to the self-governance of the Department and the College. Required Qualifications* MFA or equivalent Strong record of produced and optioned work Extensive teaching experience at a major university Evidence of leadership experience Demonstrated commitment to program building/fundraising Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form, and must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Review of dossiers will begin on November 1, 2026; applications received after October 30, 2026 may not receive full consideration. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282260 Working Title Associate Professor w/ tenure Job Title ASSOC PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Film,Television,and Media Posting Begin/End Date 8/27/2026 - 10/30/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/281877": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Modern Chinese Culture | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant Professor - Modern Chinese Culture Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In addition to applying on careers.uimich.edu, all application materials should be submitted online through https://apply.interfolio.com/191706 . Materials should include a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy and experience, statement of current and future research plans, a writing sample, teaching evaluations and other evidence of teaching excellence, and three letters of reference. There may be some overlap with your research proposal and teaching statements (1-3 pages). Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of Asian Languages and Cultures at the University of Michigan invites applications for a tenure-track position in modern Chinese culture to begin August 25, 2027. This is a university year appointment and will be at the level of assistant professor. Applicants with interdisciplinary teaching and research interests in modern Chinese culture, including in global and comparative contexts from the late nineteenth through the twenty-first century, are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will teach a range of courses in modern Chinese culture, from introductory undergraduate lecture courses through graduate seminars. They will also supervise doctoral dissertations and participate in the programs of the department as well as in area studies initiatives within the larger university community. Required Qualifications* The successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about the application or materials may be directed to the departmental Chief Administrator, Tricia Chiado ( [email protected] ) Application Deadline Applications must be received by November 1, 2026 to be assured of consideration. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 281877 Working Title Assistant Professor - Modern Chinese Culture Job Title ASST PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Asian Languages & Cultures Posting Begin/End Date 8/19/2026 - 11/02/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/281903": {
      "title": "Lico Family Professorship in Social Work",
      "description": "Lico Family Professorship in Social Work | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Lico Family Professorship in Social Work Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Candidates should visit the online application to submit: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Scholarship statement (up to 2 pages) highlighting research in efforts in homelessness and housing insecurity Teaching/mentoring statement (up to 2 pages) highlighting teaching and mentoring philosophies, and experience in developing courses Three writing samples May include published works, media works, syllabi or detailed schedules for courses and workshops, case examples, and opinion pieces. The application will also collect information about your current employer, degree information, credentials, and full names and email addresses for at least three references. Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later date. Application Timeline Review of applications will begin on October 9 and continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Job Summary Lico Family Professorship in Social Work Position: Tenured Associate/Full Professor The School of Social Work seeks outstanding applicants who will advance the School's mission and leaders in scholarship, education and service for communities in Michigan, the United States and across the world. We seek a distinguished faculty member whose work advances knowledge and solutions to problems of housing insecurity and homelessness, to be appointed as the inaugural holder of the Lico Family Professorship in the School of Social Work. Job Summary The Lico Family Professorship was given to the School of Social Work to expand the School's expertise in innovative solutions to problems of homelessness and housing insecurity that pose barriers to health, wellness, and full social and community inclusion. For the inaugural holder of the Lico Professorship, the School of Social Work seeks an exceptional mid-career or senior scholar with an established research program aimed at informing and driving innovation in policy and programs at the national , state, and local levels by leveraging new technologies, including but not limited to artificial intelligence.  Potentially exciting and avenues could include, but are not limited to, community-engaged research and multi-sector capacity building, inter-agency data integration, digital therapeutic tool development, resource optimization for housing services, and development and scaling of innovative initiatives in affordable housing development, job creation and placement, and eviction prevention. We seek candidates with a strong interdisciplinary and collaborative orientation, the capacity to mentor early-career scholars and doctoral students, a strong record of extramural funding, and an exceptional portfolio of publications and and evidence of impact through dissemination or influence on policy or practice. Required qualifications include holding a PhD in social work or a related discipline and accomplishments commensurate with a tenured appointment at Associate or Professor level. Preferred qualifications include a degree in social work (MSW or PhD). Required Qualifications* A doctoral degree in social work or a related field is required.(MSW or PhD in social work preferred). Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Faculty positions are considered in-person positions and all applicants must eventually live or reside within commuting distance to the School of Social Work in Ann Arbor, MI. Learn more about the work modes . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 281903 Working Title Lico Family Professorship in Social Work Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Social Work Department School of Social Work Posting Begin/End Date 8/19/2026 - 10/18/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/281910": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Professor of Urban Technology (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Professor of Urban Technology (Open Rank) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Tenure-Track Professor of Urban Technology (Open Rank) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit one PDF (500kb size limit) containing the following: Cover letter describing your interest and qualifications for the position Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references For full consideration, please submit your materials by October 16, 2026. Job Summary The University of Michigan (UM) Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning seeks a tenure-track faculty member at the rank of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor to develop field-defining research and teaching in the area of urban technology. We seek applicants whose teaching and research will primarily contribute to our newly approved Master of Urban Technology program , with a planned launch in Fall 2027 at the University of Michigan Center for Innovation in Detroit. The successful candidate will join a vibrant and collaborative community of faculty in the Urban and Regional Planning (URP) Program, home to our highly-ranked Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), the Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, and the Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology. URP faculty interests, research methods, and expertise are diverse, and we consider ourselves an innovative, welcoming, and inclusive community. Our academic programs span the fields of urban planning, real estate, and urban technology. This position is being made specifically to expand capacities to launch a Master's of Urban Technology (MUT), which will be offered at the University of Michigan Center for Innovation (UMCI) in Detroit. Consistent with the overall model for UMCI, all academic programs and faculty who teach at UMCI are housed and administered by U-M Ann Arbor units. Research: We seek applicants whose research advances the urban technology field, which encompasses data, AI, and digital technologies applied to the work of the built environment. We are particularly interested in research that incorporates private-sector perspectives on how urban spaces are planned, realized, operated, and governed, which may include emerging industry areas such as contech, proptech, and AECtech. This research may be in collaboration with other activities at UMCI, Taubman College, and the University as a whole. This may include participation in existing or new research initiatives aligned with the Look to Michigan impact areas, and/or future Taubman College research priorities, and the URP Strategic Plan. Teaching: Successful applicants must be qualified to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate level. The standard teaching load at Taubman College is four courses per year, and all teaching will occur in person. We expect this faculty member to teach three courses per year in Detroit for the MUT program, and one per year in Ann Arbor in one of the existing degree offerings, depending on the candidate's expertise . We especially seek instructors qualified to teach courses in entrepreneurship, digital product development, urban data, AI applied to the built environment, and evidence-based design. Service: Faculty will also carry service obligations consistent with interest and career rank, and may include service on committees and the potential to serve in academic leadership positions within the College. This new Master of Urban Technology (MUT) is a 12-month degree completed in 3 semesters. The first semester, under the thematic banner of examining a Radically Better built environment, provides coursework offering in-depth analysis of urban sustainability challenges, a survey of the current landscape of innovation in the sector, and a foundation in data science skills. The second semester, called Radically Integrated, deepens an analysis of cities by introducing a systems perspective, and digging deeper into the industry through immersion in the regional ecosystem. Students are invited to begin exploring creative ideas to deploy innovations using analytics and design. Finally, the third semester, Radically Possible, equips students with the skills to bring their ideas to life through coursework on business models, finance, change management, and a culminating Capstone project. Vision and Values: The mission of the Urban and Regional Planning Program is to lead the way for better urban and regional futures. We aim to build knowledge, develop capabilities, and imagine possibilities to cultivate flourishing communities, guided by four core values: justice-driven, environmentally minded, future-focused, and action-oriented. Required Qualifications* Terminal degree from a related technical or design discipline, such as design, urban planning, architecture, digital media, human-computer interaction, geography, engineering, or information science. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential to, conduct research in the area of urban technology and design. Additional Information In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, U-M offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include a retirement plan with two-for-one matching contributions after the first year; many choices for comprehensive health insurance; life insurance; long-term disability coverage; and flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses. Learn more about U-M benefits . The university is responsive to the needs of dual career couples . About Taubman College and the Urban and Regional Planning Program Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning is one of the leading schools of architecture and planning in the United States, offering degree programs in architecture, urban and regional planning, urban design, and urban technology. The college is housed in a recently expanded studio, classroom, and research complex on the University of Michigan's North Campus, alongside design fabrication labs, visualization facilities, and dedicated space for student-led research and collaboration. The Urban and Regional Planning Program offers a Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), a Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, a Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology, an undergraduate minor in real estate, and a graduate certificate in real estate development. The MURP is consistently ranked among the top planning programs in the country, and the BS in Urban Technology is the first program of its kind in the United States, preparing students to design and govern the data-rich cities of the future. New faculty join a community of approximately 40 planning faculty and 500 students-200 studying real estate-with access to the resources of one of the world's preeminent research universities. About the University of Michigan Our location offers a rare advantage: faculty and students work at the doorstep of metropolitan Detroit, one of the most distinctive real estate and planning landscapes in North America, and within easy reach of Chicago, Toronto, and other major markets in the Great Lakes region. The setting supports applied research, partnerships with public agencies and developers, and project-based learning that few peer programs can match. For questions about the opportunity, contact Taubman College Human Resources at [email protected] . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 281910 Working Title Tenure-Track Professor of Urban Technology (Open Rank) Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Coll Arch Urn Pln Department Urban Planning Posting Begin/End Date 8/19/2026 - 10/17/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/280148": {
      "title": "Chief, Division of General Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine",
      "description": "Chief, Division of General Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Chief, Division of General Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested applicants may apply with a CV and a cover letter outlining their interest and qualifications, addressed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Stephanie Taylor, and emailed to silvanaj@med.umich.edu. Job Summary The University of Michigan's Department of Internal Medicine invites applications for the position of Chief of the Division of General Medicine. The division chief is responsible for the academic, clinical, educational and administrative vision, oversight and initiatives of the division. Candidates should have demonstrated scholarly distinction at the associate professor or professor level and demonstrate capacity for leadership. Candidates eligible for appointment to the tenure track are strongly preferred. This position reports directly to the Chair of the Department of Internal Medicine. The primary responsibilities of this position include, but are not limited to the following: Effectively manage a comprehensive multi-site clinical operation Oversee and expand a robust research operation funded by federal and non-federal grants Enhance educational opportunities for medical students and residents, within the division Recruit and develop the best and the brightest faculty to enhance and grow the Division's national reputation The division chief is paired with a division administrator who fully supports the administrative needs of the division. Together, they will develop a strong and effective working relationship to support the strategic direction of the division. More information about this position can be found at the link here . Required Qualifications* Candidates must possess an M.D. or equivalent degree, board eligible/certified in internal medicine and should be, or be qualified to be, licensed in the State of Michigan. Underfill Statement This position can be filled as an Associate Professor/Professor. Background Screening Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 280148 Working Title Chief, Division of General Medicine, Department of Internal Medicine Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Int Med-General Medicine Posting Begin/End Date 7/15/2026 - 11/14/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/279407": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor of Technology & Operations",
      "description": "Associate Professor/Professor of Technology & Operations | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Associate Professor/Professor of Technology & Operations Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants are required to submit their applications electronically by visiting https://apply.interfolio.com/188537 and uploading the following: cover letter curriculum vitae research statement, one or two published or working papers that illustrate a major research interest teaching statement and teaching evaluations The application deadline is October 15, 2026. For questions related to the application process, please contact Shandra Bremer: [email protected] , Area Administrator. Who We Are The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan is a diverse learning community grounded in the principle that business can be an extraordinary vehicle for positive change in today's dynamic global economy. The Ross School of Business mission is to build a better world through business. Through thought and action, members of the Ross community drive change and innovation that improve business and society. Michigan Ross is consistently ranked among the world's leading business schools. Academic degree programs include the Bachelor of Business Administration, Full-Time MBA, Part-Time MBA (Online, and Weekend formats), Executive MBA, Global MBA, Master of Accounting, Master of Business Analytics, Master of Management, Master of Supply Chain Management, and PhD. In addition, the school delivers open-enrollment and custom executive education programs targeting general management, leadership development, and strategic human resource management. For more information, visit MichiganRoss.umich.edu. Job Summary The Stephen M. Ross School of Business at the University of Michigan invites applications for a tenured faculty position (Associate or Full Professor) in the Technology and Operations area, with a research focus on sustainable and resilient semiconductor manufacturing and supply-chain operations. This appointment is part of a major interdisciplinary faculty cluster -- Sustainable Semiconductors for a Secure Future -- that spans the Ross School of Business and the College of Engineering (Computer Science and Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, and Materials Science and Engineering). Required Qualifications* The ideal candidate would have research expertise on sustainability (e.g., low-carbon manufacturing, circular economy), supply chain resiliency (e.g., risk assessment, supply chain design), and/or advanced manufacturing (e.g., semiconductor, electronics). We are especially interested in individuals with a track record of interdisciplinary collaboration with engineering, materials, or computer science colleagues; experience translating research through industry partnerships, pilot deployments, or policy engagement; a history of external research funding from federal agencies and/or industry; and experience mentoring a diverse community of students and junior researchers. Desired Qualifications* Minimum requirements include a doctorate in Business Administration, Industrial Engineering or related field such as Economics, Statistics, or Computer Science, and a record of excellence in business research, teaching, and leadership commensurate with appointment rank. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the selected candidate's qualifications. Additional Information For more detailed descriptions of the Technology and Operations Area, Stephen M. Ross School of Business, and the University of Michigan, please visit our websites: Technology and Operations Area Stephen M. Ross School of Business University of Michigan University of Michigan Benefits Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 279407 Working Title Associate Professor/Professor of Technology & Operations Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Bus Admin Department Ross School of Business Posting Begin/End Date 6/28/2026 - 1/03/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/278043": {
      "title": "ASSOC PROFESSOR / PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSOC PROFESSOR / PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASSOC PROFESSOR / PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply All applicants should submit, in PDF format (1) a detailed resume, (2) a statement of research and teaching interests, (3) up to three representative publications, and (4) the names and contact information of at least three referees. Applications must be submitted electronically at http://me.engin.umich.edu/facultysearch. For best consideration, candidates are encouraged to apply now and certainly before October 30, 2026 as applications will be reviewed immediately upon receipt. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement - enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Department of Mechanical Engineering (ME), University of Michigan (U-M), Ann Arbor, seeks outstanding scholars to apply for one senior tenure track position (associate or full professor). This position is part of a larger cluster hire that includes the Mechanical Engineering Department, the Computer Science and Engineering Department, and the Ross School of Business; the goal of the cluster is to position Michigan as a national leader in cognitive manufacturing, which is the intersection of next generation artificial intelligence, advanced manufacturing, and supply chain resilience. Responsibilities* We expect the faculty member to work closely with faculty in other units to advance next-generation resilient manufacturing systems, including resilient supply chains. All who have strong backgrounds in disciplines relevant to mechanical engineering and the manufacturing sciences are welcome to apply. We are especially interested in individuals who are committed to developing collaborative research programs and engaging in undergraduate and graduate teaching as well as eliminating systemic racism and sexism in STEM fields. Required Qualifications* Applicants must have an earned Ph.D. in mechanical engineering or a related field.  We seek strong scholars who will provide inspiration, leadership, and impact in research, teaching, and service and contribute to the goals of the University and Department. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Additional Information The U-M Mechanical Engineering Department is the home of 60 tenured/tenure-track faculty, 15 research faculty, over 700 graduate students (around 280 Ph.D. students) and 700 undergraduate students. The U-M ME Department is well known for its strengths in major mechanical engineering core disciplines as well as in interdisciplinary and emerging thematic areas. The Department is consistently ranked among the top nationwide and worldwide by various ranking systems, such as the QS World rankings, the US News and World Report, and the National Research Council Ph.D. Program evaluation. More information about the Department can be found at: http://me.engin.umich.edu/ . The University of Michigan has a long and distinguished history.  It was founded in 1817, twenty years before the territory became a state, and was one of the first public universities in the nation. Throughout its nearly 200-year history, it has maintained the highest levels of education, scholarship, and research. Ann Arbor, a vibrant mid-sized city known for its sprawling greenery and exposed-brick facades, its array of enticing eateries and historic theaters, and, of course, Michigan Football, is regularly ranked as one of the best places to live in the nation. The U-M campus is also less than a thirty-minute drive from the Detroit Metropolitan Airport, a hub for major airlines like Delta, Air Canada, Air France, and American Airlines with over 400 flights to and from Europe, East Asia, and every corner of the United States every day. From Ann Arbor, the Michigan Flyer and D2A2 are very accessible bus services that connect the U-M Ann Arbor campus with DTW and all the offerings of downtown Detroit, like the Detroit Riverwalk, the Detroit Institute of Art, and the world-class eateries in Hamtramck. Learn more about Life in Ann Arbor on YouTube. Additional Information The University of Michigan is responsive to the needs of dual career families. Underrepresented minority and women candidates are strongly encouraged to apply. Background Screening Employment will require a criminal background check and may require an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. If an institutional reference check is necessary, candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 278043 Working Title ASSOC PROFESSOR / PROFESSOR Job Title ASSOC PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Mechanical Engineering Posting Begin/End Date 6/03/2026 - 10/30/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/278295": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Professor of Land Use and Resilience (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Professor of Land Use and Resilience (Open Rank) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Tenure-Track Professor of Land Use and Resilience (Open Rank) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit one PDF (500kb size limit) containing the following: Cover letter describing your interest in and qualifications for the position Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references For full consideration, please submit your materials by October 16, 2026. Job Summary The University of Michigan (UM) Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning seeks a tenure-track faculty member at the rank of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor to develop field-defining research and teaching in land use and resilience planning. The successful candidate will join a vibrant and collaborative community of faculty in the Urban and Regional Planning (URP) Program, home to our highly-ranked Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), the Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, and the Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology. URP faculty interests, research methods, and expertise are diverse, and we consider ourselves an innovative, welcoming, and inclusive community. Research: We seek applicants whose research advances the intersection of land use and climate resilience, with expertise that focuses on the United States and/or a global and comparative lens. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work addresses pressing challenges, including community resilience and sustainability; climate mitigation, vulnerability reduction, or adaptation to a changing climate; disaster preparedness, planning and recovery; indigenous planning; or other land use and resilience-related topics. We welcome applicants whose work examines disparities in exposure to environmental hazards and climate risks, and inequalities in access to environmental benefits and protections. Potential collaborations across campus may include the Graham Sustainability Institute, the School for Environment and Sustainability, School of Public Health, and others. We are particularly interested in scholars whose research demonstrates strong potential for policy relevance, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration, and we invite diverse methodological approaches. Teaching: We seek applicants qualified to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, including one or more current or future core MURP offerings and electives that align with the Land Use and Environmental Planning and/or Sustainability, Resilience, Adaptation, and Climate Change focus areas. The standard teaching load at Taubman College is four courses per year, and all teaching will occur in person in Ann Arbor. Vision and Values: The mission of the Urban and Regional Planning Program is to lead the way for better urban and regional futures. We aim to build knowledge, develop capabilities, and imagine possibilities to cultivate flourishing communities, guided by four core values: justice-driven, environmentally minded, future-focused, and action-oriented. Required Qualifications* Terminal degree (Ph.D. preferred) in urban and regional planning or a related discipline, such as environment and sustainability, geography, indigenous studies, international development, landscape architecture, planning law, public administration, political science, public policy, or sociology. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential to, conduct research in the area of land use and resilience. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in teaching and mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. Additional Information In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, U-M offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include a retirement plan with two-for-one matching contributions after the first year; many choices for comprehensive health insurance; life insurance; long-term disability coverage; and flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses. Learn more about U-M benefits. The university is responsive to the needs of dual career couples . About Taubman College and the Urban and Regional Planning Program Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning is one of the leading schools of architecture and planning in the United States, offering degree programs in architecture, urban and regional planning, urban design, and urban technology. The college is housed in a recently expanded studio, classroom, and research complex on the University of Michigan's North Campus, alongside design fabrication labs, visualization facilities, and dedicated space for student-led research and collaboration. The Urban and Regional Planning Program offers a Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), a Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, and the Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology. The MURP is consistently ranked among the top planning programs in the country, and the BS in Urban Technology is the first program of its kind in the United States, preparing students to design and govern the data-rich cities of the future. New faculty join a community of approximately 40 URP instructors and 500 students 200 studying real estate with access to the resources of one of the world's preeminent research universities. About the University of Michigan Our location offers a rare advantage: faculty and students work at the doorstep of metropolitan Detroit, one of the most distinctive urban landscapes in North America, and within easy reach of Chicago, Toronto and the Great Lakes region. U-M researchers have cultivated a number of unique assets from a longitudinal survey of the state's 1,856 local governments, to zoning databases, to a growing number of Tribal government partnerships that support applied research, partnerships, and project-based learning across a unique rural-urban land use spectrum that few peer programs can match. For questions about the opportunity, contact Taubman College Human Resources at [email protected] . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 278295 Working Title Tenure-Track Professor of Land Use and Resilience (Open Rank) Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Coll Arch Urn Pln Department Urban Planning Posting Begin/End Date 6/01/2026 - 10/16/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/277886": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Professor of Real Estate Development (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Professor of Real Estate Development (Open Rank) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Tenure-Track Professor of Real Estate Development (Open Rank) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit one PDF (500kb size limit) containing the following: Cover letter describing your interest in and qualifications for the position Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references Optional: A portfolio of no more than 10 pages submitted by web link or PDF attachment For full consideration, please submit your materials by October 16, 2026. Job Summary The University of Michigan Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor to develop field-defining research and teaching in real estate. The successful candidate will join a vibrant and collaborative community of faculty in the Urban and Regional Planning (URP) Program, home to our highly ranked Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), the Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, and the Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology. The successful candidate will play a central role in shaping a fast-growing real estate academic portfolio that includes a newly launched undergraduate minor in real estate and an established graduate certificate in real estate development. The portfolio is augmented by the Weiser Center for Real Estate, which hosts an active program of professional engagement and events. Research: We seek applicants whose research advances real estate and intersects with areas such as land and property markets, regulation, public-private partnerships, urban economics, and community ownership models. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work addresses timely problems, including housing affordability, sustainability, climate resilience, and urban development policy. The successful candidate will provide thought leadership for the professional real estate field and help our students translate that scholarship into practice. Teaching: We seek applicants qualified to teach courses in our real estate area at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, with the opportunity to develop signature courses for the real estate minor and the graduate certificate in real estate development. The candidate will also teach one or more current or future electives and core offerings in the MURP curriculum, such as public economics or fiscal planning, or potentially courses in urban technology. The standard teaching load at Taubman College is four courses per year, and all teaching will occur in person in Ann Arbor. Service: The successful candidate will be affiliated with the Weiser Center for Real Estate, contribute significant service to the unit, and may have the opportunity to direct the graduate certificate in real estate development and/or the undergraduate minor in real estate programs. These roles offer a meaningful platform to shape curriculum, programming, and the next generation of real estate leadership at Michigan. Vision and Values: The mission of the Urban and Regional Planning Program is to lead the way for better urban and regional futures. We aim to build knowledge, develop capabilities, and imagine possibilities to cultivate flourishing communities, guided by four core values: justice-driven, environmentally minded, future-focused, and action-oriented. Required Qualifications* Terminal degree (Ph.D. preferred) in urban and regional planning, real estate (finance or development), business administration, or related fields. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential for, conducting research in the areas of real estate that intersect with urban and regional planning or urban technology. Demonstrated experience in, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. Desired Qualifications* Knowledge of and/or experience with the real estate industry and its networks. Familiarity with analytic tools used in real estate research and practice. Commitment to the mission and values of the Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning. Additional Information In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, U-M offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family, and plan for a secure future. Benefits include a retirement plan with two-for-one matching contributions after the first year; many choices for comprehensive health insurance; life insurance; long-term disability coverage; and flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses. Learn more about U-M benefits . The university is responsive to the needs of dual career couples . About Taubman College and the Urban and Regional Planning Program Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning is one of the leading schools of architecture and planning in the United States, offering degree programs in architecture, urban and regional planning, urban design, and urban technology. The college is housed in a recently expanded studio, classroom, and research complex on the University of Michigan's North Campus, alongside design fabrication labs, visualization facilities, and dedicated space for student-led research and collaboration. The Urban and Regional Planning Program offers a Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), a Ph.D. in Urban and Regional Planning, a Bachelor of Science in Urban Technology, an undergraduate minor in real estate, and a graduate certificate in real estate development. The MURP is consistently ranked among the top planning programs in the country, and the BS in Urban Technology is the first program of its kind in the United States, preparing students to design and govern the data-rich cities of the future. New faculty join a community of approximately 40 planning faculty and 500 students-200 studying real estate-with access to the resources of one of the world's preeminent research universities. Our location offers a rare advantage: faculty and students work at the doorstep of metropolitan Detroit, one of the most distinctive real estate and planning landscapes in North America, and within easy reach of Chicago, Toronto, and other major markets in the Great Lakes region. The setting supports applied research, partnerships with public agencies and developers, and project-based learning that few peer programs can match. About Real Estate at Michigan Real estate at the University of Michigan brings together faculty, students, and industry leaders across Taubman College, the Stephen M. Ross School of Business, the Law School, and the College of Engineering, creating one of the most distinctive real estate communities in higher education. The undergraduate minor in real estate offers students from across the university a structured path into the field. The graduate certificate in real estate development draws students from planning, business, law, architecture, and engineering into a multidisciplinary credential anchored in development practice. Both programs feature site visits, treks, case competitions, case studies, and capstone projects with leading developers, public agencies, and community-based organizations across Michigan and beyond. Students engage with practitioners through the Michigan Real Estate Forum, the annual Weiser Real Estate Conference, and a growing network of alumni working in development, investment, public finance, and community real estate. About the Weiser Center for Real Estate The Weiser Center for Real Estate is the University of Michigan's hub for real estate research, teaching, and industry engagement. The Center convenes faculty across schools, supports student fellowships and applied research, and hosts conferences, lectures, and practitioner-in-residence programs that connect Michigan to leaders in development, finance, public policy, and community practice. Faculty affiliated with the Weiser Center benefit from dedicated programming support, graduate student funding, and a broad network of alumni and industry partners. For questions about the opportunity, contact Taubman College Human Resources at [email protected] . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 277886 Working Title Tenure-Track Professor of Real Estate Development (Open Rank) Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Coll Arch Urn Pln Department Urban Planning Posting Begin/End Date 5/19/2026 - 10/16/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/251580": {
      "title": "DIVISION DIRECTOR-PEDIATRIC GASTROENTEROLOGY",
      "description": "DIVISION DIRECTOR-PEDIATRIC GASTROENTEROLOGY | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ DIVISION DIRECTOR-PEDIATRIC GASTROENTEROLOGY Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested candidates are asked to submit a letter of interest along with a current CV to Dr. David Kershaw ( [email protected] ) . Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Priority will be given to applications received by July 1, 2026. Review of Candidates Send cover letter and CV to: David Kershaw, MD, FAAP Professor 12-250 CS Mott Pediatric Nephrology 1540 E. Hospital Drive SPC 4297 Ann Arbor, Michigan  48109-4279 Email: [email protected] Job Summary The Department of Pediatrics of the University of Michigan Health System invites applications from qualified faculty specializing in Pediatric Gastroenterology to lead its Division of Gastroenterology. The Division Director position is a full-time appointment reporting directly to the Chair of the Department of Pediatrics. This highly ranked division is comprised of 16 faculty members and has robust clinical programs in inflammatory bowel disease, motility disorders, eosinophilic esophagitis, intestinal rehabilitation, pancreatic disease, advanced endoscopy, hepatology, and liver transplantation. The division has multiple clinical research studies and high-profile quality improvement activities in addition to an academic fellowship training program with five current fellows. Responsibilities include maintaining and growing a strong independent research program, overseeing clinical care delivery, promoting research activities, and engaging in the training and professional development of students, house officers, fellows, and junior faculty. Department of Pediatrics and C.S. Mott Children's Hospital The Department of Pediatrics includes 17 clinical divisions and over 330 faculty members. C.S. Mott Children's Hospital, a 1.1 million square foot, 12 story, 348-bed facility that opened in 2011, is located on the University of Michigan medical campus. Mott is honored to be the #1 children's hospital in Michigan and ranked in the top 3 in the Midwest by U.S. News and World Report. Mott is also the only hospital in Michigan ranked in all 10 specialties. This state-of-the-art facility includes the on-site Von Voigtlander Women's Hospital birthing center, a Level 1 Children's Surgery Center, and a Level 1 Pediatric Trauma Center. Ann Arbor and the University of Michigan Ann Arbor is consistently ranked among the top places to live in the United States combining the atmosphere of a small college town with the cultural and recreational opportunities of a larger city. The University offers a variety of cultural, educational, and sports activities. Ann Arbor and the University provide a continuous bill of concerts, plays, lectures, festivals, as well as galleries and museums. The downtown area offers a variety of specialty shops, bookstores, restaurants, and an outdoor farmers' market. The city and University also offer extensive parks and gardens as well as a variety of outdoor activities, including biking, canoeing, and running. Detroit Metropolitan Airport (DTW), a half-hour drive from Ann Arbor, offers easy access to major cities in the U.S. and internationally (https://medschool.umich.edu/our-community). Required Qualifications* Required Qualifications Board Certification in Pediatric Gastroenterology Desired Qualifications* Preferred Qualifications The ideal Division Director candidate will have a robust understanding of pediatric gastroenterology operations, a good track record of grant funding, knowledge of the broad scope of gastroenterology research, and be able to effectively lead, mentor, and expand the clinical, research, and education programs of the division. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend on qualifications. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 251580 Working Title DIVISION DIRECTOR-PEDIATRIC GASTROENTEROLOGY Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Pediatrics-Gastroenterology Posting Begin/End Date 7/12/2024 - 12/31/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284391": {
      "title": "Adjunct Clinical Professor / International Education Programs Director",
      "description": "Adjunct Clinical Professor / International Education Programs Director | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Adjunct Clinical Professor / International Education Programs Director Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Job Summary The University of Michigan Law School is seeking to hire an Adjunct Clinical Professor/International Education Programs Director to direct off-site, international educational programs including administering and teaching our highly-regarded Geneva International Fellows Program and designing and directing our new program of faculty-led, short-term, study abroad programs. The position is based in Geneva, Switzerland for at least four months each year and involves some travel to the United States and elsewhere. This is a full-time position. The Geneva International Fellows Program is a 14-credit course that takes place January-May and combines field placements with leading international organizations (IOs) and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) in Geneva with a contemporaneous seminar intended to provide substantive context for the externships. The program provides Michigan Law students the opportunity to develop their knowledge and understanding of public international law, the role and function of IOs and NGS, the practice of various specialty areas in the international context as well as to develop professional and lawyering skills. The Adjunct Clinical Professor is responsible for helping to recruit, select, and orient students to the program; helping students secure their field placements in Geneva; assisting students with visa entry requirements, etc., helping students adjust to life in Geneva; following the work of students in their field placements (including through liaison with the students' supervisors), serving as their primary contact in Geneva, and organizing a 2-credit course for the students during the winter semester. Additionally, the successful candidate will design and direct a new program of faculty-led, short-term study abroad opportunities for Michigan Law students. As part of a significant expansion of international opportunities for JD students, the Adjunct Clinical Professor/International Education Programs Director will work collaboratively with international education professionals across the University of Michigan and at peer institutions to create a portfolio of opportunities that align with the mission of the law school and leverage the strengths of our faculty and international alumni. The candidate will work within the Center for International and Comparative Law and ensure that all new and existing credit-bearing international opportunities follow best practices for short-term study abroad and evaluate and implement the administrative and compliance requirements for the varied credit-bearing international opportunities open to JDs. Responsibilities* Managing all aspects of the University of Michigan Law School's Geneva International Fellows Program; Identifying potential placement sites and liaising with IOs and NGOs to facilitate externship placements; Working with students to select placement choices, draft application materials, field administrative questions, and advocating with placement sites for their hiring; Teaching the accompanying Michigan Law Geneva International Fellows seminar, focusing on key areas such as international law, human rights, global governance, and experiential learning. Evaluating student performance during externships and in the seminar through regular assessments. Design a short-term study abroad program framework, identify existing models and best practices, and work with Associate Dean and Assistant Dean for International Affairs to select faculty and staff to lead international opportunities; Promote and support short term study abroad programs: MIGOs, class/clinic travel weeks, and existing short-term travel-study options (Japan and Tanzania); Work collaboratively with Michigan Law faculty to design weeklong academic credit programs to ensure academic quality and safety; Support students finding and securing non-Geneva international externships; and Support the international activities of Michigan Law including alumni relations, development, and graduate admissions activities in Europe and elsewhere. Required Qualifications* The qualified candidate should: Hold a J.D. from an accredited U.S. law school, though consideration may be given to those with equivalent exposure to U.S. legal education; Have a deep understanding of the Geneva international community, the IOs (in particular UN agencies), NGOs, diplomatic missions, and adjacent legal community including knowledge of the substantive mandate, institutional organization, and bureaucratic (in particular human resource-related) workings of these institutions and offices within them; Have knowledge of best practices for academic outcomes and safety in international education, work-study, or study abroad programs; Have experience teaching law students and designing innovative curriculum in a highly regulated academic environment; Have deep familiarity with U.S. legal education through time spent in the United States or equivalent exposure. Have a legal right to work and reside in Switzerland and travel to the United States for short term work assignments. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information For applicants who applied to the .4 FTE position (Assistant Clinical Professor/Geneva Director) listed in September 2026, you will be automatically considered for this role and need not apply again. Such candidates will be contacted about this full-time position and offered the opportunity to submit additional materials by the hiring committee if they are going to be evaluated as a semi-finalist or finalist for this role. **Please note:  This is position is part of an additional position which is a two-year contract with the preponderance of the work taking place during the period from early January until early May, while the Michigan students are in residence in Geneva. Please direct questions to the Law School's Externship Director, Amy Sankaran, at [email protected] . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284391 Working Title Adjunct Clinical Professor / International Education Programs Director Job Title ADJUNCT CLINICAL PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Mobile/Remote Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Law School Department Law School Posting Begin/End Date 10/14/2026 - 10/21/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284277": {
      "title": "CLINICAL LECTURER",
      "description": "CLINICAL LECTURER | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CLINICAL LECTURER Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Interested individuals should submit a CV and the names and contact information of three to five references to: Manager, Academic Human Resources | Department of Pathology and Clinical Laboratories | 2800 Plymouth Rd, Building 35, Faculty HR Rm 30-1560 | Ann Arbor, MI 48109-2800 Job Summary The Department of Pathology at Michigan Medicine has open advanced learner positions within the Divisions of Anatomic and Clinical Pathology. We are seeking candidates who will participate in the teaching and clinical-based research activities within the Department of Pathology. Candidates will supervise the activities of House Officers and Medical Students. Required Qualifications* Doctor of Medicine degree (or equivalent), American Board of Pathology board certified or board eligible in AP/CP, AP or CP. Hold or have the ability to obtain the appropriate medical licenses in the State of Michigan. Evidence of clinical competence and competence in clinical and didactic teaching is expected. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284277 Working Title CLINICAL LECTURER Job Title CLINICAL LECTURER Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Pathology Department Posting Begin/End Date 10/12/2026 - 10/19/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
