    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284371": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer - AI in Appellate Practice | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer - AI in Appellate Practice Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Please also include a list of states in which you have ever been licensed to practice law. Who We Are Founded in 1859, Michigan Law School is one of the world's leading institutions of legal education and enjoys a reputation for academic excellence. Michigan is consistently ranked among the top law schools in the nation, and enjoys international renown for its intellectual rigor, stunning physical surroundings, preeminent faculty scholarship, and a community noted for collegiality and warmth. Michigan Law has an exceptional and cooperative community of talented and interesting students, with 1000 in the JD and 40 in the LLM program.  Our graduates serve with distinction in the private, public, non-profit, and academic sectors, in large numbers in every major market in the nation, as well as internationally. The University of Michigan is located in Ann Arbor, Michigan, which is considered among the best places in the United States to live and work. Job Summary Reporting to the Associate Dean for Academic Programming, the University of Michigan Law School is hiring a LEO intermittent lecturer to teach a two-credit course (20% effort for the semester) on AI in Appellate Practice during the winter 2027 semester. The selected candidate will be expected to teach, hold regular office hours, evaluate students' performance, and assign grades. Required Qualifications* - JD degree or equivalent - 10+ years of legal experience - Experience teaching courses on AI in Appellate Practice Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information This appointment will begin on January 1, 2027, and will end on April 30, 2027. The appointment opportunity as posted is subject to the University of Michigan Lecturers' Employee Organization collective bargaining agreement. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284371 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer - AI in Appellate Practice Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Law School Department Law School Posting Begin/End Date 10/14/2026 - 10/24/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284322": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants should submit one PDF (500kb size limit) containing a letter describing their interest in and qualifications for the position, a curriculum vitae, names and contact information for three references. Applications are due by October 23, 2026. If you have questions about how to apply, please contact Taubman College HR at [email protected] . Job Summary The Architecture Program at the University of Michigan Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning seeks applicants for a one semester position at the rank of Intermittent Lecturer in the architectural history curriculum. The expected effort for the appointment is 25% per course. Course Description: History of Architecture II This course examines the global histories of modernist architectures in the sociocultural, political, and economic contexts in which they were theorized, conceptualized, created, deployed, critiqued, co-opted, adopted, and rejected. It will explore these histories through both hegemonic and counter hegemonic frameworks that scholars and critics have employed such as alternative modernism, and global modernisms. Throughout, the course will examine architectural modernism's historical antecedents, the various manifestations and offshoots, as well as the philosophies and forms that developed in response. This course will run in multiple concurrent sections of different thematic foci during the winter 2027 semester (January through mid-May). Responsibilities* Teach one course including lecture and two discussion sections in winter semester, all in-person in Ann Arbor, MI Prepare course materials including syllabi and supplemental presentations Design, evaluate and grade assignments and provide timely feedback Design, administer, and grade examinations Maintain student attendance records, grades, and other required records Maintain regularly scheduled office hours in order to advise and assist students Required Qualifications* Doctoral degree in architecture with a focus on architectural history, or an equivalent degree from a related discipline Previous experience teaching architecture history courses Additional Information In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, U-M offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well. Learn more about U-M benefits eligibility . Please note: Applicants must be authorized to work in the U.S. without requiring visa sponsorship. This position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. The salary for this position is distributed from September through April . Our Shared Values We aim to create a community of mutual respect and trust where everyone's backgrounds, identities, and views are represented without any threat of bias, harassment, intimidation, or discrimination. Learn more about the shared values we have for our community. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours, and other terms and conditions of employment. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. The posting will close on October 23, 2026. Decision Making Process Review of applications may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. Final hiring approval is made by the dean in consultation with the program chair, and offers are expected to be made by December 14, 2026. The selection criteria include meeting the required qualifications listed above and the demonstrated potential to design and conduct energetic, active, collaborative learning experiences. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284322 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Coll Arch Urn Pln Department Architecture Posting Begin/End Date 10/13/2026 - 10/23/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283982": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement - enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The Climate and Space Science department in the College of Engineering is seeking a Lecturer I to develop and teach course focused on \"Extreme Weather CLIMATE 102.001. The course is 3 credits and will run during the coming winter semester (January 1 - April 30, 2027). Climate 102 is an introduction to the physics of extreme weather events, using thunderstorms, floods, lake-effect snowstorms, lightning, thunder, hail, hurricanes, and tornadoes to illustrate the physical laws governing the atmosphere. Students also apply these principles through hands-on storm forecasting and weather analysis assignments. Responsibilities* Duties of the Lecturer I position are expected to include teaching, developing course materials, evaluating and grading students, and holding regularly scheduled office hours. Required Qualifications* Ph.D. in atmospheric science, meteorology, or related field Demonstrated knowledge of extreme weather, atmospheric processes, and related climate or environmental topics. Demonstrated experience teaching, instructing, or communicating scientific concepts to large classes of undergraduate students or other audiences. Excellent written and oral communication skills Modes of Work The work requires a fully onsite presence. Onsite is defined as a designated U-M owned or leased work location within or outside of the State of Michigan. Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. Decision Making Process Applications may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. Final hiring approval is made by the dean in consultation with the program chair. The selection criteria include meeting the required qualifications listed above and the demonstrated potential to design and conduct energetic, active, collaborative learning experiences. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283982 Working Title LEO Lecturer I Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Climate & Space Sci Eng Posting Begin/End Date 10/13/2026 - 10/27/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284188": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - N262 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - N262 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through the U-M Careers website. A cover letter of introduction explaining your specific interest in the position and outlining skills and experience that directly relate to this position and CV must be uploaded as one document. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources - LEO Lecturer (N262 Nursing Therapies I) to [email protected] (electronic materials). *Cover letter *Current curriculum vita *Evidence of didactic and clinical teaching experience (teaching statement) *Three (3) letters of professional recommendation *Copy of your nursing diploma *Copy of your nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position *Any certifications For further information regarding this position please contact [email protected] . Who We Are The University of Michigan is a world-class research-intensive, enriched and highly collaborative institution. The UMSN offers a full range of accredited educational programs and is consistently ranked among the top nursing schools in the country. Please visit our website at http://www.nursing.umich.edu/ for more information. Job Summary The School of Nursing (UMSN), Undergraduate Program is recruiting nursing professionals with clinical experience in medical/surgical nursing to teach clinical PNEs associated with course N262 - Nursing Therapies I. Each PNE is 4 credits or 33.33% FTE. The days/times are as follows (clinical site may be subject to change): Tuesday, 2:00 p.m. -11:30 p.m. Michigan Medicine Thursday, 6:30 a.m -4:00 p.m. Michigan Medicine Friday 6:30 a.m -4:00 p.m. Michigan Medicine Required Qualifications* *A Master of Nursing degree is required. *Preference will be given to applicants who have both didactic and clinical teaching experience and content expertise in medical/surgical nursing in an in-patient setting. *A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). * Prior to start date must be in compliance with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). *Ability to promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately. Course Description N262 - Nursing Therapies I This is the first of three sequential clinical courses to apply knowledge gained in previous science and nursing courses to the care of individuals and families. Emphasis will be on clinical reasoning, decision making, critical thinking, and clinical judgement; communication; and use of evidenced based information within the clinical care setting. Students will learn to apply strategies within the clinical setting necessary to manage illness responses, evaluate client outcomes of care, and promote health. Concepts including pain, mobility, infection, thermoregulation, tissue integrity, and elimination will be emphasized. Concepts related to person, significant others, community, society, and culture are integrated within the course in order to direct the student to provide safe, optimal care. This position includes classroom and/or clinical teaching that will take place in the hospital and clinical lab setting and may include other on-site work hours NOTE: This appointment is for Winter term, 2027 only. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. The confidential review of applications will occur as they are received and will continue until the position(s) is/are filled. Our anticipated date of decision is 12/31/2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284188 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - N262 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 10/12/2026 - 12/11/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284193": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - N373 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - N373 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through the U-M Careers website. A cover letter of introduction explaining your specific interest in the position and outlining skills and experience that directly relate to this position and CV must be uploaded as one document. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources - N373 Behavioral Health - Lecturer I at [email protected] (electronic materials). The Office of Human Resources will follow up with all applicants to request the following documentation, if it is not received. *Cover letter *Current curriculum vita *Evidence of didactic and clinical teaching experience (teaching statement) *Three (3) letters of professional recommendation from master's prepared or higher individuals; on letterhead/signed and/or sent from a professional organization email *Copy of your highest nursing diploma *Copy of your nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position *Copy of certifications For further information regarding this position please contact [email protected] Who We Are The University of Michigan is a world -class research-intensive, enriched and highly collaborative institution. The UMSN offers a full range of accredited educational programs and is consistently ranked among the top nursing schools in the country. Please visit our website at http://www.nursing.umich.edu/ for more information. Job Summary The School of Nursing (UMSN), Undergraduate Program is recruiting nursing professional(s) with experience in the area of behavioral health nursing to teach a clinical PNE associated with N373 Behavioral Health. The effort is 25% (3 credits) and course day and time is Fridays 7:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. Michigan Medicine Required Qualifications* *A master of nursing degree is required. *A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). * Prior to start date must be in compliance with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). *Ability to promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately. Course Description N373 Behavioral Health This clinical nursing course focuses on the care of individuals with emotional, behavioral, and cognitive difficulties that affect functioning. Students will provide care for individuals in acute, outpatient, and community settings. Emphasis will be on building therapeutic communication skills that allow for safe, holistic, and evidence-based care for those with mental illness or in need of interventions that assist people in their daily functioning and promote mental health, considering the influence of developmental stage, families, communities, and culture. This position includes classroom and/or clinical teaching that will take place in the hospital, extended care facility and clinical lab setting and may include other on-site work hours. NOTE: This appointment is for Winter term, 2027 only. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. The confidential review of applications will occur as they are received and will continue until the position(s) is/are filled. Our anticipated date of decision is 12/31/2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284193 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - N373 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 10/12/2026 - 12/11/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284199": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - N480 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - N480 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through the U-M Careers website. A cover letter of introduction explaining your specific interest in the position and outlining skills and experience that directly relate to this position and CV must be uploaded as one document. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources - LEO Lecturer I- N480 Population Health at [email protected] (electronic materials). The Office of Human Resources will follow up with all applicants to request the following documentation, if it is not received. *Cover letter *Current curriculum vita *Evidence of didactic and clinical teaching experience (teaching statement) *Three (3) letters of professional recommendation from master's prepared or higher individuals; on letterhead/signed and/or sent from a professional organization email *Copy of your highest nursing diploma *Copy of your nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position *Copy of certifications For further information regarding this position please contact [email protected] . Who We Are The University of Michigan is a world -class research-intensive, enriched and highly collaborative institution. The UMSN offers a full range of accredited educational programs and is consistently ranked among the top nursing schools in the country. Please visit our website at http://www.nursing.umich.edu/ for more information. Job Summary The School of Nursing (UMSN), Undergraduate Program is recruiting nursing professional(s) with experience in the area of Population Health nursing to teach clinical sections of N480 Population Health (3 credits, 25% effort or 6 credits, 50% effort). Clinical sites are located in the greater Ann Arbor Area. Thursdays, 8:30 a.m. - 3:30 p.m. Fridays, 8:30 a.m. - 3:30 p.m. Responsibilities* The Lecturer appointed to this position is expected to attend all simulations and clinical conferences, perform clinical site visits, and provide feedback to students. The Lecturer is responsible for grading all assignments and simulations and bears primary responsibility for computing final grades in a timely fashion. This position includes classroom and/or clinical teaching that will take place in the hospital, clinic, or community setting and may include other on-site work hours. NOTE: This appointment is for Winter term, 2027 only. Required Qualifications* *A master of nursing degree is required. *A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). * Prior to start date must be in compliance with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). *Ability to promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to E mpower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; P ractice communication that is beneficial, kind and true; I nspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; C ultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately. Course Description N480 Population Health PNE During this clinical course, students will demonstrate leadership in organizations, systems, and populations as a team member and coordinator of health care. Students will practice within an ethical framework at the community and systems levels in their work with vulnerable and diverse populations. They will have the opportunity to reflect on their own values and beliefs. Principles emphasized in the course include advocacy, integrity, accountability in practice and leadership, inclusiveness, respect, and social justice using evidence-based strategies. Students will examine the influence of nursing on the health of populations with consideration for how socio-economic and physical environments affect care. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled at any time after the minimum posting period has ended. The confidential review of applications will occur as they are received and will continue until the position(s) is/are filled. Our anticipated date of decision is 12/31/2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284199 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - N480 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 10/12/2026 - 12/11/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284092": {
      "title": "CSE LEO Lecturer III",
      "description": "CSE LEO Lecturer III | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CSE LEO Lecturer III Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Apply here and direct questions to CSE HR at [email protected] . Include a cover letter, CV, teaching statement, research statement (optional), three references, and teaching evaluations (if any). Job Summary We are seeking a Lecturer IIl for a full-time, 100% effort appointment, to join our community of teaching-focused faculty, and who will support our teaching mission and further our goal of creating a diverse, inclusive, and representative Computer Science community within our department and in the field at large. Teaching faculty are active and engaged members of the department faculty. They are involved in departmental service and leadership, and many choose to be involved in scholarly research in their own technical area or in computer science education (though research is not required). A plethora of opportunities are available to teaching faculty for professional development, innovation, and research in teaching and learning in CS. Faculty are supported by outstanding teaching assistants, who play a critical role in instruction, student support, building an inclusive environment, and course development. Department-level course support staff assist with administrative and logistic needs. Responsibilities* Teaching Faculty are typically responsible for 2 courses per semester, sometimes 2 sections of the same course, with a mix of introductory, core, and specialized upper-level courses based on preference and expertise in Computer Science and Engineering. Faculty often work in collaborative teams when courses have multiple sections. Teaching faculty hold leadership roles in designing the curriculum and structuring our student experiences. As a Lecturer III, you will: Architect and deliver upper-level computer science curricula focusing on low-level engine design (C++, SDL, Box2D, Lua, data-oriented programming, custom scripting architectures) and 3D technical art pipelines (Blender, armatures, dynamic hair/cloth physics, and shader logic). Oversee capstone and Major Design Experience (MDE) programs in Virtual and Augmented Reality (Unity, Unreal Engine), guiding multidisciplinary teams through interaction-affordance design, project management, and applications targeting social impact. Design, deploy, and maintain custom multimedia autograding infrastructure capable of executing and evaluating real-time game engines across varied input/output streams (scripting, frame comparison, audio analysis). Mentor student-led commercial game production studios, manage multi-university exhibitions (1,000+ attendees). Direct research teams developing web-first, accessible engines for specific genres (RTS, RPG, Tower Defense) and facilitate cross-departmental collaboration bridging computer science, digital art, and music faculties. Required Qualifications* Master's degree in Computer Science and Engineering (MSE CSE or equivalent) Demonstrated excellence in university-level technical instruction (e.g., college-wide teaching awards) Minimum 8 years teaching experience in a computer science and engineering curriculum Prior consulting experience in interactive software development. Selection criteria based on resume with supplemental materials, in-person interview and presentation to be given to CSE faculty. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. CSE hopes to extend an offer for this position by November 20, 2026. Positions start as early as the Winter 2027 term. Initial appointments include a renewable 3-year contract. Applicants will be evaluated based on demonstrated evidence of excellent teaching, ability to positively impact and support students, and commitment to creating a diverse and inclusive CS community, as well as potential for growth and professional development in these areas. This appointment opportunity is subject to final approval by the Computer Science & Engineering Department and the College of Engineering. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284092 Working Title CSE LEO Lecturer III Job Title LEO Lecturer III Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE EECS - CSE Division Posting Begin/End Date 10/12/2026 - 10/22/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284174": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer III",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer III - LSA Linguistics | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer III - LSA Linguistics Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants are asked to submit: a letter of application a curriculum vitae a statement of teaching philosophy and experience teaching evaluations and other evidence of teaching excellence contact information for three recommenders. Recommenders for an initial shortlist of applicants will be contacted later to submit recommendations online Applications need be submitted through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195202 Review of dossiers is expected to begin on November 9 ; applications received after November 8 may not receive full consideration. Anticipated start date of August 25, 2027. More information about the Department of Linguistics can be found on our website: https://lsa.umich.edu/linguistics Job Summary The Department of Linguistics at the University of Michigan invites applications for a full-time Lecturer III position in Linguistics. Applicants are required to have a PhD in Linguistics or related field. Additional specialization in Speech and Hearing Science is desirable. This role supports departmental initiatives to enhance undergraduate education, specialized advising, and career-path development for students preparing for post-graduate study and professional careers in speech-language pathology, speech and hearing sciences, and adjacent fields. Because the University of Michigan does not offer a specialized undergraduate degree in speech and hearing sciences, approximately one-third of linguistics majors rely on our curriculum and advising to fulfill required prerequisites for graduate programs. The successful candidate will provide high-level undergraduate instruction, specialized student advising and mentoring, and administrative leadership related to the appointment. Evidence of excellence in teaching and service is expected. The initial appointment period will be four (4) years and the appointment may be renewed (based on programmatic need, funding, and performance). The appointment is subject to final approval by the college. Responsibilities* 1. Instructional & Curricular Duties Teach undergraduate linguistics courses that connect foundational knowledge to professional applications. Develop and instruct course offerings that are relevant for Speech and Hearing science pathways, possibly including LING 335: Speech Disorders and LING 336 / PSYCH 335: Reading: Language, the Brain, and Cognition , both of which satisfy graduate program application prerequisites. Ideally re-develop and manage LING 385: Experiential Practice in Linguistics , supporting internship-based training and experiential learning opportunities for vocational advancement of undergraduate students. Participate in course rotation for different undergraduate courses, including large-enrollment courses that support teaching assistants, based on applicants expertise. 2. Specialized Academic Advising & Student Mentorship Serve as the primary departmental advisor for Linguistics majors and minors interested in speech and hearing sciences, speech pathology, and related fields, while also contributing to general department advising. Provide intensive one-on-one mentorship to guide students through complex prerequisite course selection and application strategies for competitive graduate programs. Offer detailed feedback on graduate application materials and write comprehensive letters of recommendation for prospective graduate applicants. Serve as the faculty advisor to the student-led Pre-Speech and Hearing Club, sponsored by the Department of Linguistics. Collaborate with general advisors at the College level to build collaborative and consistent guidance for students preparing to pursue careers in speech pathology and audiology. 3. Administrative & Service Leadership Serve as an active member of the Department Undergraduate Committee, providing leadership and insights on curriculum, funding, programming, and pre-speech pathology educational tracks. Manage experiential learning initiatives and program coordination that maintain the departments strong placement record in top-tier graduate programs. Required Qualifications* Ph.D. in Linguistics or a related field. A proven track record of excellence in undergraduate instruction and pedagogical expertise relevant to linguistics. Evidence of successful administrative or professional service, possibly including student advising, curriculum development and mentorship. Desired Qualifications* Ability to connect theoretical course content with practical, experiential learning and vocational placement opportunities. Ability to advise and mentor student-led academic or professional organizations. Demonstrated skills and/or pedagogical experience to develop and teach courses relevant to speech and hearing sciences, speech-language pathology, or related disciplines. Evidence of skills to guide undergraduates through prerequisite planning and admissions processes for elite graduate programs in speech and hearing sciences. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Employment will require a successful criminal background check and may require an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates may be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form, and must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Women and members of minority groups are encouraged to apply. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Contact Information For any questions about this job or the application, please email [email protected] . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284174 Working Title LEO Lecturer III - LSA Linguistics Job Title LEO Lecturer III Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Linguistics Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 11/09/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284155": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 550: Interpersonal Skills: Leveling Up to Leadership | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 550: Interpersonal Skills: Leveling Up to Leadership Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement - enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The College of Engineering, Center for Entrepreneurship at the University of Michigan, invites applicants for a part-time LEO Lecturer I position to teach ENTR 550: Interpersonal Skills: Leveling Up to Leadership during the Winter 2027 term. This is a part-time (30% effort), in-person position. The Mission of the CFE is to unlock the full entrepreneurial potential at the University of Michigan by helping people to understand, experience, practice, and refine the skills needed to successfully translate their knowledge, ideas, leadership, creativity, and enthusiasm into lasting value in the world. We achieve this potential by providing broad and inclusive access to a wide variety of educational opportunities, immersive learning experiences, timely and powerful mentorship, and meaningful connections to the global industry leaders that are building the future. Additional information about the CFE can be found here . Responsibilities* The Center for Entrepreneurship's courses exemplify experiential learning, demonstrate an entrepreneurial mindset, and are open to all U-M students. We are seeking a dynamic, student-centered Instructor to lead our graduate-level professional development and leadership course during the Winter 2027 term. The ideal candidate brings a strong background in Emotional Intelligence (EQ) as a practical foundation to help students with personal growth, team dynamics, and modern leadership. This course guides students through self-awareness and personal management, interpersonal relationships and team effectiveness, and the integration of entrepreneurial strategy, risk management, and team formation across corporate, venture, and organizational environments. Course faculty will facilitate experiential learning, mentor students as they build their personal Plans of Action and Milestones (POA&M), and foster an inclusive environment grounded in psychological safety and self-care. Course faculty will: Refine and deliver an established curriculum spanning foundational EQ concepts, self-awareness models (including Quadrant Four analyses), motivation, psychological difference, resilience, executive communication, and synthesizing multi-instrument assessment data. Guide graduate students through the design and execution of their Personal Leadership Plans, offering feedback on multi-year arcs, self-assessments (such as StrengthsFinder), career alignment, and interview strategy. Facilitate collaborative group projects and team-building frameworks, equipping students with practical tools like the Plan of Action and Milestones to build high-performing, inclusive team dynamics. Develop student understanding and application of evolving leadership theories in order to evaluate EQ models in practice, power dynamics, ethics, and modern workplace challenges (such as hybrid work and AI adoption), and evaluate learning through structured rubrics and peer feedback systems. Oversee applied course-community project presentations and facilitate structured reflection sessions to help students articulate their personal leadership legacy and ongoing professional development. The course is scheduled for 14 weeks during the Winter 2027 term. Course faculty will be responsible for preparing interactive course materials, weekly lecturing, holding office hours each week, preparing and grading assignments, managing and grading class projects, and attending monthly CFE's Faculty Community Meetings. Required Qualifications* Master's or terminal degree (Ph.D., Ed.D.) in Industrial-Organizational Psychology, Educational Psychology, Organizational Leadership/Behavior, Executive Coaching, Higher Education, Business Administration (MBA), or a closely related field. Practical Leadership and Organizational Experience: 5+ years of leadership or executive experience, with demonstrated success managing teams, projects, and programs while adapting to changing workplace environments across small- and large-scale organizations. Demonstrated experience embedding applied leadership topics such as self-awareness assessment tools (e.g., CliftonStrengths/StrengthsFinder), foundational psychological principles of individual differences, Emotional Intelligence (EQ) frameworks, goal-setting methodologies, and group project management tools (such as Plan of Action & Milestones / POA&M). Demonstrated experience embedding positive psychology, burnout prevention, executive presence, AI, and hybrid work trends into higher education instruction and applied leadership coaching. Strong communication skills, including oral and visual presentations, with a proven ability to establish credibility and rapport with graduate and undergraduate students. Demonstrated ability to teach and use rubric-based assessments for undergraduate and graduate students in an interactive classroom setting, build a psychologically safe learning environment, and lead discussions on complex workplace and interpersonal dynamics. Desired Qualifications* Professional Certifications : Active certification in recognized EQ assessment frameworks (e.g., EQ-i 2.0, Six Seconds), Gallup Certified Strengths Coach, or International Coaching Federation (ICF) credentials. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information This position is posted for the Winter 2027 term at 30% effort (12 hours per week). Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284155 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 550: Interpersonal Skills: Leveling Up to Leadership Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE CFE Ctr for Entrepren Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 10/19/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284156": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 475.200: Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 475.200: Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement - enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The College of Engineering, Center for Entrepreneurship at the University of Michigan, invites applicants for a part-time LEO Lecturer I position to co-teach ENTR 475.200 - Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change during the Winter 2027 term. This is a part-time (15% effort), in-person position. The Mission of the CFE is to unlock the full entrepreneurial potential at the University of Michigan by helping people to understand, experience, practice, and refine the skills needed to successfully translate their knowledge, ideas, leadership, creativity, and enthusiasm into lasting value in the world. We achieve this potential by providing broad and inclusive access to a wide variety of educational opportunities, immersive learning experiences, timely and powerful mentorship, and meaningful connections to the global industry leaders that are building the future. Additional information about the CFE can be found here . Responsibilities* The Center for Entrepreneurship's courses exemplify experiential learning, demonstrate an entrepreneurial mindset, and are open to all U-M students. The person in this position will be appointed to co-teach ENTR 475.200: Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change (3 credits) during the Winter 2027 term. The position is anticipated to continue with co-teaching ENTR 485.200: Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change (3 credits) during the Fall 2027 term. Together, these courses comprise a two-course (6-credit) sequence. Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change provides students with unique opportunities to experience entrepreneurship by working with startup companies that are building the future, specifically in the field of sustainable, carbon-reducing technology. PJTL Climate Change pairs emerging transportation startups (post-Series A funding) with top-tier undergraduate and graduate students who want to tackle industry challenges through technical projects, providing mentorship and immersive interaction with high-growth ventures. The program aims to develop a talent pipeline for the growing climate change industry by connecting students with company sponsors and leveraging U-M's access to the Global CO2 Initiative. In Semester 1, student project teams collaborate with sourced startups advancing emerging technologies to develop core skills for future leadership roles (leading technical execution, applying project management, and aligning product development with business strategies). Instructors will teach how to: Align Technical Vision with Startup Strategy: Guide students to understand how strategic technical decisions directly drive a startup's mission, growth, and competitive edge. Apply Project Management Skills: Teach tools and techniques for planning, executing, and adapting technical projects within fast-paced startup environments. Facilitate Effective Communication: Mentor students on collaborating and communicating clearly with startup leadership and cross-functional teams to achieve target outcomes. Analyze Market & Technology Trends: Help students evaluate market shifts and emerging technologies to shape product development and commercialization pathways. Introduce Strategic Business Tools: Train students to apply foundational frameworks like the Business Model Canvas and Porter's Five Forces to analyze and pivot business strategies. Lead Technical Execution: Direct students in building actionable technical execution plans and product roadmaps from concept to delivery. Foster Entrepreneurial Thinking: Coach students through ambiguity, change, and risk to build decisiveness in early-stage environments. Develop Professional & Team Skills: Cultivate personal growth, accountability, and interdisciplinary collaboration to help students transition into high-performing professionals. The course is scheduled for 14 weeks during the Winter 2027 term. Course faculty (co-instructors) will be responsible for preparing interactive course materials, weekly lecturing, holding office hours weekly, preparing class exercises, preparing and grading assessments, managing and grading class projects, and attending the CFE's monthly Faculty Community Meetings. Required Qualifications* Master's degree in engineering or a relevant field (and/or equivalent combination of education and technical work experience) Demonstrated and successful practice of the entrepreneurial process in environmental disruptive technologies Demonstrated expertise in organizational management, program management, and the entrepreneurial process, from idea conception, technology development, and growing a team, to fundraising Successful practice of entrepreneurship and new venture creation, both at small and large scales, within the global climate change ecosystem Ability to learn conceptual frameworks quickly and apply them to practical student needs Strong communication skills, including oral and visual presentations Ability to establish credibility and rapport with students Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate and graduate students in a classroom environment Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Each co-instructor will hold a 15% effort (6 hours per week) appointment during the Winter 2027 term. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284156 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - ENTR 475.200: Perot Jain TechLab Climate Change Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE CFE Ctr for Entrepren Posting Begin/End Date 10/09/2026 - 10/19/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283787": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer III",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer III | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer III Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please submit the following as a single PDF document: cover letter, current CV, teaching statement, and contact information of three professional references. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis beginning October 15, 2026 until the position is filled. Priority will be given to applications received by December 31, 2026. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement-enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary Applications are invited for a Lecturer (full-time teaching faculty) in the Biomedical Engineering (BME) Department at the University of Michigan. The successful candidate will be part of a dedicated teaching team focused on experiential learning, engineering design, and professional development of biomedical engineers. The BME Department at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor (bme.umich.edu) is a joint department between the College of Engineering and the Medical School. It harnesses intellectual and physical resources from across campus to create a world-class learning environment. Applications from individuals in all areas of biomedical engineering and related disciplines will be considered. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in one or more of the following areas: Bioinstrumentation - knowledge and experience in software that supports biomedical instrumentation, such as LabView and supporting our current courses in this space. Biocomputation - Statistics, AI and machine learning in biomedical engineering. Biomedical Engineering Design - Knowledge in modeling, simulation, Capstone Design. Responsibilities* The main responsibilities of this position are: Teaching of undergraduate biomedical engineering courses, with an emphasis on engineering fundamentals, bioinstrumentation, biocomputation and/or design. Teaching load is expected to be up to 4 courses per academic year, and course assignments will depend on expertise, interest, and departmental needs. Contribution to curricular development and continuous improvement of the BME curriculum through departmental service and professional development. Required Qualifications* PhD in engineering or the physical sciences, or a BS/MS degree with significant and relevant industry experience. Desired Qualifications* Demonstrated commitment to teaching and education, as well as a strong motivation to improve the educational experience and engage students in experiential learning. Why Work at Michigan? In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, The University of Michigan offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions with immediate vesting Many choices for comprehensive health insurance Life insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Maternity and Parental Leave Additional Information The start date is flexible, with an ideal start date in August 2027.  This is a three-year appointment, with the possibility of renewal. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283787 Working Title LEO Lecturer III Job Title LEO Lecturer III Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Biomedical Engineering Posting Begin/End Date 10/08/2026 - 1/08/2027 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283986": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - BIO 116 - WN27 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - BIO 116 - WN27 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In order to be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through this website (the cover letter and CV must be uploaded as one document).  In addition, please send an application letter of introduction explaining your qualifications and teaching philosophy, a current CV, evidence of teaching excellence, and two letters of recommendation to: Ben Winger ( [email protected] ). Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The College of Literature, Science, and the Arts, and the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at the University of Michigan invite applications for a Lecturer I position at 33% effort for the Winter Term 2027.  This appointment is scheduled to begin on January 13, 2027 and end on May 7, 2027. Responsibilities* Duties of these Lecturer I positions are expected to include teaching, developing course materials, evaluating and grading students, and holding regularly scheduled office hours. A typical full-time (100% effort) load for a Lecturer I in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts is three courses per semester.  Full-time and part-time positions are available. Course Title(s): EEB116: Biology of Sex Course Objectives: The main goal of this course is to help students understand the nature of science and how to evaluate the quality of science. The focus of the course is on the biology of sex from an evolutionary perspective. By surveying a wide scope of diverse organisms and some amazing sexual adaptations, students will come to better appreciate how the sex lives of other organisms can help us understand ourselves, including the basis for mate choice and mate preference, the role of developmental biology, the endocrine system, and genetics and neurobiology, as well as the evolutionary costs and benefits of sex. The course also examines facts and fallacies about sex, including aspects of human sexual biology. By the end of the course, students will not only have a greater appreciation of the diversity and the role of sex in much of life on earth, but they will also improve their scientific literacy so that they can navigate and understand the role of science for us as individuals and as a society. Required Qualifications* The candidate must hold a Ph.D. in ecology, evolutionary biology or organismal biology, or a related discipline, with a focus on life histories and reproductive physiology.  The candidate must have demonstrated excellence in college-level teaching in the United States. Desired Qualifications* The applicant should also be familiar with phylogenetic concepts, including the application of evolutionary principles in a comparative context.  Lastly, many examples focus on fish given their different modes of reproduction so expertise in fish biology is desirable. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position can be emailed to: [email protected] . Application Deadline Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.  Deadline for applications is October 16, 2026.  The anticipated date by which an offer will be made is no later than November 2, 2026.  The appointment opportunity described in this posting is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Selection Process The selection criteria used for this search is based on how the applicant's education and experience meets the required minimum qualifications for the position.  Excellence in teaching and instruction will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283986 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - BIO 116 - WN27 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Ecology & Evolutionary Bio Posting Begin/End Date 10/06/2026 - 10/16/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283931": {
      "title": "Sociology LEO Lecturer I and/or Adjunct Lecturer",
      "description": "Sociology LEO Lecturer I and/or Adjunct Lecturer | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Sociology LEO Lecturer I and/or Adjunct Lecturer Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your CV. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relates to this position and what courses you might be qualified to teach. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of Sociology at the University of Michigan is one of the premier sociology departments in the world. The goals of the undergraduate program are to instruct students about the value of recognizing and understanding the influence of social environments on individual outcomes and behaviors, to help them acquire different ways of thinking about the social world, and to provide theoretical and empirical frameworks for understanding social institutions and processes of social change. A major hallmark of the department's curriculum is the opportunities we provide our students to connect their classroom work to practical experience. The Sociology Department is hiring two full time equivalent (FTE) LEO Lecturer I and/or Adjunct Lecturer positions for Winter term (Jan-May 2027). A full time appointment requires teaching 3 courses. Part-time appointments may be offered (1 course = 33.33% appointment, 2 courses = 66.66% appointment). Candidates will be selected based on specific expertise required for the courses. These positions may be renewable, contingent on performance and funding. Criteria for renewal will include 1) continued curricular need, 2) quality of classroom teaching, 3) having a PhD in Sociology, and 4) participation in curriculum development, with classroom teaching receiving the greatest weight. Responsibilities* The LEO Lecturers will teach up to three courses each in Winter 2027. Courses needing coverage may include: SOC 105 - First Year Seminar in Sociology* SOC 295 - Topics in Sociology* (up to two courses) SOC 303 - Race and Ethnic Relations SOC 330 - Population Problems: Social Inequality and Changing Families SOC 428 - Contemporary China SOC 495 - Topics in Sociology* (up to two courses) *Examples of topics could include race, sexuality, environment, sociology of digital life, social psychology, social problems, or other topics based on candidate expertise. In addition to teaching responsibilities, the instructor will oversee any course Graduate Student Instructor(s), if applicable; hold office hours for students; do course planning and grading; and provide appropriate syllabus and course materials electronically. Required Qualifications* PhD degree in sociology or another social science. Experience serving as an Instructor of Record for sociology or other social science courses with excellent teaching evaluations. Desired Qualifications* Experience supervising GSIs / teaching assistants. Experience teaching large scale intro courses (large lecture class of ~300 students). The successful candidate should have cutting edge and contemporary knowledge and experience in teaching in the field of Sociology. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline For Winter 2027, applications are due Monday, October 19, 2026. Selection Process Review of candidates will continue until the position is filled. Only complete application packets will be reviewed. The hiring decision will be announced within fourteen (14) days after the opening has been filled. This appointment is subject to final approval. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283931 Working Title Sociology LEO Lecturer I and/or Adjunct Lecturer Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Sociology Posting Begin/End Date 10/05/2026 - 10/22/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283861": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I - SI 540 Privacy in Information Technology | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I - SI 540 Privacy in Information Technology Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please upload your Curriculum Vitae or Resume and a cover letter detailing your experience and interest in the position as one document. Who We Are The School of Information is a progressive, interdisciplinary school with five academic programs. UMSI delivers innovative, elegant, and ethical solutions connecting people, information, and technology. The school was one of the first iSchools in the nation and our interdisciplinary faculty spans such areas as data science, computational social science, human-computer interaction, social media, library and archival science, and digital curation. Our school mission is to create and share knowledge so that people will use information ? with technology ? to build a better world. Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Information (UMSI) invites applications for a part-time LEO Lecturer I position. The successful candidate will join UMSI as a primary instructor for SI 540 Privacy in Information Technology in the Winter 2027 semester. This is a 3-credit graduate-level course. This will be a part-time position starting 1/1/2027 and ending 4/30/2027 with 25% effort. Applicants must be available for in-person instruction on the University of Michigan Ann Arbor Campus. Responsibilities* As a Lecturer I at UMSI, you will be the primary instructor for SI 540 Privacy in Information Technology. The successful candidate will develop course syllabi and materials, deliver course content to students, and provide student assessments including grading and feedback. You will be expected to coordinate and work collaboratively with the teaching team for this course, and respond to school and teaching team communications, ensuring daily/evening/weekend coverage for students will be expected. You will also work collaboratively with UMSI administration and staff to ensure that students receive an excellent education and are aware of support services in the larger UM and UMSI communities. Required Qualifications* Master's degree or higher in a related field, such as Information or Computer Science Proven expertise in privacy research and practice and other subjects related to the course Experience teaching in a higher education context Desired Qualifications* Demonstrated excellence in teaching Deep interest in improving pedagogy and collaboration to perform instructional best practices Current participation in professional associations Significant experience in privacy research, as seen in professional publications or similar outputs. Understanding of the social and policy dimensions of the privacy landscape. Modes of Work Applicants must be available for in-person instruction on the University of Michigan Ann Arbor Campus. Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours, and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third-party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten (10) calendar days. The posting will close on October 16. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. Review of applications will commence immediately. Offers will continue to be made until open teaching needs are met. Final hiring approval is made by the Associate Dean for Teaching and the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs and is expected to be made by December 1, 2026. The selection criteria include meeting the required qualifications listed above. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283861 Working Title LEO Lecturer I - SI 540 Privacy in Information Technology Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Information Department School of Information Posting Begin/End Date 10/01/2026 - 10/16/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
