    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284439": {
      "title": "CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR-CLINICAL PROFESSOR",
      "description": "CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR-CLINICAL PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR-CLINICAL PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply For further inquiries, please contact: Peter Mourani, M.D. Director, Division of Pediatric Critical Care Medicine Department of Pediatrics University of Michigan Health System 1500 East Medical Center Drive F6790 UH South/5243, Ann Arbor, MI  48109 [email protected] Job Summary The University of Michigan's Division of Pediatric Critical Care Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics and C.S. Mott Children's Hospital is seeking an outstanding pediatric critical care physician with clinical expertise in caring for complex, critically ill children to join our team. The position includes a faculty appointment at the University of Michigan Medical School. Applicants must have excellent clinical skills, board certification or eligibility in pediatric critical care medicine, and meet criteria for medical licensure in Michigan. This position is posted as Clinical Instructor/Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor/Clinical Professor. Rank of selected candidate is dependent upon qualifications. Independent external funding, lead or senior authorship of peer-reviewed journal articles, and national reputation as demonstrated by service roles will be prioritized for this role. Significant leadership experience and/or nationally recognized scholarship in pediatric critical care medicine are highly desired. Special consideration will be given to candidates with research and academic interest in quality improvement, basic, translational clinical trial, data or implementation science. Additional desirable qualities include interest and experience in leadership roles. Opportunities exist for the development of scholarly activities, leadership development, and faculty growth. Duties include coverage of the inpatient PICU service at the C.S. Mott Children's Hospital. Responsibilities include in-house night coverage, supervision and teaching of medical students, residents, and fellows, scholarly activity, and administrative duties. Required Qualifications* The candidate will have an MD or DO degree, be Board Eligible/Board Certified in Pediatrics and in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine and hold or have the ability to obtain the appropriate medical licenses in the State of Michigan. Evidence of excellence in clinical care and clinical and/or didactic teaching is expected. Why Join Michigan Medicine? Michigan Medicine is one of the largest health care complexes in the world and has been the site of many groundbreaking medical and technological advancements since the opening of the U-M Medical School in 1850. Michigan Medicine is comprised of over 30,000 employees and our vision is to attract, inspire, and develop outstanding people in medicine, sciences, and healthcare to become one of the world's most distinguished academic health systems.  In some way, great or small, every person here helps to advance this world-class institution. Work at Michigan Medicine and become a victor for the greater good. What Benefits can you Look Forward to? Excellent medical, dental and vision coverage effective on your very first day 2:1 Match on retirement savings Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as clinical instructor/clinical assistant professor/clinical associate professor/clinical professor.  Rank of selected candidate is dependent upon qualifications. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284439 Working Title CLINICAL INSTRUCTOR-CLINICAL PROFESSOR Job Title CLINICAL PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Pediatrics-Intensive Care Posting Begin/End Date 10/15/2026 - 10/22/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282157": {
      "title": "CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/CLINICAL ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR",
      "description": "CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/CLINICAL ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/CLINICAL ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The confidential review of applications will occur September 2026 through December 2026 until the position is filled. A cover letter and current curriculum vitae must be uploaded online via the U-M Careers website http://careers.umich.edu/ for this Job ID 282157. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources at [email protected] Teaching statement which should describe teaching philosophy, experience in teaching across programs and settings (i.e., didactic and clinical) Practice statement Scholarship (research) statement that includes a statement of impact of his/her/their work in the first paragraph Service statement If selected to move forward in the process, applicants will be asked to submit additional materials as noted in the School of Nursing Faculty Handbook including professional letters of recommendation, transcripts of your highest degrees, and compliance requirements. For further information regarding these positions please contact [email protected] . For further information regarding these positions, please contact Patricia Hershberger, PhD, APRN, FNP-BC, FAAN at : [email protected] . Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Nursing (UMSN) is a vibrant community of scholars, educators, clinicians, and innovators embedded within one of the world's leading public research universities. Consistently ranked among the nation's top nursing schools, we are committed to advancing health and health equity through research, education, leadership, and service. Our faculty generate knowledge that improves lives, informs policy, strengthens healthcare delivery, and addresses pressing local and global health challenges. The UMSN invites applications for a full-time, 12-month clinical track faculty position ideally beginning in January 2027. We seek accomplished board-certified Family Nurse Practitioners, clinical educators, and clinical scholars who demonstrate excellence in advanced practice nursing, teaching, mentorship, scholarship, and service. We welcome both emerging and established clinical leaders whose expertise contributes to the preparation of future advanced practice nurses and the advancement of evidence-based healthcare. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to innovative teaching, interprofessional collaboration, and clinical excellence. Applicants should have experience teaching in undergraduate and/or graduate nursing programs and be actively engaged in clinical practice. Preference will be given to Family Nurse Practitioners with strong clinical expertise in primary care. Candidates must demonstrate commitment to student mentorship, innovative clinical education, and the integration of teaching, practice, scholarship and service. Interest in global health practice is desirable. Courses are delivered both online and in person, and this is an on-site position. The search will be conducted during the fall term of the 2026-2027 academic year, with application reviews beginning in September 2026. Applicants will be considered for the ranks listed above depending on applicant experience and scholarship productivity. Required Qualifications* NECESSARY QUALIFICATIONS Doctorate in Nursing. A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). A specialty certification in the State of Michigan is required for this position. You must hold a Family Nurse Practitioner Board Certification (FNP-C or FNP-BC) from the American Association of Nurse Practitioners or the American Nurses Credentialing Center. Demonstrated excellent communication, networking and relationship building skills. Current clinical experience in a practice relevant role. Demonstrated ability to participate in the development and implementation of innovative curricular activities. Ability to collaborate with faculty and clinical partners to develop and sustain high-quality educational programs. Evidence of increasing service to the profession and academic community. Evidence of clinically relevant scholarship that advances advanced practice nursing and healthcare outcomes. Scholarship may include peer-reviewed publications, evidence-based practice initiatives, quality improvement projects, program evaluation, guideline development, dissemination activities, and grant-related work. Prior to the start date must be in compliance with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies, and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). Ability to promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to Empower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; Practice communication that is beneficial, kind and true; Inspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; Cultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately. Additional Qualifications for the Clinical Associate Professor Rank Demonstrated excellence and sustained impact in clinical and/or didactic teaching. Evidence of leadership in advanced practice education, curriculum development, clinical program innovation, or professional organizations. Sustained record of clinically relevant scholarship with regional or national recognition. Significant service contributions to the profession, academic community, healthcare organizations, or clinical practice initiatives. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Clinical Assistant Professor/Clinical Associate Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the selected candidate's qualifications. Application Deadline Complete application packets must be submitted by October 31, 2026. The Faculty Search Committee will review applications beginning in the Fall 2026. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282157 Working Title CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR/CLINICAL ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR Job Title CLINICAL ASSOC PROF Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 8/23/2026 - 10/31/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282158": {
      "title": "CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR",
      "description": "CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The confidential review of applicants will occur as they are received and will continue until the position(s) are filled. A cover letter and current curriculum vitae must be uploaded online via the U-M Careers website http://careers.umich.edu/ for this Job ID 282158. In addition to applying online, applicants are requested to send the following to the Office of Human Resources at [email protected] Teaching statement which should describe teaching philosophy, experience in teaching across programs and settings (i.e., didactic and clinical) Practice statement Scholarship (research) statement that includes a statement of impact of his/her work in the first paragraph and a description of three peer-reviewed publications, with an emphasis on the most recent and representative Service statement If selected to move forward in the process, applicants will be asked to submit additional materials as noted in the School of Nursing Faculty Handbook including professional letters of recommendation, transcripts of your highest degrees, and compliance requirements. For further information regarding these positions please contact [email protected] . For further information regarding these positions, please contact Dana Tschannen, PhD, RN at: [email protected] . Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Nursing (UMSN) is a vibrant community of scholars, educators, clinicians, and innovators embedded within one of the world's leading public research universities. Consistently ranked among the nation's top nursing schools, we are committed to advancing health and health equity through research, education, leadership, and service. Our faculty generate knowledge that improves lives, informs policy, strengthens healthcare delivery, and addresses pressing local and global health challenges. The UMSN invites applications for a full-time, 12-month clinical track faculty position beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. We seek accomplished clinical scholars with a strong background in nursing practice, demonstrated expertise in a practice role, and a proven record of teaching and scholarship. Faculty will be responsible for didactic and/or clinical instruction in the following undergraduate specialty areas: Behavioral Health Nursing Medical-Surgical Nursing Obstetrics Nursing Pediatric Nursing All undergraduate courses are delivered in person. Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in clinical education, healthcare delivery, and student mentorship. Preference will be given to applicants with active clinical engagement and a passion for integrating practice, teaching, and scholarship. The University of Michigan is a world-class research institution. The School of Nursing offers a range of accredited educational programs and is consistently ranked among the top nursing schools in the country. Please visit http://www.nursing.umich.edu/ for more information about the School. Required Qualifications* NECESSARY QUALIFICATIONS Doctorate in Nursing. A nursing license in the State of Michigan is required for this position (current licensure or willingness to obtain licensure). Demonstrated excellent communication, networking and relationship building skills. Demonstrated ability to participate in the development and implementation of innovative curricular activities. Demonstrated clinical teaching ability and practice excellence in a defined relevant area of clinical expertise. Current clinical experience in a practice relevant role. Evidence of increasing service to the profession and academic community. Collaborates with faculty regarding visionary educational programs. A record of scholarly attainment including publications, evidence-based practice guidelines and other forms of scholarship. Prior to the start date must be compliant with all health requirements including standard precautions such as Basic Life Support (BLS) certification, orientations, licenses/certifications, policies, and procedures both internal and external to the University for your assigned classroom(s) and/or clinical site(s). 'The employee will promote the School of Nursing's Declaration of Values to Empower each other to interrupt or disrupt disrespect; Practice communication that is beneficial, kind, and true; Inspire and be inspired by our members' worth, significance and integrity; Cultivate respect for ourselves and others routinely, publicly and privately.' Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Application Deadline Complete application packets must be submitted by October 31, 2026. The Faculty Search Committee will review applications beginning Fall term 2026. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282158 Working Title CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR Job Title CLINICAL ASST PROF Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nursing Department School of Nursing Posting Begin/End Date 8/23/2026 - 10/31/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Clinical Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284440": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW",
      "description": "RESEARCH FELLOW | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ RESEARCH FELLOW Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now Job Summary POSTDOCTORAL FELLOWSHIP IN BRAIN CANCER RESEARCH We are looking for a talented and experienced individual for a postdoctoral fellowship in a translational brain tumor research laboratory in the Department of Neurosurgery at the University of Michigan Medical School. Using a combination of in vitro and in vivo modeling, analyses of clinical brain tumors samples and multi-omics (including single cell and spatial transcriptomics) our lab focuses on exploring the intrinsic and extrinsic tumor microenvironmental interactions mediating treatment resistance in gliomas. The projects in the laboratory will also have a strong translational component including involvement in an ongoing clinical trial. The ideal candidate will possess extensive experience in molecular and cellular biology and will be capable of leading research projects that unravel aberrant cellular signaling mechanisms in cancer. Demonstrating the ability to work independently in the laboratory is paramount. Furthermore, as a postdoctoral fellow, you will be able to pursue innovative and exciting projects while simultaneously fostering your own independent research career. Responsibilities* Development of targeted gene modification in cell lines, co-cultures, and mouse models to study intrinsic and extrinsic mechanisms mediating tumor progression. Develop model systems and employ novel methodologies to elucidate the underlying mechanisms of treatment resistance in brain cancer Development of tools for biomarker assessment in translational brain cancer research Exhibit meticulous scientific rigor and attention to detail when designing and testing hypotheses, as well as analyzing data. Required Qualifications* PhD in a recognized field of study related to the position with laboratory experience in cancer biology. Experience in the use of genetically engineered cell lines and mouse models. Have strong oral and written communication skills Have a positive, independent, and collaborative attitude suitable for working in a research laboratory Strong scientific curiosity and record of rigorous evidence based publications. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Candidate will have an opportunity to be supported and co-mentored by a team of collaborative investigators. References: Al-Holou WN... Rehemtulla A . Subclonal evolution and expansion of spatially distinct THY1-positive cells is associated with recurrence in glioblastoma. Neoplasia. 2023 Feb;36:100872. doi: 10.1016/j.neo.2022.100872. Epub 2023 Jan 6. PMID: 36621024. Scott AJ, Mittal A, Meghdadi B, Palavalasa S, ... Lyssiotis CA*, Al-Holou WN* , Nagrath D*, Wahl DR*. Rewiring of cortical glucose metabolism fuels human brain cancer growth. Nature. 2025 Sep 3. doi: 10.1038/s41586-025-09460-7. Epub ahead of print. PMID:40903569.2023. doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.10.24.23297489 . Nature. (*Co-Corresponding Authors ) Liu Y, ..., Rehemtulla A . Activation of the Unfolded Protein Response via Inhibition of Protein Disulfide Isomerase Decreases the Capacity for DNA Repair to Sensitize Glioblastoma to Radiotherapy. Cancer Res. 2019 Jun 1;79(11):2923-2932. doi: 10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-18-2540. Epub 2019 Apr 17. PMID: 30996048; Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284440 Working Title RESEARCH FELLOW Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Neurosurgery Posting Begin/End Date 10/15/2026 - 11/05/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284446": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW",
      "description": "RESEARCH FELLOW | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ RESEARCH FELLOW Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Job Summary A Postdoctoral Research Fellow position is available in the McLaughlin lab at the University of Michigan in the Department of Cell & Developmental Biology (CDB). We use a combination of multi-omics (single-cell transcriptomics and proteomics), cell biology, and genetics in the Drosophila melanogaster (fruit fly) to investigate how neural circuits are built, refined, and maintained. Our group values community, scientific rigor, and intellectual growth. We are seeking a postdoctoral research fellow to independently design and execute experiments, generate and analyze data related to the lab?s ongoing research projects, and bring new perspectives and expertise to the lab. We expect the postdoctoral fellow to be organized, collaborative, and provide mentorship and guidance to members of the group. Working under the guidance of Dr. Colleen McLaughlin (Principal Investigator), the fellow will also have opportunities to develop independent research directions, present at conferences, prepare work for publication, and apply to fellowships in support of their long-term career goals. Responsibilities* Research projects (80%) Independently: Design, execute, and troubleshoot experiments Generate, analyze, and interpret data Maintain accurate and detailed records of experimental methods and results Prepare figures, manuscripts, and presentations Present experimental results internally and at scientific meetings Contribute to the development of new research directions Apply for fellowships Lab citizenship and mentoring (20%) Contribute to a collaborative, respectful, and inclusive lab environment Train new lab members in experimental procedures Mentor junior students including graduate students and undergraduates Required Qualifications* PhD in neuroscience, cell biology, developmental biology, or related field Excellent written and verbal communication skills, as well as interpersonal skills contributing to a strong team environment Expertise in cell and molecular biology Background working with genetic model organisms Desired Qualifications* Background knowledge and expertise in developmental biology research Previous experience working with Drosophila melanogaster or in glial cell development Experience with single-cell transcriptomic data analysis or development new multi-omic tools Why Join Michigan Medicine? Michigan Medicine is one of the largest health care complexes in the world and has been the site of many groundbreaking medical and technological advancements since the opening of the U-M Medical School in 1850. Michigan Medicine is comprised of over 30,000 employees and our vision is to attract, inspire, and develop outstanding people in medicine, sciences, and healthcare to become one of the world?s most distinguished academic health systems.  In some way, great or small, every person here helps to advance this world-class institution. Work at Michigan Medicine and become a victor for the greater good. What Benefits can you Look Forward to? Excellent medical, dental and vision coverage effective on your very first day Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284446 Working Title RESEARCH FELLOW Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Cell & Developmntl Biology Posting Begin/End Date 10/15/2026 - 11/14/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284361": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Research Fellow Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply For consideration of this position, applicants must submit the following materials as one document: 1) a cover letter describing their interest in working in computational approaches to understanding the epidemiology of Alzheimer's disease and related dementias, and 2) a current CV. Job Summary The Department of Epidemiology is seeking a postdoctoral research fellow-with a record of accomplishment in computational epidemiology and modeling applied to aging epidemiology or adjacent field-to contribute to studies seeking to answer epidemiological questions about cognitive transitions across the older adult life course and interventions targeting modifiable dementia risk factors. Primary responsibilities include the design and conduct of data analysis of longitudinal cohorts and simulation models to project trends and examine potential interventions, as well as the preparation of scientific manuscripts as lead and co-author. There may be opportunities available for collaboration on a range of funded studies in the area of aging research, as well as for participation in the preparation and submission of extramural research grants. This position, funded by a National Institutes of Aging grant, will be under the supervision of Dr. Andrew Brouwer, a computational epidemiologist whose research leverages mathematical and statistical modeling for inference in epidemiology. The University of Michigan has a vibrant and dynamic intellectual community for aging research, including the Center for Lifecourse Epidemiology & Aging Research, the Michigan Alzheimer's Disease Research Center, the Center to Accelerate Population Research in Alzheimer's, the Claude D. Pepper Older Americans Independence Center, and the Institute of Gerontology, among others. Responsibilities* Data management: Manages the organization, documentation, merging, cleaning, and storage of complex population datasets. Creates final analytic datasets Data analysis and modeling: Designs and executes complex data analyses and simulation models. Independently interprets results, including creation of data tables, visualizations, and reports for presentation Manuscript preparation: Conceptualizes and contributes to the writing of scientific manuscripts as lead author and co-author Collaboration and mentorship: Provide collaborator feedback and supplementary mentorship to master's- and PhD-level students engaged in related research projects Grant preparation: Assists in acquiring extramural research funding, including the technical portions of grant proposals Required Qualifications* Doctorate in epidemiology or other public health science. Applicants with PhD in computational or quantitative fields (statistics, applied mathematics, etc.) are encouraged to apply if they meet the other criteria. Applicants must have a conferred doctorate degree prior to the start of the fellowship A record of accomplishment in computational epidemiology and modeling applied to aging epidemiology or adjacent field, as evidenced by publications, presentations, and training Prior training or research in simulation modeling Proficiency in programming in R (preferred) or Python. Excellent skills in data management and analysis, data visualization, and scientific communication Desired Qualifications* The following qualifications are not required or expected but should be highlighted in the application materials if relevant. Prior experience in the analysis of longitudinal cohorts Prior experience with multistate transition models and microsimulation models, or similar approaches Prior experience with the National Alzheimer's Coordinating Center Uniform Data Set, the Health and Retirement Study, or the Study of Women's Health Across the Nation Prior experience in women's health, cardiovascular disease, or biomarkers of ADRD (or biomarkers of aging more broadly) Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Work Locations This position allows for a hybrid work schedule. The selected candidate will be expected to work on-site 2 days per week. Additional Information This is a term-limited position for one-year from the start of the appointment, with possibility of extension contingent upon funding and performance. We are seeking an experienced and dynamic staff leader/member with a commitment to contributing to a welcoming environment for all members of our community. Anticipated start date: Between November 1, 2026 and January 1, 2027. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284361 Working Title Research Fellow Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Pub Health Department Epidemiology Department Posting Begin/End Date 10/15/2026 - 10/29/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284874": {
      "title": "Associate of Full Professor in Societal Adaption and Resilence to Climate Extremes",
      "description": "Associate of Full Professor in Societal Adaption and Resilence to Climate Extremes | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Associate of Full Professor in Societal Adaption and Resilence to Climate Extremes Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply The position will be filled at the associate or full professor (tenured) level, beginning Fall 2027. Applications should include (1) a cover letter; (2) CV; (3) a concise (up to 2-page) personal statement describing your vision and plans for research and teaching; and (4) a list of three names of academic references with contact information. Please do not include letters of recommendation with your application; we will contact references as needed. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. To apply, submit application materials in Interfolio, via this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195496 . Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Mission Statement SEAS's mission is to contribute to the protection of the Earth's resources and the achievement of a sustainable and just society. The School contributes new scientific knowledge, visionary leadership, and trained professionals toward that end. The faculty of the School is diverse, with natural scientists, social scientists, engineers, and designers working collaboratively in an integrative setting. A professional school set within a major research university, SEAS provides a model of interdisciplinary and applied research and a focal point of research, teaching and societal engagement on sustainability. The School focuses on key cross-cutting sustainability themes, including climate and energy; water; food, land use, and agriculture; conservation and restoration; and cities, the built environment, and mobility. There are many opportunities for research collaborations across the University of Michigan, including with academic units like Earth and Environmental Sciences, Civil Engineering, Climate and Space Sciences and Engineering, and the School for Public Health, as well as with institutes and research centers like the Erb Institute for Global Sustainable Enterprise, the Graham Environmental Sustainability Institute, the Center for Land Surface Hazards (CLaSH), the William Davidson Institute, and the SEAS-led NOAA GLISA Partnership. The School offers a Ph.D. as well as both research and professional M.S. degrees in seven specializations: ecosystem science and management; geospatial data science; sustainable systems; environmental policy and planning; behavior, education and communication; environmental justice; and sustainable development. The School also offers a Master's degree in Landscape Architecture (M.L.A.). The student body includes over 500 M.S./M.L.A. and 60 doctoral students. SEAS also co-manages an undergraduate Program in the Environment and participates in dual master degree programs with the Schools of Architecture and Urban Planning, Business, Engineering, Law, Public Health, and Public Policy. Additional information about the School can be found at www.seas.umich.edu . At SEAS we are committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable environment that respects diverse experiences, promotes generous listening and communications, and discourages and restoratively responds to acts of discrimination, harassment, or injustice. To learn more about SEAS and our values, please visit our website at https://seas.umich.edu/about/seas-values . Job Summary The School for Environment and Sustainability (SEAS) at the University of Michigan is seeking applicants for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track faculty member to advance social science research and engagement on societal adaptation and resilience to hazards and extremes driven by climate change. We invite applications from candidates working in domestic and/or global context. The position will be filled at the Associate or Full Professor level as part of the University of Michigan Look to Michigan Faculty Expansion Program involving faculty hires in SEAS , Earth and Environmental Science , and Climate and Space Sciences and Engineering . Specific topics of interest include governance, institutions, and management of societal risks of extreme events; innovative risk and vulnerability assessment; adaptation planning; monitoring and evaluation of mitigation and adaptation strategies; engaged research partnerships; knowledge coproduction or other forms of knowledge-to-action research; policy and program innovation; and attention to equity. We are looking for scholars with research relevant to Environmental Policy and Planning ; Behavior, Education and Communication ; Environmental Justice and/or Sustainability and Development specializations at SEAS. Candidates must hold a PhD and be an internationally recognized scholar with training in human-environment geography, political science, public policy, applied economics, planning, or a related field. Candidates must have a strong record of transdisciplinary research; scholarly publication; teaching in a multidisciplinary setting; engaged research strategies to generate actionable knowledge, such as action research and co-production for societal impact; and national and international recognition. We are particularly interested in candidates who will help solve real-world sustainability challenges through internal and external partnerships, including by elevating cross-campus interdisciplinary collaborations to attract the funding and partnerships needed for transformative impact. The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop a widely recognized research program that attracts external funding and contributes to the interdisciplinary mission of the School; Support SEAS's teaching mission at both graduate and undergraduate levels, including mentoring and supervising doctoral and master's students; Contribute to the stewardship of SEAS, the University, relevant professions and society through service, collaboration and engagement. Why Work at Michigan? In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, The University of Michigan offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: A retirement plan that provides two-for-one matching contributions with immediate vesting Many choices for comprehensive health insurance Life insurance Long-term disability coverage Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284874 Working Title Associate of Full Professor in Societal Adaption and Resilence to Climate Extremes Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nat Res Envir Department Sch for Environ and Sustain Posting Begin/End Date 10/26/2026 - 6/30/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Research Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284787": {
      "title": "Department Chair and Tenure Track Associate/Full Professor Position in Biologic and Material Sciences & Prosthodontics",
      "description": "Department Chair and Tenure Track Associate/Full Professor Position in Biologic and Material Sciences & Prosthodontics | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Department Chair and Tenure Track Associate/Full Professor Position in Biologic and Material Sciences & Prosthodontics Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply \"ATTENTION FACULTY APPLICANTS: DO NOT USE THE \"APPLY NOW\" BUTTON AT THE TOP OF THIS PAGE. If you are interested in applying for this position within the School of Dentistry, PLEASE APPLY HERE \" Interested candidates should submit the following via the secure Interfolio link : Curriculum Vitae Statement of Interests and Goals related to this position (up to 3 pages) Contact information for three references (references will be contacted at the finalist stage) Questions regarding the position and responsibilities should be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Vesa Kaartinen ( [email protected] ). For questions regarding the application or search process, please contact the Administrative Specialist, Melanie LaPointe ( [email protected] ). Applications will be accepted and evaluated on an ongoing basis until the position is filled, so we encourage applicants to please apply as soon as possible. The search committee plans to begin reviewing applications in late November 2026. Employment is contingent upon the successful completion of a criminal background check and may involve an institutional reference check regarding misconduct. Applicants may also be required to complete a self-disclosure form and an authorization to release information. Mission Statement MDentistry: Advancing health through education, service, research and discovery. MDentistry Core Values: Compassion, Leadership, Excellence, Responsibility, Trust, Creativity, and Inclusion. The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future.  The University of Michigan is committed to foster learning, creativity and productivity, and to support the vigorous exchange of ideas and information, not only in the classroom but in the workplace by: Creating a work environment in which people treat each other with respect and dignity, regardless of roles, responsibilities or differences. Providing support, direction and resources enabling us to accomplish the responsibilities of our jobs and to reach the goals that are set for professional and personal growth. Job Summary The University of Michigan School of Dentistry in Ann Arbor invites qualified applications for the chair position in the Department of Biologic and Materials Sciences & Prosthodontics . Salary and rank will be commensurate with experience and credentials. This is an outstanding opportunity for an Associate Professor or Professor on the tenure track to lead a vibrant, growing, and well-established department. The department faculty spans fundamental science, translational research, and prosthodontics. The chair is responsible for leading the department faculty members in support of the DDS program (didactic, preclinical, and clinical); advancing an outstanding graduate program in Prosthodontics; participating in PhD programs; fostering access to cutting-edge resources to enable high-quality, rigorous scholarship for faculty and trainees; and implementing strategic plans to strengthen academic and clinical missions through recruitment and community building. This large department is inclusive, creative, and dynamic, featuring strong research and clinical faculty. Current research foci include bone biology, computational biology, craniofacial development and disease, materials science, microbiology and immunology, neurobiology, organ development, pain research, stem cell biology, salivary gland biology, tissue engineering, and clinical research. There is a long history of collaborative, multidisciplinary research, and many faculty members hold joint appointments within the university (e.g., Medical School and College of Engineering). In the prosthodontic arena, major research emphasis is on innovative approaches to patient care by integrating contemporary technologies for crowns, bridges, and implants. The department chair leads and manages the department in alignment with the school's strategic plan. Responsibilities include planning, goal setting, financial stewardship, faculty development, oversight of graduate and DDS educational programs, and support of school and university teaching, service, clinical, & research missions. The successful candidate has a DDS/DMD and/or a PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree. Top candidates will have an international reputation as a scholar with outstanding and innovative research accomplishments, demonstrated leadership experience and knowledge of key aspects of education and patient care in modern academic dentistry. Work Locations This position is located in the School of Dentistry at 1011 N University Ave. Underfill Statement The successful candidate will be appointed on the tenure track at the rank of associate professor or professor or the clinical track at the rank of clinical associate professor or clinical professor with a recruitment package commensurate with experience and credentials. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days.  This job may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284787 Working Title Department Chair and Tenure Track Associate/Full Professor Position in Biologic and Material Sciences & Prosthodontics Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Dentistry Department DENT Bio & Materials Science Posting Begin/End Date 10/23/2026 - 12/22/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284718": {
      "title": "Professor",
      "description": "Professor - LSA Economics | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Professor - LSA Economics Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applicants must submit curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, a statement of current and future research plans, and evidence of teaching excellence to [email protected] . Job Summary The Department of Economics at the University of Michigan invites applications for a full-time, endowed tenure-track position to begin August 25, 2027. This is a university year appointment and will be at the tenured level of Associate Professor or Professor, commensurate with the successful candidate's qualifications and experience. Candidates should demonstrate excellence in scholarly productivity and impact in Economics. Responsibilities* Duties include: teaching and research in Economics, advising students and providing service to the department and university. Required Qualifications* Ph.D. in Economics or related field required. Research capacity and teaching ability serve as the primary criteria for selection. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Background Screening Employment will require both a successful criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form, and must meet all degree requirements prior to the start date of the position. The University of Michigan is supportive of the needs of dual career couples and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Application Deadline Applications will be reviewed as we receive them. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284718 Working Title Professor - LSA Economics Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Economics Posting Begin/End Date 10/23/2026 - 1/01/2027 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
