    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284712": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please address inquiries to Division Chief John Varga at [email protected] Job Summary The University of Michigan's Department of Internal Medicine Division of Rheumatology provides over 22,000 patients visits per year in 7 different locations in and surrounding scenic and culturally diverse Ann Arbor.  With over 35 clinical and basic science faculty members, a robust clinical fellowship program and 9 fellows, we endeavor to provide the best rheumatological care available anywhere.  Our purpose is to improve lives through curing, treating and preventing rheumatologic disease.  The Division of Rheumatology has extensive programs for clinical care, clinical research, translational research and basic science research in lupus, scleroderma, rheumatoid arthritis, vasculitis, myositis, as well as other rheumatologic diseases. Our guiding principles are teamwork, caring, integrity, and innovation.  As a USNWR top 15 program we are seeking candidates for tenure-track faculty positions in the assistant professor through professor ranks. Required Qualifications* MD or equivalent, ability to obtain MI Medical License, ABIM certification.  Academic clinical and research experience in rheumatology.  Evidence of clinical competence and competence in clinical and didactic teaching is expected. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend on qualifications. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days.  The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284712 Working Title ASSISTANT PROFESSOR-PROFESSOR Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Int Med-Rheumatology Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 10/29/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/282808": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Psychiatrist - Director of Brain Stimulation Program",
      "description": "Clinical Research Psychiatrist - Director of Brain Stimulation Program | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Clinical Research Psychiatrist - Director of Brain Stimulation Program Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please send curriculum vitae and letter of interest to: Jen Sullivan Department of Psychiatry Faculty Affairs Manager [email protected] Job Summary The Department of Psychiatry at the University of Michigan is seeking a full-time, instructional track psychiatrist to lead a brain stimulation program, focusing on bipolar and mood disorders. The ideal candidate for this position will be leading a clinical research program with non-invasive brain stimulation. The program will be a component of the Prechter Bipolar program, which is a longstanding clinical research program in the Department of Psychiatry. It is expected that this program will be anchored by transcranial magnetic stimulation, but opportunities include transcranial focused ultrasound, transcranial electrical stimulation and invasive brain stimulation. In addition to seeking funding for and carrying out research on brain stimulation, it is expected that the candidate will also direct clinical, FDA-cleared brain stimulation for depression. The candidate will be expected to have already established a robust research footprint in non-invasive brain stimulation, and is ready to move to a leadership position in a unique environment. A generous package of resources will be provided. The U-M Department of Psychiatry involves more than 170 faculty, robust research programs, busy general and subspecialized clinical programs, and clinical and research training opportunities. The environment at the University of Michigan provides fertile grounds for collaborations in biomedical engineering, AI/data sciences, psychology, neurology and functional brain imaging. Required Qualifications* Applicants must hold an M.D. or equivalent degree, be certified by the American Board of Psychiatry and Neurology, and must hold or be eligible for Michigan medical and pharmacy licenses. Evidence of clinical competence and competence in clinical and didactic teaching is expected. Desired Qualifications* Specialized training and expertise in non-invasive brain stimulation Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon candidate's qualifications. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third-party administrator to conduct background screenings. Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. In addition to the screenings indicated above under Michigan law, a criminal history check including fingerprinting is required as a condition of transfer or employment for this position. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 282808 Working Title Clinical Research Psychiatrist - Director of Brain Stimulation Program Job Title ASSOC PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Psychiatry Department Posting Begin/End Date 10/21/2026 - 11/20/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284302": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sociocultural Dimensions of Sport Management",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sociocultural Dimensions of Sport Management | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sociocultural Dimensions of Sport Management Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply *NOTE: Please do NOT submit your application via this system; to be considered, you must submit your application via the Interfolio process outlined below. Interested candidates should submit the following materials electronically through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194360 . The search committee will review applications as they are received. For best consideration, please apply by Friday, November 20, 2026. If the application deadline is extended, additional applications may be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled. The materials below should be consolidated into one (1) PDF document before uploading to Interfolio. Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement (up to 2 pages) Teaching statement (1 page) Up to three examples of research publications At least three references: include full name, position, relationship to applicant, e-mail address, and telephone number Nominations, applications, and expressions of interest will be held in confidence, and references will be contacted only with the candidate's permission. Should you need support related to the Interfolio system, please visit this link or contact Interfolio at [email protected] or (877) 997-8807, 9am-6 pm Eastern Time, Monday-Friday. For questions about the position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Kathy Babiak at [email protected] . Who We Are About the School and University: The University of Michigan is a leading public university and one of the world's preeminent research institutions. The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan is one of 19 independent academic units and one of seven health science schools and colleges within the University of Michigan. In Fall 2023, the National Academy of Kinesiology ranked the School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan as the #1 Kinesiology program in the US . The school is led by Dean John Cairney and has 72 faculty members, including tenure-line, clinical-line, part-time, and full-time lecturers, as well as approximately 1,449 undergraduate students and 166 graduate students. The school is home to some of the country's top academic programs focusing on movement science, applied exercise science, athletic training, and sport management. In winter 2021, the school moved into a newly renovated, state-of-the-art academic and research building ( Kinesiology Building ) in the heart of the University of Michigan's main campus. The $120M renovation project expands the School of Kinesiology's research, classroom, and office space to approximately 189,000 gross sq. ft. The research space includes a 9,800 sq. ft. biomechanics research suite, a 9,000 sq. ft. wet lab research suite, a 5,000 sq. ft. neuro research center, a 1,700 sq. ft. vivarium, and a 3,500 sq. ft. clinical testing suite and exercise research center. Additionally, new faculty members will have access to the University of Michigan's state-of-the-art core computing and laboratory facilities. About Sport Management: Sport Management at the University of Michigan is recognized as a top program in the country. Niche rated the University of Michigan as the number two public college for sport management in the U.S. The Sport Management program has 24 faculty, including 11 tenure-line faculty, 7 clinical faculty, and 6 lecturers. The breadth of faculty research is reflected in the following centers: the Center for Race & Ethnicity in Sport, the Center for Sport Marketing Research, the Center for Sports Venues & Real Estate Development, the Michigan Center for Sport & Social Responsibility, and the Center for Sport & Sustainability. Sport Management has 593 undergraduate and 52 graduate students. The School offers the following degrees in Sport Management: Bachelor of Arts, Master of Science, and Doctor of Philosophy. The program prepares students for careers in the global sport industry with a curriculum that explores the management of the sport enterprise (from organizational, marketing, financial, economic, environmental sustainability, legal, urban planning, and sociocultural perspectives). Sport Management students at the University of Michigan have the valuable opportunity to lead and participate in student-run campus organizations, including the Michigan Sport Business Conference, the premier undergraduate-run sport business conference in the U.S. Upon graduation, many of our students will begin careers at sport organizations and corporate businesses, while others will pursue postgraduate studies in business, law, accounting, and urban planning. Our graduates work in the front offices of the NFL, NBA, NHL, MLB, and MLS; with professional sports teams like the Detroit Red Wings, New York Mets, and Milwaukee Bucks; within agencies like Creative Artists Agency and Endeavor; and with industry-leading organizations like ESPN, the NCAA, The New York Times, Nike, and many more. The Sport Management program has an administrative structure that includes a program chair and an associate program chair. The program maintains an external Advisory Board to develop and nurture connections with leaders in the sport industry. The Advisory Board contributes to our vision of achieving excellence in sport business education. Job Summary The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan invites applications for a tenure-line faculty position at the rank of assistant professor, associate professor, or full professor in sociocultural dimensions of sport management. We seek dynamic and innovative scholars whose work is anchored in this area. Successful candidates will join a vibrant and collaborative faculty dedicated to excellence in research, teaching, and service. The primary responsibilities for this position include conducting high-quality, impactful research on the sociocultural dimensions of sport management using qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods and teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. The role entails advising and mentoring students across all academic levels and securing funding to support research initiatives as appropriate. Additionally, selected candidates will collaborate with faculty across disciplines within the School of Kinesiology and other units at the University of Michigan, contribute to the development and implementation of the sport management curriculum, and engage in service activities within the university, professional organizations, and the broader community. Tenure-track faculty typically carry a 45% (Research), 45% (Teaching), and 10% (Service) load. We seek candidates whose scholarship advances understanding of the sociocultural dimensions of sport management. We are particularly interested in scholars who bring humanistic social science perspectives to the study of sport, expanding how we conceptualize, investigate, and interpret organizational, cultural, and societal phenomena. We value methodological pluralism and innovation, and welcome scholars whose work applies these perspectives to a wide range of substantive areas, sectors, and levels of analysis. We are especially interested in candidates whose scholarship can foster interdisciplinary collaboration and broaden the theoretical and methodological approaches that inform research across the sport management field. Specific areas of research may include, but are not limited to, race, gender, and identity in sport organizations and leadership; sport, culture, and organizational strategy; global sport and transnational dynamics; equity and inclusion in sport governance and policy; participant rights, voice, and advocacy in organizational environments; the sociocultural dimensions of NIL and collegiate reform; culturally responsive management and stakeholder engagement; and sport for development and social change. Required Qualifications* A doctoral degree in Sport Management, Sociology, Organizational Behavior, Cultural Studies, Sport Studies, or a related field is required. Candidates should demonstrate their contributions to a positive workplace culture aligned with our school's core values . Assistant professor candidates should exhibit promise, trajectory, and/or achievements suitable for junior faculty at a major research university. At the assistant professor rank, candidates should have evidence of: scholarly prominence and academic independence, or high potential, with respect to publishing high-quality, peer-reviewed research; experience and/or willingness to mentor undergraduate and graduate students and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of expertise; reasonable engagement in service (to the industry and/or the academy); and the ability to seek and secure external funding. At the associate and full professor ranks, candidates should exceed the expectations outlined at the assistant rank. In addition to those requirements, candidates should have: a consistent record of securing research funding, to the extent necessary to facilitate their research agenda and scholarly goals; a demonstrated, elevated, and consistent profile of scholarly and pedagogical excellence; and the experience and skills necessary to advance the international reputation of the Sport Management program in the School of Kinesiology. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284302 Working Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sociocultural Dimensions of Sport Management Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Kinesiology Department School of Kinesiology Posting Begin/End Date 10/16/2026 - 11/24/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/283542": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sport Finance",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sport Finance | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sport Finance Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply *NOTE: Please do NOT submit your application via this system; to be considered, you must submit your application via the Interfolio process outlined below. Interested candidates should submit the following materials electronically through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194359 . The search committee will review applications as they are received. For best consideration, please apply by Friday, November 20, 2026. If the application deadline is extended, additional applications may be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled. The materials below should be consolidated into one (1) PDF document before uploading to Interfolio. Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement (up to 2 pages) Teaching statement (1 page) Up to three examples of research publications At least three references: include full name, position, relationship to applicant, e-mail address, and telephone number Nominations, applications, and expressions of interest will be held in confidence, and references will be contacted only with the candidate's permission. Should you need support related to the Interfolio system, please visit this link or contact Interfolio at [email protected] or (877) 997-8807, 9am-6 pm Eastern Time, Monday-Friday. For questions about the position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Brian McCullough at [email protected] . Who We Are About the School and University: The University of Michigan is a leading public university and one of the world's preeminent research institutions. The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan is one of 19 independent academic units and one of seven health science schools and colleges within the University of Michigan. In Fall 2023, the National Academy of Kinesiology ranked the School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan as the #1 Kinesiology program in the US . The school is led by Dean John Cairney and has 72 faculty members, including tenure-line, clinical-line, part-time, and full-time lecturers, as well as approximately 1,449 undergraduate students and 166 graduate students. The school is home to some of the country's top academic programs focusing on movement science, applied exercise science, athletic training, and sport management. In winter 2021, the school moved into a newly renovated, state-of-the-art academic and research building ( Kinesiology Building ) in the heart of the University of Michigan's main campus. The $120M renovation project expands the School of Kinesiology's research, classroom, and office space to approximately 189,000 gross sq. ft. The research space includes a 9,800 sq. ft. biomechanics research suite, a 9,000 sq. ft. wet lab research suite, a 5,000 sq. ft. neuro research center, a 1,700 sq. ft. vivarium, and a 3,500 sq. ft. clinical testing suite and exercise research center. Additionally, new faculty members will have access to the University of Michigan's state-of-the-art core computing and laboratory facilities. About Sport Management: Sport Management at the University of Michigan is recognized as a top program in the country. Niche rated the University of Michigan as the number two public college for sport management in the U.S. The Sport Management program has 24 faculty, including 11 tenure-line faculty, 7 clinical faculty, and 6 lecturers. The breadth of faculty research is reflected in the following centers: the Center for Race & Ethnicity in Sport, the Center for Sport Marketing Research, the Center for Sports Venues & Real Estate Development, the Michigan Center for Sport & Social Responsibility, and the Center for Sport & Sustainability. Sport Management has 593 undergraduate and 52 graduate students. The School offers the following degrees in Sport Management: Bachelor of Arts, Master of Science, and Doctor of Philosophy. The program prepares students for careers in the global sport industry with a curriculum that explores the management of the sport enterprise (from organizational, marketing, financial, economic, environmental sustainability, legal, urban planning, and sociocultural perspectives). Sport Management students at the University of Michigan have the valuable opportunity to lead and participate in student-run campus organizations, including the Michigan Sport Business Conference, the premier undergraduate-run sport business conference in the U.S. Upon graduation, many of our students will begin careers at sport organizations and corporate businesses, while others will pursue postgraduate studies in business, law, accounting, and urban planning. Our graduates work in the front offices of the NFL, NBA, NHL, MLB, and MLS; with professional sports teams like the Detroit Red Wings, New York Mets, and Milwaukee Bucks; within agencies like Creative Artists Agency and Endeavor; and with industry-leading organizations like ESPN, the NCAA, The New York Times, Nike, and many more. The Sport Management program has an administrative structure that includes a program chair and an associate program chair. The program maintains an external Advisory Board to develop and nurture connections with leaders in the sport industry. The Advisory Board contributes to our vision of achieving excellence in sport business education. Job Summary The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan invites applications for a tenure-line faculty position in sport finance at the rank of assistant professor, associate professor, or professor. We seek dynamic and innovative scholars whose work is anchored in this area. Successful candidates will join a vibrant and collaborative faculty dedicated to excellence in research, teaching, and service. The primary responsibilities for this position include conducting high-quality, impactful research in sport finance and teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. The role entails advising and mentoring students across all academic levels and securing external funding to support research initiatives as appropriate. Additionally, selected candidates will collaborate with faculty across disciplines within the School of Kinesiology and other units at the University of Michigan, help develop and implement the sport management curriculum, and engage in service activities within the university, professional organizations, and the broader community. Tenure-track faculty typically carry a 45% (Research) - 45% (Teaching) - 10% (Service) load. We are seeking candidates with expertise in finance applied to sport or sport business contexts. Specific areas of research may include, but are not limited to, the following: revenue optimization and consumer ticket pricing; franchise valuation and private equity; media rights, sponsorships, and licensing; gaming and betting; league design and competitive balance; venue financing and public finance of stadiums; risk management; and the economic impacts of sport across professional, collegiate, and major sporting event contexts. Required Qualifications* A doctoral degree in Sport Management, Business, Finance, Economics, or a related field is required. Candidates should demonstrate their contributions to a positive workplace culture aligned with our school's core values . Assistant professor candidates should exhibit promise, trajectory, and/or achievements suitable for junior faculty at a major research university. At the assistant professor rank, candidates should have evidence of: scholarly prominence and academic independence, or high potential, with respect to publishing high-quality, peer-reviewed research; experience and/or willingness to mentor undergraduate and graduate students and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of expertise; reasonable engagement in service (to the industry and/or the academy); and the ability to seek and secure external funding. At the associate and full professor ranks, candidates should exceed the expectations outlined at the assistant rank. In addition to those requirements, candidates should have: a consistent record of securing research funding, to the extent necessary to facilitate their research agenda and scholarly goals; a demonstrated, elevated, and consistent profile of scholarly and pedagogical excellence; and the experience and skills necessary to advance the international reputation of the Sport Management program in the School of Kinesiology. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Underfill Statement This position is posted as Assistant Professor/Associate Professor/Professor. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Employment will require both a criminal background check and an institutional reference check regarding any misconduct. Candidates will be required to submit a self-disclosure form as well as an authorization to release information form. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 283542 Working Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor in Sport Finance Job Title PROFESSOR Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Kinesiology Department School of Kinesiology Posting Begin/End Date 10/16/2026 - 11/24/2026 Career Interest Instructional - Regular Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
