    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284747": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position.  The review of applications will begin after the 10-day posting. Who We Are At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Responsibilities* Civil and Environmental Engineering seeks one applicant to teach CEE 501.144, in Winter term 2027 (starting January 1, 2027). Final exams: April 29 - May 6, 2027. The class is scheduled to meet in-person on Mondays and Wednesdays, 9:00-10:30am, Rm 104 EWRE. The position will be 0.30 FTE (30% effort) and requires the lecturer be responsible for teaching students the subject titled Structural Glass & Glazing System Design . Required Qualifications* A master's degree, or equivalent education and experience, in Civil Engineering is required.  PhD in Civil Engineering preferred.  Preference will be given to applicants with knowledge of structural glass and glazing systems, previous teaching experience and knowledge of the university policies and procedures. Course Description Methods for analyzing and designing glass structures and glass exterior building systems will be covered along with the material, its physical and chemical properties, and its manufacture and fabrication techniques. Structural design methods for window glass, exterior wall glass systems, and glass as a structural element, including a review of widely used building codes and standards will be addressed. The course will also cover the environmental properties of glass, such as its architectural visual qualities, solar qualities, insulating properties, and water management properties. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284747 Working Title LEO Lecturer I Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE Civil & Environmental Engr Posting Begin/End Date 10/26/2026 - 11/05/2026 Salary $11,103.00 - $15,545.00 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284839": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I for Winter 2027",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I for Winter 2027 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I for Winter 2027 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In order to be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through this website (all documents must be uploaded as one document).  Please include a cover letter of introduction explaining your qualifications and teaching philosophy, a current CV, and evidence of teaching excellence. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The College of Literature, Science, and the Arts, and the Organizational Studies Program at the University of Michigan invite applications for an LEC I position at .50% effort for the Winter Term 2027.  This appointment is scheduled to begin on January 13, 2027 and end on May 6, 2027. The unit aims to fill the opening with either a lecturer or a graduate student serving as a GSI. Responsibilities* Duties of this LEC I position are expected to include support of the main instructor teaching ORGSTUDY 202 , teaching, developing course materials, evaluating and grading students, and holding regularly scheduled office hours (see a more detailed description of the course and a list of responsibilities below). A typical full-time (100% effort) load for a Lec I position in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts is three courses per semester. The lecturer would be responsible for attending lectures, supporting student small groups, holding office hours, answering emails, providing written feedback on assignments, meeting with the primary instructor on a weekly basis, and grading student work. Required Qualifications* Past course work and/or professional experience in the area of sociology or social scientific studies of race. Must demonstrate excellent organizational skills and ability to respond quickly to student and instructor requests. The selection criteria used for this search is based on how the applicants education and experience meets the required minimum qualifications for the position.  Excellence in teaching and instruction will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. Desired Qualifications* Interest in sociology, social inequality, race, and organizational behavior; graduate students in related disciplines. Course Description ORGSTUDY 202.  Race and Organizations (Prof. Davon Norris) This course introduces students to the array of ways in which race and racism influence organizational structures and how organizational processes then shape patterns of racial inequality. Beyond developing theoretical tools and empirical understandings of race and organizations, the class leverages wide ranging data and contexts to refine students analytic capabilities enabling them to answer complex and pressing social questions. For example, how do we create organizational structures to prevent racial discrimination in hiring or promotion? Why is racial inequality so persistent across organizations from firms to schools and even the government? Does the use of big data and algorithms produce more objective decision making that ameliorates racial inequality or not? How has regulation and legislation improved or potentially deepened problems of race in organizations? We will grapple with these questions and more to understand race in America through organizations. In the process, we will sharpen our eye for identifying problems and evaluating evidence to imagine alternative possibilities and structures for the organizations we traverse. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position can be emailed to: [email protected] Application Deadline Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.  Deadline for applications is November 2, 2026.  The appointment opportunity described in this posting is subject to final approval by the academic unit. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284839 Working Title LEO Lecturer I for Winter 2027 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Organizational Studies Posting Begin/End Date 10/23/2026 - 11/02/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284810": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 160 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 160 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please upload a cover letter and resume as one document. The cover letter should address your qualifications and interest in the position. Responsibilities* Planning and teaching the identified sessions Consulting and meeting regularly with other program faculty and staff, formally and informally; virtually and/or in person Participating in occasional instructor professional development Providing timely feedback on student work Evaluating and grading student work Holding regular office hours; virtually and/or in person Required Qualifications* Successful teaching and/or facilitation experience with children and youth Record of successful teaching experience with preservice teachers or transferable equivalent, such as leading teacher professional development and/or comparable post-secondary teaching experiences; and Graduate degree in an education program or education-related field; and Proficiency with learning management systems (e.g. Canvas) and software programs for collaborative work (e.g. Google docs, Box, etc.) Desired Qualifications* Prior work with teacher preparation programs Experience managing demands and expectations from multiple stakeholders (e.g. teaching interns, university faculty and administration) Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The individual working in this position needs to be available and able to teach one section of undergraduate students during winter 2027 term from January 1-April 30, working 25% effort. Selection criteria include: Knowledge of theories and practices related to school-based inequality, disruption of exclusionary school-based routines and norms, design and enactment of inclusionary school-based routines and norms. Training and evidence of skill in large group teaching and facilitation Expertise and experience in leading inter group dialogue  and navigating sensitive conversations Knowledge of the curriculum of the undergraduate teacher education programs The offer of appointment is expected to be made by the department by December 18, 2026. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284810 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 160 Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Education Department MARSAL EDUC - Educator Prep Posting Begin/End Date 10/23/2026 - 11/02/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284766": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer III",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer III | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer III Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Submit letter of application, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, and evidence of teaching excellence through E-Recruit. References will be requested from finalists. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Program in International and Comparative Studies in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts at the University of Michigan seeks applicants for a full-time Lecturer III position with an anticipated start date of August 25, 2027. International Studies is an interdisciplinary undergraduate major housed within the International Institute and consisting of roughly 400 majors and minors. This is a non-tenure track position, with a University Year appointment period (i.e., runs August 25 through May 24 each year). A typical full-time (100% effort) load for a Lecturer III in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts is three (3) courses per semester, or the equivalent in administrative and/or service duties. Responsibilities* Duties and responsibilities for this Lecturer III position are expected to include four (4) in-person undergraduate courses per year, including both lectures and seminars, as well as departmental service, including undergraduate advising,  mentoring, and programmatic work equivalent to a third class per semester, and service on appropriate committees. Departmental service will include work to implement and advise for the new intercultural experience requirement beginning Fall 2027. Required Qualifications* Qualified candidates will hold a  PhD (ABD acceptable) in a relevant social science or humanities discipline relevant to international studies. Candidates must be able to teach lower- and upper-level classes relevant to the study of international studies. Interest in building co-curriculum and intercultural and/or study abroad advising and transcript evaluation experience is a plus. Excellence in teaching (including in larger classes), alignment with student demand, and potential in instructional service (including undergraduate advising, mentorship, and programmatic work) will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position can be emailed to [email protected] . Application Deadline The anticipated application deadline is November 2, 2026, and we anticipate notifying the successful candidate by December 20, 2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284766 Working Title LEO Lecturer III Job Title LEO Lecturer III Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA II: Prg Intl Comp Studies Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 11/02/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284782": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer - LSA New England Literature Program (Spring 2027) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer - LSA New England Literature Program (Spring 2027) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Application materials should consist of: 1. An application letter explaining your qualifications and teaching philosophy 2. A current CV 3. Evidence of teaching excellence, which ideally will include formal teaching evaluations (from students and/or in the form of reports or reviews following a classroom observation) 4. Two letters of recommendation (sent directly by recommender via email) In addition to applying via careers.umich.edu, send all application materials to [email protected] . The application deadline is November 12, 2026. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Department of English Language and Literature at the University of Michigan seeks applicants for Intermittent Lecturer positions for the New England Literature Program (NELP) in New Hampshire during the spring half-term of 2027 (exact dates below). These non-tenure track positions constitute 100% effort each and are to be appointed for one term only with the possibility of reappointment for future Spring half-terms. Responsibilities* This position requires instructors to collaborate to plan and teach a curriculum based on the literature, history, and culture of primarily 19 th and 20 th century New England. Much of that teaching takes place in non-traditional class spaces - on mountains, in the woods, in the rain, in the camp kitchen - and as a result takes on forms that differ from those usually encountered in more traditional classroom settings. This position also involves the teaching of creative writing, both in formal, workshop-style classes and in alternative writing groups that meet in a variety of settings. Instructors must feel comfortable leading small groups of students on multi-day hiking trips into New England's rugged backcountry. Intermittent Lecturers at NELP live on-site in rustic accommodations that may or may not be furnished with heat and electricity. They are expected to participate fully in all aspects and activities of the program, which begins on May 2, 2027 with a week of planning and preparations before students arrive and ends June 29 after a week of collaborative grading. Because of the compressed nature of the program's schedule and the fact that we are a residential learning community, instructors do not get weekends or days off during the program. You can learn more about NELP by visiting www.lsa.umich.edu/nelp. NELP instructors will need to attend this year's curriculum-planning retreat in Michigan, February 26-28. NELP covers travel expenses to and from the Michigan retreat center, as well as food and lodging for the weekend. Required Qualifications* The candidate must hold a B.A. in English Language and Literature or in another field with significant experience in literary studies or creative writing; candidates with a Ph.D. or M.F.A. in English are preferred. The candidate should have demonstrated excellence in the teaching of writing and literature in the United States. Preference will be given to candidates with a background in experiential and alternative education, a history of wilderness leadership, or experience with living-learning programs. Work on ecocriticism, alternative learning communities, and pedagogy is also of particular interest. The candidate must have, or commit to earning by the start of the program, a wilderness medicine certification, WFA or higher. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284782 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer - LSA New England Literature Program (Spring 2027) Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA English Language & Lit. Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 11/12/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284615": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer for Winter 2027",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer for Winter 2027 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer for Winter 2027 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In order to be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through this website (the cover letter and CV must be uploaded as one document).  In addition, please send an application letter of introduction explaining your qualifications and teaching philosophy, a current CV, evidence of teaching excellence, and two letters of recommendation to the Associate Chair of the History Department: [email protected] . Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The College of Literature, Science, and the Arts, and the Department of History at the University of Michigan invite applications for an Intermittent Lecturer position to teach a survey course on modern East Asia at 33.33% effort for the Winter Term 2027.  This appointment is scheduled to begin on January 1, 2027 and end on April 30, 2027. The successful applicant will teach the following course: HISTORY/ASIAN 205 Modern East Asia This course is an introduction to modern China, Korea, and Japan from 1600 to the present, with emphasis on the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. It covers the following topics: China's progressive decline and rejuvenation, the impact of imperialism, the rise and development of the PRC; the struggles of Korea, its colonization by Japan; liberation and division into the two Koreas, and the rising economic status of the South; and the end of feudalism in Japan, the building of a modern state and economy, Japanese imperialism, postwar recovery, and the rise to super-power status. Taking a broad comparative perspective on East Asia, the course explores the inter-relations between political economy, society, and culture in each country within an emerging modern world system. Responsibilities* Duties of this position are expected to include teaching, developing course materials, evaluating and grading students, and holding regularly scheduled office hours. In addition to teaching responsibilities, the instructor will oversee the course Graduate Student Instructor (GSI). Required Qualifications* The candidate must hold a Ph.D. in history or a closely related field. The candidate must have demonstrated excellence in college-level teaching in the United States. The candidate should show evidence of being able to work with/ guide the GSI for the course. The selection criteria used for this search is based on how the applicants education and experience meets the required minimum qualifications for the position.  Excellence in teaching and instruction will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. Desired Qualifications* Additional requirements include demonstrated successful teaching experience and enthusiasm for teaching undergraduate students. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information The chair of the hiring committee is LaKisha Simmons, Associate Chair of the History Department. Questions may be emailed to: [email protected] . Application Deadline Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The deadline for applications is November 1, 2026.  The anticipated date by which an offer will be made is no later than November 14, 2026.  The appointment opportunity described in this posting is subject to final approval by the academic unit. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284615 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer for Winter 2027 Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA History Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 11/01/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284610": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer/Instructor, LSA First Fellows Program",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer/Instructor, LSA First Fellows Program | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer/Instructor, LSA First Fellows Program Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration and should be attached as the first page of your resume or curriculum vitae. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position, present a teaching statement/philosophy, and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Who We Are The College of Literature, Science, and the Arts (LSA) is the largest of the University of Michigan's 19 schools and colleges, enrolling more than 18,000 students. The LSA First Fellows Program (LFF) is a new program that supports first-generation sophomores through academic exploration, mentorship, community building, and summer opportunities. Building on LSA's long-standing commitment to first-generation student success, we play a central role in advancing LSA's First-Generation Pathways initiative in partnership with the Dean of LSA. This work aligns key programs- including Kessler Scholars, the First-Generation Commitment, and the development of the LSA First Fellows program- to build a cohesive, scalable model for supporting first-generation students across the college. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The LSA First Fellows Program (LFF) invites applications for three lecturer positions for the Winter 2027 term. Successful candidates will teach two to three sections of 20-25 students of an Applied Liberal Arts (ALA) course: LSA First Fellows: Thriving in Your Second Year. This course is required for all First Fellows and plays an essential role in orienting students within the First Fellows Program and supporting their transition to, and flourishing in, their sophomore year at the University of Michigan (UM). Each section of this 1-credit course meets in person for one hour, one day per week. Teaching times will vary depending on the section to which the instructor is assigned. Classes are generally offered M/T/TH at 9 am and 4 pm, and Fridays at 2 pm. The seminar is designed to empower second-year, first-generation college students in the College of LSA as they navigate the academic, professional, and personal landscapes of life at UM. All First Fellows are first-generation LSA students in their second year on campus. Enrollment in this course is limited to students accepted into the new LSA First Fellows program, and both enrollment and course completion are required. Through an intentionally designed curriculum, interactive cohort activities, reflective journaling, and practical skill-building, this course demystifies the \"hidden curriculum\" of university life. Students will learn specific, relevant information and strategies to facilitate their success during their sophomore year. The LSA First Fellows course also provides a strong community for program participants while equipping them with the navigational capital, self-efficacy, and cohort support needed to thrive throughout their undergraduate journey. The course is designed to teach transferable knowledge and skills that will support students' existing and future coursework. The course invites students to think deeply about what they want from their education, how their academic and professional goals intersect with their personal lives and values, and how their experience as a first-generation student in LSA is an asset to the community. Responsibilities* As an instructor of this course, you'll guide students through reading, reflection, discussion, and writing on these themes, creating space for them to engage with their peers and build meaningful connections within the LSA First-Gen community. The course includes small weekly assignments and activities alongside a cumulative assessment, all designed to help students increase their sense of belonging at UM. Instructors should be comfortable facilitating discussion-based, reflective learning and mentoring second-year students through an exploratory, values-driven curriculum. In addition to teaching responsibilities, the instructor will hold office hours for students, provide appropriate syllabus and course materials electronically, and handle routine administrative matters. Required Qualifications* A Master's Degree in Higher Education or an associated field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. A demonstrated commitment to equity-focused, inclusive, and accessible teaching practices. Desired Qualifications* A PhD in Higher Education or an associated field. Instructional experience teaching undergraduates. Modes of Work This is an on-site position that is not eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information This position is for either 22.22% or 33.33% for the Winter Term 2027.  This appointment is scheduled to begin on January 1, 2027, and end on April 30, 2027. The First Fellows program and associated course will be a continued offering; this is a one-semester commitment with the option to renew annually, pending instructional review. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position can be emailed to: [email protected] Application Deadline Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The deadline for applications is Friday, November 06, 2026 . The anticipated date by which an offer will be made is no later than Tuesday, December 01, 2026 . The appointment opportunity described in this posting is subject to final approval by the academic unit. Selection Process The selection criteria used for this search are based on how the applicant's education and experience meet the required minimum qualifications for the position. Excellence in teaching and instruction will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284610 Working Title LEO Lecturer/Instructor, LSA First Fellows Program Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Dean: First Fellows Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 11/06/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284611": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration and should be attached as the first page of your CV. The cover letter should include: (1) An indication of which of the three courses for which you would like to be considered; (2) A brief statement of your teaching philosophy, demonstration of prior teaching, and how you might approach a course at UMBS; and (3) a brief research statement addressing research you could pursue at UMBS or how your work fits within the UMBS research and teaching mission. Who We Are The Community You will join a robust, residential scientific community. We expect all members to cultivate a culture of safety and inclusivity for the entire community of students, instructors, researchers and staff. We strive to foster a field station environment where every individual feels a sense of belonging and well-being. Our goal is for everyone to learn from each other, grow, thrive and improve our collective understanding of the world. Job Summary The University of Michigan Biological Station (UMBS) is seeking applications for Intermittent LEO Lecturers to engage with our summer Undergraduate Teaching and Research program. We run rigorous, hands-on, field-based courses and student research experiences as a critical part of our education mission. We are especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our community. We seek faculty who could teach one of the following courses listed below during spring term (June 1-July 1, 2027) or summer term (July 13-August 12, 2027). BIOLOGY 282 (General Ecology Lecture) - Spring EEB 373 (General Ecology Lab) - Spring EEB 330 (Field Ornithology) - Spring or Summer All UMBS courses are held in residence at the UMBS campus in Pellston, Michigan. Courses are 3-credits and meet 3 full days/week (M/Tu/W or Th/F/Sat) for 4 weeks. The expected appointment effort will be 66.67% for the half-term to be paid over two months.  For instructors who do not currently hold tenure-track status at U-M or another institution, terms and conditions of employment for this position as posted are subject to the provisions of a Collective Bargaining Agreement between the University of Michigan and the Lecturers Employee Organization (LEO). UMBS is located in Pellston, Michigan on Douglas Lake, has more than 10,000 acres of adjacent forest, streams, wetlands, and a variety of ecological infrastructure (long-term experiments, flux towers, stream lab) that faculty and students can use to explore and answer ecological, evolutionary, and earth systems questions. Required Qualifications* A Ph.D. and research experience in any of the many fields and disciplines connected to ecology and evolutionary biology such as ecology, evolution, forestry, natural resources management, and earth science is required. Desired Qualifications* Previous instructional experience in field-based course. Ideal candidates will also conduct research at UMBS and integrate elements of their own research into their courses at UMBS. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information Benefits of working at UMBS include: 1.    Coverage for instructor housing and dining expenses for the duration of the classes. 2. Research fellowship support for undergraduate and graduate students working in conjunction with UMBS faculty. 3.    Excellent field and laboratory support for research and teaching. 4.    First-time lead instructors are guaranteed at least one instructional assistant. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions regarding this position may be directed to Candice Everett, UMBS Academic Program Manager ( [email protected] ). Application Deadline Friday, November 6th, 2026 Decision Making Process Applications will be reviewed by the departmental leadership committee. Selection Process Review of candidates will continue until the position is filled. Only complete application packets will be reviewed. UMBS will provide notification of hiring decisions to all applicants within 14 days after the selection process has been completed. Actual hiring will be dependent on candidate qualifications and student enrollment. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284611 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Other MI Location Pellston, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Biological Station Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 11/06/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284483": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To apply, please upload your cover letter and resume as one document. The cover letter should address your interest and experience relevant to teaching this subject matter. An offer will be made by December 15, 2026. Job Summary The Marsal Family School of Education launched a new, full asynchronous online degree in Fall 2025: the Master of Arts in Leading Educational Innovation and Transformation program, which requires instructors for several courses in its core curriculum. (See the program website for program details: https://marsal.umich.edu/academics-admissions/degrees/masters-online/leading-educational-innovation-and-transformation ) Responsibilities* Online instruction for EDUCONL 508: Strategic Organizational Leadership Grading Holding virtual office hours Use of Canvas as web-based instructional tools Required Qualifications* Familiarity with the scholarship and practice relevant for course content Previous teaching experience in a course of this nature This course is integral in preparing students to earn the PK-12 School Administrator Certification ; an instructor who has earned this certification is required Course Description This course equips students with transformational, strategic, and instructional leadership skills to enhance organizational effectiveness across diverse contexts. By bridging foundational theory with practical approaches, students explore effective leadership strategies, decision making, and methods to drive continuous improvement. Participants will gain insights into fostering innovation, leading change initiatives, and navigating complex organizational challenges, preparing them to create positive impact and transformation within an organization. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284483 Working Title LEO Lecturer I Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Education Department MARSAL EDUC - School of Educ Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 10/30/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284486": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply To apply, please upload your cover letter and resume as one document. The cover letter should address your interest and experience relevant to teaching this subject matter.   An offer will be made by December 15, 2026. Job Summary The Marsal Family School of Education launched a new, full asynchronous online degree in Fall 2025: the Master of Arts in Leading Educational Innovation and Transformation program, which requires instructors for several courses in its core curriculum. (See the program website for program details: https://marsal.umich.edu/academics-admissions/degrees/masters-online/leading-educational-innovation-and-transformation ) Responsibilities* Online instruction for EDUCONL 506: Leading Effective Collaboration Grading Holding virtual office hours Use of Canvas as web-based instructional tools Required Qualifications* Familiarity with the scholarship and practice relevant for course content Previous teaching experience in a course of this nature Course Description This course explores data-driven improvement through distributed leadership, emphasizing capacity-building among staff and volunteers. Students learn to facilitate effective collaboration and analyze data to inform instruction, addressing student needs and societal contexts. Key concepts include improvement science and networked improvement communities for sustained school growth and continuous refinement. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks. Background checks will be performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eleventh day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284486 Working Title LEO Lecturer I Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Education Department MARSAL EDUC - School of Educ Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 10/30/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284390": {
      "title": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer",
      "description": "LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 215 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 215 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please attach a cover letter to your resume describing your qualifications and interest in the position. Applicants should specifically address their experience with teaching, global or cross-cultural education, experiential learning, and supporting students abroad. Applicants should also describe their professional commitment to educational equity and culturally sustaining approaches to teaching and learning.  A hiring decision will be made by December 1, 2026. Job Summary The Marsal Family School of Education seeks an intermittent lecturer to support EDUC 215: Exploring Education, a three-credit Education Studies course that includes an intensive study abroad experience. This is a 25% appointment, equating to roughly 10 hours per week. EDUC 215 is designed for undergraduate students who are curious about education and interested in exploring teaching and learning through cross-cultural and experiential learning. Using education as a lens, students consider what may be universal about teaching and learning and what is shaped by local culture, identities, language, history, community, and geography. The course emphasizes perspective-taking, culturally sustaining teaching practices, global and community-based education, and Indigenous education. Students learn through a combination of on-campus coursework and an immersive educational experience abroad that includes school and community visits, engagement with local educators, and collaboration with university faculty and students. During the 2027 study abroad experience in Ecuador, students will explore educational and cultural contexts in Quito and surrounding areas and spend significant time collaborating with students and educators at Saminay, an Indigenous school in Otavalo. The course is designed to help students examine their own assumptions, learn from educational practices situated in another cultural context, and develop a deeper understanding of the relationships among education, culture, language, identity, community, and place. Responsibilities* Collaborate in Course and Study Abroad Planning Become knowledgeable about the goals, curriculum, assignments, and experiential learning components of EDUC 215. Collaborate with the lead faculty member in planning course sessions and preparing students for the study abroad experience. Help students develop the knowledge, dispositions, and expectations necessary to learn respectfully and responsibly within educational and community settings abroad. Contribute expertise related to global education, cross-cultural learning, culturally sustaining education, and experiential learning. Participate in planning and coordination related to the study abroad component. Serve as an Instructor for EDUC 215 Support instruction during pre-travel and post-travel class sessions. Facilitate student learning and reflection related to global education, perspective-taking, culturally sustaining teaching, Indigenous and community-based education, and educational practices across cultural contexts. Connect students' experiences abroad to course concepts, readings, discussions, and assignments. Support students in examining assumptions and considering multiple perspectives about education, culture, language, identity, and community. Use course management systems and technology to effectively manage instructional and administrative responsibilities. Support and Guide Students During the Study Abroad Experience Travel with students and faculty as part of the EDUC 215 study abroad program. Help guide students through an intensive experiential learning experience involving schools, universities, community organizations, and cultural and environmental sites. Facilitate reflection and discussion that help students make meaning of their experiences and connect observations to larger questions about education. Model culturally responsive and respectful engagement with local educators, students, community members, and institutional partners. Support students as they navigate unfamiliar cultural, linguistic, educational, and social environments. Work collaboratively with U-M faculty and international partners. Assist with student support, group leadership, and problem-solving that arise during international travel. Teach, Mentor, and Evaluate Students Build supportive relationships with undergraduate students from a range of majors, backgrounds, and levels of experience with education. Facilitate thoughtful discussion and reflection about students' experiences before, during, and after study abroad. Provide timely feedback on student work and participation. Communicate effectively with students regarding their progress, questions, and concerns. Collaborate with the lead faculty member regarding student learning, well-being, and course expectations. Maintain and submit appropriate records of student participation and performance. Required Qualifications* Graduate degree in education or an education-related field. Recent successful teaching experience in PK-12, higher education, or another relevant educational setting. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in global education, international education, cross-cultural education, or a closely related area. Experience supporting, mentoring, or teaching students abroad. Demonstrated knowledge of culturally sustaining and culturally responsive approaches to education. Ability to facilitate thoughtful reflection and perspective-taking related to culture, identity, language, community, and educational practice. Strong interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and flexibly as part of a team. Ability to exercise sound professional judgment and respond effectively to student needs and unexpected situations within an international educational setting. Availability and ability to travel internationally with the program for the full study abroad period, March 6-14, 2027. Desired Qualifications* Experience designing, leading, or supporting faculty-led study abroad or other international experiential learning programs. Experience preparing undergraduate students for cross-cultural learning and facilitating reflection before, during, and after study abroad. Knowledge of experiential learning and place-based, community-based, or Indigenous education. Experience working collaboratively with schools, universities, educators, or community organizations abroad. Experience teaching, leading, or supporting students in unfamiliar cultural and educational contexts. Spanish-language proficiency and/or experience working effectively across linguistic differences. Experience teaching or mentoring undergraduate students who are exploring education but are not necessarily enrolled in a teacher certification program. Demonstrated commitment to educational equity and to approaching international education from a stance of curiosity, humility, reciprocity, and respect for local knowledge and expertise. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information EDUC 215 includes both on-campus instruction and required international travel. Pre-travel class meetings are scheduled from 4:00-7:00 p.m. on January 26, February 9, and February 23, 2027. The study abroad component will take place March 6-14, 2027. A post-travel class meeting will be held from 4:00-7:00 p.m. on April 6, 2027. The instructor must also be available for course planning and coordination meetings prior to travel and for student support and instructional responsibilities associated with the study abroad experience. Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of ten calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284390 Working Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer - EDUC 215 Job Title LEO Intermittent Lecturer Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Part-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Education Department MARSAL EDUC - Educator Prep Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 10/30/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284575": {
      "title": "LEO Lecturer I Winter 2027",
      "description": "LEO Lecturer I Winter 2027 | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ LEO Lecturer I Winter 2027 Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply In order to be considered for this position, all applicants must apply to this posting through this website. All documents must be uploaded as a single file. Please include a cover letter explaining your qualifications and teaching philosophy, a current CV, a syllabus for a course you have designed or taught, and evidence of teaching excellence. Mission Statement The mission of the University of Michigan is to serve the people of Michigan and the world through preeminence in creating, communicating, preserving and applying knowledge, art, and academic values, and in developing leaders and citizens who will challenge the present and enrich the future. Job Summary The Organizational Studies Program in the University of Michigans College of Literature, Science, and the Arts invites applications for one or two Lecturer I appointments to teach ORGSTUDY 208, Business and the Natural Environment, in Winter Term 2027. This appointment is scheduled to begin on January 1, 2027 and end on May 31, 2027. The course has two roles: a primary instructor, who leads the course and teaches the lecture, and a section instructor, who teaches discussion sections and supports the primary instructor. We are open to several configurations: Primary instructor only (33% effort). Sections would be taught by a graduate student instructor or a second lecturer. Section instructor only (50% effort). Teach three sections in support of the primary instructor. Both roles, with three sections (83% effort). Teach the course and all three sections, with total enrollment capped at 75 students. Both roles, with four sections (100% effort). Teach the course and four sections, with total enrollment capped at 100 students. Please state in your cover letter which configuration(s) you are interested in. Responsibilities* The primary instructor for ORGSTUDY 208 will: adapt existing course materials, deliver lectures, supervise the section instructor (a Graduate Student Instructor or Lecturer I), evaluate and grade students, and hold regularly scheduled office hours. A typical full-time (100% effort) load for a Lec I position in the College of Literature, Science, and the Arts is three courses per semester. This role will be 33% effort. The section instructor for ORGSTUDY 208 will: attend lectures and provide a guest lecture; prepare and teach discussion sections; grade and provide detailed feedback on individual and group work, hold office hours, answer emails, meet with the instructor on a weekly basis, and provide other support to the primary instructor as needed. This role will be either 50% effort (for three sections) or 67% effort (for four sections). Lectures will likely meet Mondays and Wednesdays, 9 to10am. Discussion sections will likely meet Fridays at 10 to 11am, 11am to 12pm, and 12 to 1pm, with a fourth section added in the 100% configuration. Sections are capped at 25 students each. ORGSTUDY 208 offers a broad introduction to the study of business and the natural environment, integrating insights from sociology, psychology, and economics. It starts with an overview of the triple bottom line framework, in which corporations take into account social and environmental performance in addition to financial performance. It then focuses on contemporary business activities that address the natural environment. These include the role of the natural environment on business management and strategy, operations, supply chain, product innovation, and marketing. Students will review cases on business and the natural environment and share their insights with others so that all can learn about different approaches for business to address concerns with the environment. The course uses sustainability reports of a diverse group of corporations to analyze and critique current business practices with respect to the environment. Finally, student teams complete industry analyses in various geographies to identify future opportunities for business and the natural environment. The goal of the course is to provide students with an understanding of a variety of activities that corporations take to address challenges with the natural environment and to help them develop the skills to critically analyze these corporate activities and recognize strengths and weaknesses in different corporate strategies. The Organizational Studies Program is an interdisciplinary major built on a foundation of three disciplines, sociology, psychology, and economics, that examines the behavior, structure, and dynamics of organizations. Required Qualifications* Completed Masters or PhD in a relevant social science, professional field (e.g. management), or environmental science. Must have subject matter expertise and demonstrated success in the undergraduate classroom. Excellence in teaching and instruction will be the principal criteria used to select the successful candidate. Desired Qualifications* For the primary instructor, demonstrated success in creating an engaging, interactive, inclusive environment in a class of similar or larger size, and supervisory experience. For the section instructor, must demonstrate excellent organizational skills and ability to respond quickly to student and instructor requests. For both roles, academic background in organizational theory or organizational behavior, experience in project-based classes, and work experience in project or consulting-based roles. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Union Affiliation This position is covered under the collective bargaining agreement between the U-M and the Lecturers Employee Organization, AFL-CIO, which contains and settles all matters with respect to wages, benefits, hours and other terms and conditions of employment. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Contact Information Questions about applying for this position can be emailed to: [email protected] . Application Deadline Review of applications will begin after 10/29, but applications will be accepted until 11/12. The anticipated date by which an offer will be made is no later than December 31, 2026. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284575 Working Title LEO Lecturer I Winter 2027 Job Title LEO Lecturer I Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Of Lsa Department LSA Organizational Studies Posting Begin/End Date 10/19/2026 - 11/12/2026 Career Interest LEO - Lecturers Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284754": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW",
      "description": "RESEARCH FELLOW | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ RESEARCH FELLOW Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply For consideration of this position, applicants must submit the following materials as one document: 1) cover letter describing their interest in working in the global social epidemiology of cognitive aging and dementia, 2) their experience working with cross-nationally harmonized longitudinal studies of aging, and 3) a current CV. Job Summary The Department of Epidemiology is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute to research on the global social epidemiology of cognitive aging and dementia. Primary responsibilities include the design and conduct of data analysis in cross-nationally harmonized longitudinal studies of aging in the Health and Retirement Study (HRS) and Harmonized Cognitive Assessment Protocol (HCAP) Networks, with emphasis on the Longitudinal Study of Aging in India - Diagnostic Assessment of Dementia (LASI-DAD), preparation of scientific manuscripts and presentations as lead and co-author. Opportunities will be available for collaboration on a range of funded studies using the HRS and HCAP Network study data, as well as for participation in the preparation and submission of extramural research grants, including career development awards for independent development. The Postdoctoral Research Fellow position will be located within the Social Epidemiology of Global Aging (SEGA) Lab, directed by Dr. Lindsay Kobayashi within the Center for Social Epidemiology and Population Health (CSEPH) in the Department of Epidemiology. The SEGA Lab is a dynamic and collaborative research environment supported by a portfolio of National Institute of Aging (NIA)-funded projects on the global social epidemiology of cognitive aging and dementia. Responsibilities* Data analysis: Designs and executes complex data analyses. Independently interprets results, including creation of data tables, visualizations, and reports for presentation Data management: Manages the organization, documentation, merging, cleaning, and storage of complex population datasets. Creates final analytic datasets Manuscript preparation: Conceptualizes and contributes to the writing of scientific manuscripts as lead author and co-author Grant preparation: Assists in acquiring extramural research funding, including the technical portions of grant proposals Required Qualifications* Doctorate in epidemiology or other public health science. Applicants must have a conferred doctorate degree prior to the start of the fellowship Record of accomplishment in epidemiologic research as evidenced by publications, training, and proficiency in statistical analysis Should have a career trajectory in the field of global and/or social epidemiology of cognitive aging as evidenced by prior research activities and knowledge Excellent skills in data management and analysis, data visualization, written and oral scientific communication, and time management and planning Desired Qualifications* Experience working with the HRS and HCAP Network study data, especially the Longitudinal Study of Aging in India - Diagnostic Assessment of Dementia (LASI-DAD). Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Work Locations The selected candidate will be expected to work on-site three days per week. Additional Information This is a term-limited position for one year from the start of the appointment, with possibility of extension contingent upon funding and performance. We are seeking an experienced and dynamic postdoctoral research fellow with a commitment to contributing to a welcoming environment for all members of our community. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284754 Working Title RESEARCH FELLOW Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Pub Health Department Epidemiology Department Posting Begin/End Date 10/23/2026 - 11/06/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284607": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hydrology and Water Resources",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hydrology and Water Resources | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hydrology and Water Resources Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your resume. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. We welcome applicants with disabilities and support individuals in requesting accommodations. If you are unable to upload your full materials to this website, please send an email to [email protected] with the job ID (284607) in the subject line. Who We Are At the University of Michigan School for Environment and Sustainability (SEAS), we are at the forefront of building a more sustainable and just world for all by transforming the impact of higher education and reimagining the future. We are advancing action through innovation, research, education and engagement in society, and developing leaders who are empowered to halt the climate crisis and create an environmentally sound future for generations to come. To learn more about SEAS and our values, please visit our website at https://seas.umich.edu/about/seas-values . Job Summary The University of Michigan's School for Environment and Sustainability (SEAS) and Dr. Tiantian Yang's research lab are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hydrology and Water Resources. The successful candidate will conduct advanced research in hydrologic modeling, flood forecasting, extreme weather prediction, snow observation, snow modeling, soil moisture data analytics, and global reservoir/lake system analysis, modeling using remotely sensed information, and AI/ML models in support of water resources decision-making. The postdoc will also mentor graduate students, contribute to grant proposal development, engage in agency outreach and partnership activities, publish high-quality research articles, and present research outcomes at national professional conferences. Responsibilities* Conduct and develop research related to hydrologic modeling, flood forecasting, extreme weather prediction, snow observation, snow modeling, soil moisture data analytics, and global reservoir/lake system analysis Prepare high-quality publications, present results at leading conferences, and contribute to interdisciplinary workshops Contribute to grant proposals and collaborative research project development, collaborating with SEAS scholars and administrators Mentor graduate students and engage in community/agency outreach and stakeholder engagement Required Qualifications* PhD in civil/environmental engineering, hydrology, hydraulics, water resources engineering, GIS, remote sensing, atmospheric sciences, climate science, computer science, data science, geography, or a related field (completed by start date or within four months). Strong records of journal article publications and conference participation experience Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with interdisciplinary teams Desired Qualifications* Experience with large-scale datasets and computationally intensive models. Experience with large-scale observation data (not limited to soil moisture data, reservoir and lake data, remote sensing data, ET data, or streamflow data in support of hydrologic modeling and/or reservoir system modeling). Strong programming and computational skills (Python, PyTorch, TensorFlow, or equivalent). Experience in either developing or applying Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning tools and algorithms to solve hydrology and water resources problems. Familiarity with high-performance computing (HPC), cloud platforms, or GPU clusters. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. This job may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. Applications will be reviewed as received throughout the posting period and continue until the position is filled. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284607 Working Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hydrology and Water Resources Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Nat Res Envir Department Sch for Environ and Sustain Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 1/03/2027 Salary $60,000.00 - $65,000.00 Career Interest Primary Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/277122": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW",
      "description": "RESEARCH FELLOW | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ RESEARCH FELLOW Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Applications for the postdoctoral fellowship should include the following: - A cover letter describing background in occupational health and safety, career goals; research interests and possible research activities at the University of Michigan if the fellowship is awarded; and, optionally, possible research mentors. - CV and graduate transcripts. - Two letters of recommendation. - Contact information for two or more individuals who know your work and background. The cover letter should be attached as the first page of your application. Applicants are required to work with a COHSE faculty member. Job Summary The University of Michigan Center for Occupational Health and Safety Engineering (COHSE) is seeking to recruit an outstanding post-doctoral fellow under the mentorship of faculty conducting cutting-edge cross-disciplinary research in occupational health sciences. Our ongoing research is extensive and spans exposure and risk science, occupational and environmental epidemiology, ergonomics and safety, and occupational health nursing (see https://cohse.umich.edu/research-faculty/research/ ). Highlights include, for example, automation and robotics, autonomous vehicles, control of exoskeletons, PFOS exposure and menopause, noise measurements and hearing loss, extreme heat and emergency preparedness, air monitoring instrumentation and applications, community-based research, hazards in e-waste disposal and management, and policy evaluation. The post-doctoral fellow will have numerous research training opportunities, all with the goal of developing independent and highly productive researchers, scientists and educators. The successful candidate will be encouraged to identify a mentoring team; participate in ongoing research; independently come up with research ideas that align or complement the mission of the Center; conduct laboratory, field, or secondary research; compose scientific manuscripts and conference presentations; and most importantly, become a productive member of the scientific community. This position is supported by a T42 training grant funded by the National Institute of Occupational Health Sciences. The grant supports the University of Michigan Center for Occupational Health and Safety Engineering, which has the mission of serving the region, nation, and world as a center of excellence for research and graduate education in occupational health and safety (OHS). Since inception in 1982, COHSE has supported comprehensive graduate-level educational programs to aspiring practitioners and researchers in Industrial Hygiene (IH), Occupational and Environmental Epidemiology (OEE), Occupational Health Nursing (OHN), and Occupational Safety Engineering and Ergonomics (OSE) to help meet the nation's needs for qualified practitioners and researchers. Our academic programs span the School of Public Health, the College of Engineering, and the School of Nursing. This position is for a one year term. A second year is possible with sufficient progress shown and availability of funding. This position will be for a FY26 Level 0 Postdoc $63,480 with potential consideration for Level 1-2. Responsibilities* The research fellow will work under the mentorship of a faculty member or faculty team at the Center for Occupational Health and Safety Engineering (COHSE). With the goal of becoming an independent researcher, research fellow will: - Design and Conduct research related to occupational health and safety. - Conduct analyses of occupational health data. - Prepare, compile, and analyze statistical data. - Prepare manuscripts and research grants. - Participate in professional development opportunities, including organizing the annual Research Symposium in Occupational Health and Safety held at the University of Michigan or one of our collaborating universities. - Participate in interdisciplinary collaboration with other researchers. Fellow's research will by proposed by the fellow, and potential applicants should view the training program faculty and contact those whose research is of interest in order to identify and get the support of an appropriate mentor. Areas of interest include: exposure and risk science, occupational and environmental epidemiology, ergonomics and safety, and occupational health nursing. Required Qualifications* - Required qualification includes a PhD from an accredited domestic or foreign institution at the time of appointment. - Be a US citizen, a noncitizen national of the United States (e.g., born in outlying possessions of the USA), or have been lawfully admitted for permanent residence in the USA (e.g., possess a current Permanent Resident card). Desired Qualifications* - Training, experience and research interests in occupational health and safety. - Publications and conference presentations. - Excellent communication skills. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 277122 Working Title RESEARCH FELLOW Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Pub Health Department Environmental Health Sciences Posting Begin/End Date 10/22/2026 - 11/30/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284701": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Longitudinal Child Development and Health)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Longitudinal Child Development and Health) | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Longitudinal Child Development and Health) Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply Please submit the following as a single PDF document via the University of Michigan applicant management system: Cover Letter (detailing research interests and career goals) Curriculum Vitae (CV) including training, research strengths, and publications Contact information for three professional references Questions about this position should be directed to Dean John Cairney at [email protected] . Who We Are The University of Michigan is a leading public university and one of the world's preeminent research institutions. The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan is one of 19 independent academic units and one of seven health science schools and colleges within the University of Michigan. In Fall 2023, the National Academy of Kinesiology ranked the School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan as the #1 Kinesiology program in the US . The school is led by Dean John Cairney and has 72 faculty members, including tenure-line, clinical-line, part-time, and full-time lecturers, as well as approximately 1,449 undergraduate students and 166 graduate students. The school is home to some of the country's top academic programs focusing on movement science, applied exercise science, athletic training, and sport management. In winter 2021, the school moved into a newly renovated, state-of-the-art academic and research building ( Kinesiology Building ) in the heart of the University of Michigan's main campus. The $120M renovation project expands the School of Kinesiology's research, classroom, and office space to approximately 189,000 gross sq. ft. The research space includes a 9,800 sq. ft. biomechanics research suite, a 9,000 sq. ft. wet lab research suite, a 5,000 sq. ft. neuro research center, a 1,700 sq. ft. vivarium, and a 3,500 sq. ft. clinical testing suite and exercise research center. Additionally, new faculty members will have access to the University of Michigan's state-of-the-art core computing and laboratory facilities. Job Summary The School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join an international research team examining the longitudinal relationships among motor development, health-related fitness, and mental health across childhood. The successful candidate will work with a unique longitudinal cohort dataset comprising eight waves of data collected annually from approximately ages 5-6 through 11-12 years. A particular focus of the study is understanding the developmental consequences of persistent motor difficulties, including developmental coordination disorder (DCD), and their relationships with physical fitness, physical and mental health, and child development over time. The position provides an exceptional opportunity for a quantitatively skilled early-career researcher to lead high-quality publications using an established longitudinal dataset while developing an independent research program. The combination of eight waves of childhood data, a defined initial program of manuscripts, freedom to develop additional investigator-led analyses, and opportunities to participate in grant development and new primary studies provides a strong platform for building an independent research trajectory. We are particularly interested in candidates who are intellectually curious, quantitatively strong, highly productive, and excited by the opportunity to use longitudinal data to understand how motor development, physical health, and mental health intersect across childhood. Responsibilities* The primary focus of the position will be secondary analysis and publication of the longitudinal cohort data. The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will: Lead the analysis, interpretation, and preparation of manuscripts arising from a defined set of priority research questions; Apply appropriate longitudinal statistical methods, including mixed-effects/multilevel models, latent growth models, time-lagged models, and related approaches; Undertake data management, preparation, quality checking, documentation, and creation of analysis-ready datasets; Work collaboratively with investigators to develop analytical strategies appropriate to complex longitudinal research questions; Lead the preparation and submission of manuscripts to high-quality peer-reviewed journals; Contribute to the coordination of papers and analyses across an international, multidisciplinary investigator team; and Communicate findings through scientific presentations and other forms of research dissemination. In addition to the core program of planned papers, the successful candidate will have access to the dataset to develop and lead their own investigator-directed analyses and publications, subject to the study's established governance and publication processes. We particularly encourage candidates who can identify novel questions that take advantage of the richness and longitudinal nature of the dataset. Opportunities for Research Development Although secondary data analysis and publication will be the primary focus of the fellowship, the position is intended to provide broader opportunities for research and career development. The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will have opportunities to contribute to the preparation of competitive external grant applications, including applications to the National Institutes of Health (NIH), and to participate in the design and implementation of new primary data collection studies. The position will also provide opportunities to establish collaborations with investigators at the University of Michigan and partner institutions internationally. Additionally, the Fellow will work within a team-science environment and will be encouraged to develop collaborations that support their emerging independent research program. Required Qualifications* Applicants should have completed, or be close to completing, a PhD or equivalent research doctorate in a relevant field to apply; all degree requirements must be met on or before the start date. Appropriate disciplinary backgrounds may include: Kinesiology, human movement, exercise science, or related fields; Child development or developmental psychology; Epidemiology or public health; Biostatistics or quantitative methods; or Other relevant behavioral, health, or developmental sciences. Candidates should demonstrate strong quantitative research skills and experience analyzing complex datasets. Expertise in longitudinal data analysis is particularly important, with experience in one or more approaches such as mixed-effects/multilevel modeling, latent growth analysis, longitudinal structural equation modeling, cross-lagged/time-lagged models, or related techniques. Candidates should also be able to demonstrate their contributions to a positive workplace culture aligned with our school's core values . Desired Qualifications* The successful candidate will have: Strong statistical and quantitative research skills; Experience managing, cleaning, documenting, and analyzing complex research datasets; Excellent scientific writing skills and evidence of the capacity to produce peer-reviewed publications; The ability to work independently, manage competing priorities, and take responsibility for projects from research question through analysis and publication; Strong interpersonal and communication skills and an ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary and international research team; and A high degree of initiative, organization, and self-motivation. Experience in child development, developmental coordination disorder, motor development, physical fitness, physical activity, or child mental health would be advantageous but is not essential. Previous experience in research project management, coordination, or leadership would also be desirable. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The position will be based in the School of Kinesiology at the University of Michigan , providing access to a highly collaborative research environment and opportunities to engage with investigators across the University and internationally. The research team includes investigators with expertise spanning kinesiology, motor development, child development, mental health, epidemiology, and quantitative methods. The Fellow will be expected to work actively across disciplinary and institutional boundaries and will have opportunities to develop new collaborations across multiple institutions. There is flexibility regarding work location. The successful candidate may be based in Ann Arbor or, where University employment requirements and immigration compliance permit, work remotely from elsewhere within the United States. Remote work from outside the United States is not available for this position . Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled anytime after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284701 Working Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Longitudinal Child Development and Health) Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Kinesiology Department School of Kinesiology Posting Begin/End Date 10/21/2026 - 11/20/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284614": {
      "title": "RESEARCH FELLOW",
      "description": "RESEARCH FELLOW | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ RESEARCH FELLOW Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your CV. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Mission Statement Michigan Medicine improves the health of patients, populations and communities through excellence in education, patient care, community service, research and technology development, and through leadership activities in Michigan, nationally and internationally. Our mission is guided by our Strategic Principles and has three critical components; patient care, education and research that together enhance our contribution to society. Job Summary Postdoctoral Research Fellow position is available at the Michigan Center for Translational Pathology (MCTP). This role offers the opportunity to participate in cutting-edge cancer research projects within a highly collaborative and interdisciplinary team environment. Working under the supervision of Principal Investigators and senior research staff, the successful candidate will focus on biomarker discovery, tumor microenvironment analysis, and the evaluation of novel cancer therapeutics. Responsibilities* Molecular Assays & Tissue Analysis Advanced Assays: Perform complex molecular assays including immunohistochemistry (IHC), RNA in situ hybridization (RNA ISH), and fluorescent in situ hybridization (FISH) on human and murine samples. Morphological Evaluation: Conduct detailed morphological analysis on human and murine tissues to understand the tumor microenvironment and evaluate the tissue-modulatory effects of developing therapeutics. Perform semi-quantitative analysis such as scoring (product score, H score etc) Quantification: Utilize digital software to analyze multiplex immunofluorescence images and apply specialized skills to manually score single-plex IHC samples for protein quantification and localization. Microscopy & Documentation Image Extraction: Capture high-quality images using both brightfield and immunofluorescence microscopy. Figure Assembly: Compile and prepare visual data and figures for research presentations and peer-reviewed publications. Core Supervision & Collaboration Lab Supervision: Oversee assay optimization and routine workflows on Ventana platforms to report to the lead pathologist and lab director, providing guidance to the histotechnologist team at the center's histology core. Clinical Research: Participate as an active member of a collaborative research team involved in biospecimen harvesting from Castration-Resistant Prostate Cancer (CRPC) patients as part of the rapid autopsy program. Required Qualifications* The ideal candidate will possess a graduate degree (MD and/or PhD) in Pathology with relevant experience in Biochemistry, Biology or Chemistry. Why Join Michigan Medicine? Michigan Medicine is one of the largest health care complexes in the world and has been the site of many groundbreaking medical and technological advancements since the opening of the U-M Medical School in 1850. Michigan Medicine is comprised of over 30,000 employees and our vision is to attract, inspire, and develop outstanding people in medicine, sciences, and healthcare to become one of the world's most distinguished academic health systems.  In some way, great or small, every person here helps to advance this world-class institution. Work at Michigan Medicine and become a victor for the greater good. What Benefits can you Look Forward to? Excellent medical, dental and vision coverage effective on your very first day Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The Michigan Center for Translational Pathology (MCTP) is a collaborative effort to foster research advances in molecular medicine and apply those advances as quickly as possible to benefit patient care. The mission of our Center is to establish the University of Michigan as an international leader in the discovery and characterization of disease biomarkers and therapeutic targets using an integrated multi-disciplinary, systems biology approach and establish a new paradigm of bringing personalized medicine to routine clinical care through the use of high-throughput sequencing. More information on MCTP can be found on our website: https://pathology.med.umich.edu/mctp/. Background Screening Michigan Medicine conducts background screening and pre-employment drug testing on job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent job offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background screenings.  Background screenings are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Report Act. Pre-employment drug testing applies to all selected candidates, including new or additional faculty and staff appointments, as well as transfers from other U-M campuses. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284614 Working Title RESEARCH FELLOW Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Onsite Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group Medical School Department MM Ctr fr Translational Path. Posting Begin/End Date 10/20/2026 - 11/19/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284376": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Research Fellow Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply For consideration of this position, applicants must submit the following materials as one document: 1) cover letter describing their interest in working in computational approaches to understanding the transmission of influenza, and 2) a current CV. Job Summary The Department of Epidemiology is seeking a postdoctoral research fellow with a record of accomplishment in infectious disease transmission (mathematical/mechanistic) modeling research to contribute to studies seeking to examine demographic, behavioral, and environmental factors that facilitate or prevent influenza transmission in households, using combined data from multiple prospective household cohort studies. Primary responsibilities include the design and conduct of household simulation models to analyze longitudinal cohorts, as well as the preparation of scientific manuscripts as lead and co-author. There may be opportunities available for collaboration on a range of funded studies in the area of infectious disease dynamics, as well as for participation in the preparation and submission of extramural research grants. This position, funded by a National Institutes of Allergy and Infectious Disease grant, will be under the supervision of Dr. Andrew Brouwer, a computational epidemiologist whose research leverages mathematical and statistical modeling for inference in epidemiology, and Dr. Joseph Eisenberg, an infectious disease epidemiologist and transmission modeler with global and domestic research and an interest in environmental determinants of transmission. The University of Michigan has a vibrant and dynamic intellectual community for infectious disease research, including the Michigan Center for Respiratory Virus Research and Response, the Michigan Center for Infectious Disease Threats, the Michigan Integrated Public Health Center of Outbreak Analytics and Modeling, among others. Responsibilities* Data management: Manages the organization, documentation, merging, cleaning, and storage of complex population datasets. Creates final analytic datasets. Data analysis and modeling: Designs and executes complex data analyses and simulation models. Independently interprets results, including creation of data tables, visualizations, and reports for presentation. Manuscript preparation: Conceptualizes and contributes to the writing of scientific manuscripts as lead author and co-author. Collaboration and mentorship: Provide collaborator feedback and supplementary mentorship to master's- and PhD-level students engaged in related research projects. Grant preparation: Assists in acquiring extramural research funding, including the technical portions of grant proposals. Required Qualifications* Doctorate in epidemiology or other public health science. Applicants with PhD in computational or quantitative fields (statistics, applied mathematics, etc.) are encouraged to apply if they meet the other criteria. Applicants must have a conferred doctorate degree prior to the start of the fellowship. A record of accomplishment in infectious disease modeling research, as evidenced by publications, presentations, and training. Prior training or research in simulation modeling, parameter estimation, and uncertainty quantification. Proficiency in programming in R (preferred) or Python. Excellent skills in data management and analysis, data visualization, and scientific communication. Desired Qualifications* The following qualifications are not required or expected but should be highlighted in the application materials if present. Prior experience in household transmission models. Prior experience with models of influenza or other respiratory viruses. Modes of Work Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . This position allows for a hybrid work schedule. The selected candidate will be expected to work on-site 2 days per week. Additional Information This is a term-limited position for one-year from the start of the appointment, with possibility of extension contingent upon funding and performance. We are seeking an experienced and dynamic staff member with a commitment to contributing to a welcoming environment for all members of our community. Anticipated start date: Between November 1, 2026 and January 1, 2027. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284376 Working Title Research Fellow Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group School Pub Health Department Epidemiology Department Posting Begin/End Date 10/19/2026 - 11/09/2026 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
    "https://careers.umich.edu/job_detail/284487": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Engineering Systems Group | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Engineering Systems Group Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Apply Now How to Apply A cover letter is required for consideration for this position and should be attached as the first page of your curriculum vitae. The cover letter should address your specific interest in the position and outline skills and experience that directly relate to this position. Who We Are The University of Michigan Transportation Research Institute (UMTRI) is recognized internationally for our multidisciplinary research focusing on transportation safety and mobility ( http://umtri.umich.edu ). Established in 1965, we maintain the highest standards of excellence as we conduct research that improves peoples' lives through access to safe, equitable and efficient mobility. UMTRI is a non-degree-granting unit within the College of Engineering at the University of Michigan that includes over 25 faculty members, over 30 research and technical staff, and nearly over 300 students each year engaged in research and other experiential learning opportunities. At Michigan Engineering, we develop the talent and technologies that move society forward and serve our state and national interests. Through discovery and innovation, we create the foundational knowledge and practical technologies to solve not only today's most pressing challenges, but also power industries and change lives. Our programs and community are designed to promote personal well-being and achievement - enabling everyone to unlock their potential and contribute with confidence. Job Summary The University of Michigan Transportation Research Institute (UMTRI) invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow position. The position is for a one-year term and offers the opportunity to contribute to a highly regarded research program and enhance skills necessary for a successful academic/research career.  The successful candidate will work on research projects led by Dr. Arpan Kusari , Associate Research Scientist, in UMTRI's Engineering Systems Group (ESG), and will conduct research that focuses on the intersection of AI, computer vision and large-scale simulation. The candidate should demonstrate a keen understanding of the problems they have solved and the impact of their solutions. What You'll Do You will be expected to work on grant-funded research projects and develop a guided research program either stemming from the projects or as an independent problem.  As part of the research duties, you will be expected to: Collaborate in ongoing research and new research proposals with faculty Author and co-author peer-reviewed publications with a vision of publication to top-tier conferences or journals Participate and present in seminars, work groups and presentations at professional meetings Required Qualifications* A Ph.D. that closely aligns with the research areas outlined in the job summary Strong quantitative and analytical skills Ability to work collaboratively and independently in a research environment Excellent problem solving and communication skills Demonstrated ability to publish in peer-reviewed conferences or journals * Candidates must be eligible to work in the United States and maintain authorization for the duration of the appointment. Why Work at Michigan? In addition to a career filled with purpose and opportunity, The University of Michigan offers a comprehensive benefits package to help you stay well, protect yourself and your family and plan for a secure future. Benefits include: Generous time off Many choices for comprehensive health insurance Life insurance Flexible spending accounts for healthcare and dependent care expenses Maternity and Parental Leave Modes of Work This position is classified as hybrid.  Positions that are eligible for hybrid or mobile/remote work mode are at the discretion of the hiring department. Work agreements are reviewed annually at a minimum and are subject to change at any time, and for any reason, throughout the course of employment. Learn more about the work modes . Additional Information The Postdoctoral Research Fellow position is for a one-year. Background Screening The University of Michigan conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer and may use a third party administrator to conduct background checks.  Background checks are performed in compliance with the Fair Credit Reporting Act. Application Deadline Job openings are posted for a minimum of seven calendar days. The review and selection process may begin as early as the eighth day after posting. This opening may be removed from posting boards and filled any time after the minimum posting period has ended. U-M EEO Statement The University of Michigan is an Equal Opportunity Employer. We are committed to providing an environment of mutual respect where equal employment opportunities are available to all applicants, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Job Detail Job Opening ID 284487 Working Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Engineering Systems Group Job Title RESEARCH FELLOW Work Location Ann Arbor Campus Ann Arbor, MI Modes of Work Hybrid Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular FLSA Status Exempt Organizational Group College Engineering Department CoE UMTRI-Engineering Systems Posting Begin/End Date 10/16/2026 - 10/30/2026 Salary $63,480.00 - $66,654.00 Career Interest Research Fellows Apply Now Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    }
  },
